var WK_KOREA = [
{"id":"korea-bulgogi","name":"Bulgogi","nameAlt":"Marinated Beef — the word means 'fire meat'","aliases":["bulgogi","korean bbq beef","sobulgogi"],"course":"main","type":["main","beef","grill","bbq","everyday"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","family","bbq"],"ingredients":"150g beef sirloin or ribeye, sliced thin against the grain · 20ml soy sauce · 12ml pear or apple juice (the tenderiser that is not a secret) · 10ml mirin or dry sherry · 10ml sesame oil · 5g brown sugar · 8g garlic, minced · 3g ginger, grated · 2 spring onions, chopped · a pinch of black pepper","method":"THIN SLICES ARE THE DISH. Bulgogi means 'fire meat' and its entire character is built on slices so thin they cook in seconds on a screaming-hot pan — about 3mm, cut across the grain. Part-freeze the beef for 30 minutes and it slices like butter. NO SHEET OF BEEF SHOULD BE THICKER THAN A TOASTED SANDWICH.\n\nTHE MARINADE IS THE TWIST AND THE PEAR IS THE TRICK. Whisk the soy, pear juice, mirin, sesame oil, brown sugar, garlic, ginger and pepper. The pear is doing real chemistry: its enzymes (and apple's, its nearest shelf cousin) tenderise the meat — and its sweetness caramelises into that lacquered, savoury-sweet edge. This is the difference between Korean barbecue and plain grilled meat.\n\nMARINATE 30 MINUTES TO OVERNIGHT. Thirty minutes is the floor, overnight is the peak — the pear's enzymes need time to work their way in, and the soy deepens. Do not salt the beef first: the marinade is the seasoning.\n\nSEAR FAST, IN TWO BATCHES, IN A SMOKING PAN. Get a pan or griddle ripping hot and sear the slices in a single layer, 30–45 seconds a side, until the edges catch and caramelise. Crowd the pan and the beef steams grey — space the slices and it lacquers. Two batches are always better than one crowded one.\n\nSERVE STRAIGHT OFF THE PAN, SCATTERED WITH THE RESERVED SPRING ONION. Bulgogi waits for no one — the contrast of hot caramelised edges against cool rice and sharp kimchi is the whole plate. Leftover marinade, boiled 3 minutes in a pan, is the classic dipping sauce.","cookTime":"Prep 15–20 min · Marinate 30 min–overnight · Cook 3–4 min","kcal":"~480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 36g · Carbs 16g · Fat 30g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Marinated raw beef keeps 2 days in the fridge. Cooked bulgogi keeps 3 days and is superb cold — and famously folds into tomorrow's fried rice. Freezes well raw, in the marinade, for a month.","chefNotes":"The pear is not optional decoration — its enzymes do the tenderising that makes bulgogi silk instead of steak. Slice thin, marinate long, and sear in batches over a roaring pan. The caramelised, lacquered edges are the point.","pairsWith":"Steamed white rice · Kimchi · A bowl of sliced cucumber in vinegar · Cold beer","goesWith":["Kimchi Fried Rice","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Bulgogi's name literally means 'fire meat' — and it has been cooked over open flame since the days of the Goguryeo kingdom, around 2,000 years ago. The sweet lacquered soy marinade is the modern chapter: it only became the bulgogi the world knows in the 20th century, when the sesame-oil-and-pear glaze perfected what the fire had started.","howThisFeels":"The hiss as the thin slices hit a screaming-hot pan, the buttery-sweet smoke rising, and then the first caramelised bite against cold rice — the whole table suddenly very quiet.","leftovers":["Chop leftover bulgogi small and fold it into hot rice with a spoon of gochujang and sesame — bulgogi fried rice, the family's favourite day-two use.","Wrap cold bulgogi with lettuce and a smear of gochujang for ssam — a crisp, spicy hand roll that might be the best version of all."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Chicken Bulgogi (Dak Bulgogi)","icon":"🐔","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The same pear-and-soy lacquer on chicken thigh — juicier than breast, and it takes the glaze even better.","trivia":"Dak bulgogi uses the same marinade with chicken thighs — and thighs are the right call: the extra fat keeps the fast-seared slices silky where breast would dry out in the same 45 seconds.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef sirloin or ribeye, sliced thin against the grain","to":"150g chicken thighs, sliced thin"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Chicken must cook through, so give it a minute per side rather than 45 seconds, and let it rest one minute before serving. The pear marinade works exactly the same — the enzymes don't know what kind of meat they're tenderising."}]}},{"name":"Pork Bulgogi (Dweji Bulgogi)","icon":"🐖","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The same lacquer on pork — richer, and the most common street-version of Korean BBQ outside Korea itself.","trivia":"Dweji bulgogi swaps in pork shoulder, and it is the bulgogi you are most likely to meet from a Korean street stall — pork is cheaper than beef, the marinade loves it, and the caramelisation is even faster.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef sirloin or ribeye, sliced thin against the grain","to":"150g pork shoulder, sliced thin against the grain"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Pork shoulder wants a touch more time than beef — sear a full 90 seconds per side so it's cooked through — and skip the resting hang-up: pork is short-shrift if you prefer it well done."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-bibimbap","name":"Bibimbap","nameAlt":"The 'mixed rice' bowl — every colour of the Korean table in one bowl","aliases":["bibimbap","korean mixed rice","dolsot bibimbap"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","bowl","vegetables","egg"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","family","lunchbox"],"ingredients":"80g cooked rice · 40g beef mince (optional) · 1 egg · 30g spinach · 30g carrot (cut into fine matchsticks) · 30g courgette (cut into fine matchsticks) · 30g bean sprouts · 15g sesame oil · 10ml soy sauce · 5g gochujang (the red paste that makes it bibimbap) · 1 clove garlic · a pinch of sugar · toasted sesame seeds","method":"BIBIMBAP MEANS 'MIXED RICE' AND THE MIX IS THE MEAL. Line up the vegetables, cook each in its own oil, and pile them around the rice in the national colours: green spinach, orange carrot, pale sprouts, white rice, and the red gochujang on top. The bowl should look like a small garden before it is stirred.\n\n⚖️ EVERY VEGETABLE IS SEASONED, NOT JUST COOKED. Each one gets its own minute in the pan with a whisper of salt and, ideally, a drop of sesame oil: spinach wilted and seasoned with soy and garlic; carrot and courgette stir-fried separately so their colours and textures stay distinct. Seasoned separately is the difference between bibimbap and a rice bowl with vegetables on it.\n\nTHE EGG IS THE LAST ACT — FROM THE PAN, STRAIGHT TO THE TOP. Fry it sunny-side up with a soft, runny yolk — the yolk is the sauce of the second half of this dish, the one that binds the mix when you stir at the table.\n\nIF YOU HAVE BEEF, BROWN IT HARD FIRST. Season the mince and cook it in a hot pan until properly brown — then reserve a spoon of its pan juice to stir into the finished bowl.\n\nNOW THE MOVEMENT THE DISH IS NAMED FOR: STIR. The runny yolk, the gochujang, the sesame oil and every separate flavour are folded into the rice with chopsticks until every grain is glossy and red — bibimbap is a dish you complete at the table, not on the stove. Scatter toasted sesame seeds over the stirred bowl.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 15 min","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 22g · Carbs 68g · Fat 18g · Sodium ~900mg","storage":"Best prepared and stirred fresh. The cooked rice and vegetables keep separately 2 days in the fridge — the vegetables re-warm well in a pan; the rice in the microwave. The dish does not freeze well (the vegetables weep on thawing).","chefNotes":"Season each vegetable in its own pan minute — that is the whole textural argument of the bowl. Get real gochujang (Korean chilli paste) if you can find it; it is the red heart of the mix. And never stir it on the stove: the stirring IS the ritual, done at the table.","pairsWith":"A bowl of soup (clear or kimchi) on the side · Cold barley or green tea","goesWith":["Bulgogi","Kimchi Fried Rice","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Bibimbap exists because of Korean jesa — the ancestor memorial rites that once laid out many small dishes in a specific order. Leftovers and small portions were mixed into rice so nothing was wasted, and the 'mixed rice' became so beloved it shed its leftovers-only history entirely. In Jeonju, the dish's spiritual home, the version is famously luxurious, topped with raw beef and egg yolk.","howThisFeels":"A bowl of colour that looks almost too pretty to stir — then the runny yolk, the gochujang and the sesame oil are folded in and it becomes the happiest thing in the kitchen.","leftovers":["The next-day move is kimchi fried rice — chop any leftover vegetables and the beef into a hot pan with the cold rice and a spoon of gochujang, and fry it until it crackles.","Leftover rice and vegetables make a perfect lunchbox bibimbap — keep the gochujang in its own little tub and stir it in at the table."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Dolsot (Sizzling Stone Bowl)","icon":"🔥","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"The restaurant version with a soundtrack — served in a screaming-hot stone bowl that crackles when the rice touches it, and leaves a crust of crispy golden rice at the bottom.","trivia":"Dolsot bibimbap arrives in a stone bowl heated until smoking — the rice that touches the hot stone fries into a crispy golden crust (nurungji) that is half the pleasure. You can fake most of the experience with a cast-iron pan or a screaming-hot ovenproof bowl: heat the dish and a spoon of sesame oil in it, then build the bibimbap inside.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"sesame oil, for the hot bowl"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Heat the stone bowl or a small cast-iron pan over high heat with a teaspoon of sesame oil until it shimmers. Build the rice and toppings directly in the hot bowl — the rice will crackle and crisp on the bottom as it cooks. Serve sizzling, with the warning that the bowl is dangerously hot."}]}},{"name":"Vegetarian","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":36,"howThisFeels":"Every colour of the bibimbap garden, no meat — with mushrooms stepping in for the beef's savour.","trivia":"A vegetarian bibimbap is never a thin version in Korea: the dish is fundamentally a mixture of seasoned vegetables, and a good shiitake or shimeji mushroom, browned hard, closes most of the gap the mince would leave.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"40g beef mince (optional)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"60g mushrooms, sliced and browned"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Brown the sliced mushrooms hard in a hot dry pan — no oil for the first minute so they release water — then add a drop of soy and sesame at the end. Use them exactly where the beef would have sat. For a fully vegan bowl, finish with a spoon of crispy fried tofu in place of the egg."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-kimchi-fried-rice","name":"Kimchi Fried Rice","nameAlt":"Kimchi Bokkeumbap — Korea's favourite fridge-clearing pan","aliases":["kimchi bokkeumbap","kimchi fried rice","bokkeumbap"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","stir-fry","quick","everyday"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","quick","lunchbox","bachelor"],"ingredients":"80g cold cooked rice (day-old is best) · 60g kimchi, chopped · 20g onion (chopped) · 10ml gochujang (optional, for extra heat) · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 5g sugar · 1 egg · spring onion, sliced · a splash of vegetable oil","method":"COLD RICE, NOT FRESH. Day-old rice from the fridge fries loose and separate; fresh rice is swollen with steam and turns to porridge in the pan. No day-old rice? Spread fresh rice on a tray and freeze it 20 minutes to dry it out. THE COLD RICE IS THE WHOLE FIRST RULE.\n\nFRY THE KIMCHI HARD FIRST — THIS IS THE DISH'S SECRET SOUL. Chop the kimchi, squeeze out its juice and keep the juice, then fry the chopped kimchi in a hot pan with the oil until the edges catch and it darkens — usually 3 minutes. Raw kimchi tastes raw; fried kimchi turns sweet, sour and deeply savoury. That 3 minutes of browning is the entire difference between kimchi fried rice and rice with kimchi stirred in.\n\nADD THE GOCHUJANG AND THE RICE AND SMASH THE SAUCE INTO IT. Push the fried kimchi to one side, drop in the gochujang and let it sizzle in the oil, then the rice, and press everything flat with the spatula — let the rice toast against the hot pan for 30 seconds before you toss it, so the grains catch golden. WHICH IS ALSO THE WOK HEI OF KIMCHI FRIED RICE.\n\nSEASON AND TOSS. Splash in the soy, the sesame oil, the sugar and a spoon of the reserved kimchi juice — the juice recreates the fermented tang that frying was cooking off. Toss until every grain is glossy and red.\n\nCROWN WITH A FRIED EGG. Sunny-side-up, runny yolk — the yolk is the second sauce of the dish, broken at the table and stirred into the hot rice. Scatter spring onion and sesame seeds on top.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 10 min","kcal":"~480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 14g · Carbs 66g · Fat 17g · Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"Best the minute it's fried. Keeps 2 days in the fridge and reheats without shame in a hot pan with a splash of water. Freezes acceptably for a month — though the egg never does (add it fresh).","chefNotes":"Fry the chopped kimchi until the edges catch — that browning is the whole move. Use day-old rice, let it toast in the pan for 30 seconds before tossing, and never skip the fried egg on top: the runny yolk is half the sauce.","pairsWith":"A side of kimchi (the same pickle, this time raw) · Doenjang soup · Cold barley tea","goesWith":["Bibimbap","Bulgogi","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Kimchi fried rice was born as the ultimate fridge-clearing dish — the last-of-the-pantry move Korean students and bachelors perfected. It got so beloved that dedicated kimchi fried rice restaurants exist across Korea, serving bowls with a matching fried egg as the default, and it is regularly voted Korean comfort food.","howThisFeels":"The sharp fermented smell of kimchi hitting a screaming-hot pan, the edges catching and darkening — and then a red, glossy bowl of rice with a fried egg winking at you from the top.","leftovers":["Fold a slice of cheese into the hot rice with the lid on and let it melt — cheese kimchi fried rice is a legitimate Korean favourite, salty-tangy and unholy-good.","Wrap it in a sheet of nori with the fried egg and a little gochujang for a kimbap-style hand roll the next day."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"The pantry hero — old rice, older kimchi, one egg, and the whole pan smells like Seoul.","trivia":"The classic is the fridge-clearing original: whatever the crisper had is welcome, but the kimchi and the fried egg are the law. In Korea this is the bachelor's dish, the student's dish, and the midnight snack that outranks takeaway.","delta":{},"ingredients":"80g cold cooked rice (day-old is best) · 60g kimchi, chopped · 20g onion (chopped) · 10ml gochujang (optional) · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 5g sugar · 1 egg · spring onion, sliced","method":["Fry the chopped kimchi hard until its edges darken, 3 minutes.","Push aside, sizzle the gochujang in the oil, add the cold rice and let it toast 30 seconds before tossing.","Season with soy, sesame oil, sugar and a spoon of the reserved kimchi juice.","Crown with the fried egg and spring onion. Stir the yolk into the rice at the table."]},{"name":"Bacon & Cheese","icon":"🥓","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The Western-Korean cross that works — smoky bacon and a molten cheese crust over the tangy rice, a heart attack you will happily eat twice.","trivia":"Bacon and cheese are the two great Western additions to kimchi fried rice — cheese because its fat cuts the sharp kimchi acid beautifully, bacon because its smoke plays against the ferment like it was born to. Both are now standard-issue in Seoul's fried-rice bars.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"30g bacon, chopped"},{"item":"20g mozzarella or cheddar, grated"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fry the chopped bacon first, crisp it, and use its rendered fat in place of the oil for the kimchi — the kimchi fries in bacon fat, which is exactly as good as it sounds. After the rice is fried and seasoned, press it flat, scatter the cheese over, cover the pan for 1 minute until it melts into a molten crust, and eat straight from the pan."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-tteokbokki","name":"Tteokbokki","nameAlt":"The beloved street-food rice cakes in a spicy-sweet red sauce","aliases":["tteokbokki","spicy rice cakes","topokki"],"course":"main","type":["main","street-food","rice-cake","quick"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["street-food","quick","snack"],"ingredients":"150g cylindrical rice cakes (tteok – fresh or frozen) · 20g fish cakes (optional, the classic partner) · 100ml water or anchovy-kelp stock · 12g gochujang (Korean chilli paste) · 8g gochugaru (Korean chilli flakes) or mild red chilli flakes · 10ml soy sauce · 10ml honey or brown sugar · 5g sesame oil · 1 boiled egg (optional) · 2 spring onions","method":"THE RICE CAKES ARE THE WHOLE TEXTURE. Tteokbokki is all about glossy, chewy cylinders of rice cake — cut them if frozen, and if they are fresh and soft, no soaking needed. If frozen, soak in cold water 10 minutes first so they heat through without going rubbery. THEY SHOULD BE SO CHEWY THEY ALMOST SQUEAK ON YOUR TEETH.\n\nTHE SAUCE IS THE SOUL AND IT IS BUILT IN THE PAN. Combine the water (or anchovy-kelp stock for depth), gochujang, gochugaru, soy sauce and honey in the pan — this is a sauce that comes together in the pan, not in a bowl first, because the simmering is what thickens and blends it.\n\n⚖️ THE TRUE STOCK IS THE SECRET. Anchovy-kelp stock (dashima stock) is what the street vendors use, and it is the difference between tteokbokki and noodles in a chilli packet. A 5cm piece of dried kelp simmered in the water for 5 minutes, then removed, is enough to change the whole bowl — the shop shortcut is a splash of good broth or a scrap of kombu.\n\nBRING TO A BUBBLE, THEN ADD THE RICE CAKES. Simmer over a medium flame, stirring gently, for 8–10 minutes — the sauce reduces, thickens and clings to the cakes. Gently is the word: rice cakes are fragile and sticky, and a hard stir breaks them into mush. Add the fish cakes and boiled egg for the last 3 minutes, and the spring onion off the heat.\n\nSERVE INSTANTLY, WITH A SPOONFUL OF THE THICK SAUCE OVER EVERYTHING. The chewy rice cakes carry the sweet-spicy sauce like nothing else — and the classic finish is to mop the last sauce with a piece of fried (or even plain) gimbap.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 12 min","kcal":"~430 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 8g · Carbs 80g · Fat 6g · Sodium ~950mg","storage":"Best eaten immediately — rice cakes harden and turn off as they cool. Keeps a day in the fridge, reheated gently with a splash of water to loosen the sauce. Does not freeze (the texture is everything).","chefNotes":"Chewy rice cakes and a glossy sweet-spicy sauce are the whole dish — simmer gently so the cakes don't crack, and let the sauce reduce until it clings like a glaze. If you can find real gochugaru, the flakes give the proper fiery red; if not, mild chilli flakes plus a little extra gochujang is honest. The anchovy-kelp stock is the street-vendor secret — worth the two extra minutes.","pairsWith":"Fried or plain gimbap (rice rolls) to mop the sauce · A boiled egg plopped in the sauce · Cold barley tea","goesWith":["Kimchi Fried Rice","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Tteokbokki started life in a palace kitchen as a royal dish — sliced rice cakes simmered in a soy-meat broth, the 'palace tteokbokki'. After the Korean War, a street vendor swapped the soy for the soldier-delivered gochujang that was suddenly everywhere, and the fiery red street version was born. The street version won: today the red tteokbokki is the dish, and it is one of Korea's best-selling street foods, served from the same vendor stalls in winter.","howThisFeels":"Glossy red cylinders of rice cake — the first bite is the purest sweet-spicy-chewy hit the Korean street has to offer, with the sauce sticking to your fingers and asking for more.","leftovers":["The classic Korean finish: keep the leftover red sauce and fry gimbap, or rice, in it — sauce-too-good-to-waste is part of the street-food code.","Add the leftover sauce to a pan of instant noodles for a cheat tteokbokki-ramen hybrid the next day."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Street","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"The original street bowl — chewy rice cakes in a glossy red sauce, with fish cake and a boiled egg as the classic companions.","trivia":"The street version includes paper-thin fish cakes by default — they flavour the sauce as they simmer and become the chew that balances the rice cake's chew. The boiled egg is the optional regular: halved into the sauce, it soaks up the red and becomes the best bite in the bowl.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g cylindrical rice cakes · 20g fish cakes (optional) · 100ml water or anchovy-kelp stock · 12g gochujang · 8g gochugaru or mild chilli flakes · 10ml soy sauce · 10ml honey or sugar · 5g sesame oil · 1 boiled egg (optional) · 2 spring onions","method":["Soak frozen rice cakes in cold water 10 minutes.","Simmer the stock (or water plus a scrap of kombu, removed after 5 minutes) in a pan.","Whisk in gochujang, gochugaru, soy and honey; bubble to a glossy red sauce.","Add the rice cakes and simmer gently 8–10 min until the sauce clings; add fish cake and egg for the last 3 min.","Spring onion off the heat; serve immediately."]},{"name":"Cheese Tteokbokki","icon":"🧀","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The modern favourite — molten mozzarella over the fiery red cakes, the fat calming the fire like a benediction.","trivia":"Cheese tteokbokki is the 21st-century Seoul standard — the volcanic-red sauce meets a cooling blanket of melted cheese and the combination became so popular it now rivals the original at street stalls.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"20g fish cakes (optional, the classic partner)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"25g mozzarella, grated"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Once the sauce has thickened and clings to the cakes, scatter the grated mozzarella over the top, cover the pan 1–2 minutes until it melts into a molten blanket, and eat with the cheese pulling off every cake."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-japchae","name":"Japchae","nameAlt":"The glass-noodle stir-fry that is a party's sweetest moment","aliases":["japchae","korean glass noodles","chap chae"],"course":"side","type":["side","noodles","banquet","vegetables","make-ahead"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["celebration","party","banquet","make-ahead"],"ingredients":"60g sweet potato glass noodles (dangmyeon – the round, slippery ones) · 40g beef (very thinly sliced, optional) · 30g carrot (fine matchsticks) · 30g onion (thinly sliced) · 30g spinach · 30g shiitake mushrooms (soaked, sliced) · 15ml soy sauce · 12ml sesame oil · 8g sugar · 2 cloves garlic · sesame seeds · black pepper","method":"THE NOODLES ARE SWEET-POTATO GLASS NOODLES AND THEY ARE THE WHOLE POINT. Japchae's signature is dangmyeon — translucent, glassy, gloriously slippery sweet-potato noodles. Cook them in boiling water 6–8 minutes until just tender, drain, and CUT them with scissors a few times so they are a manageable length — they are spectacularly springy. Do not rinse them; the starch coats them and helps the sauce cling.\n\n⚖️ EVERY COMPONENT IS COOKED SEPARATELY, THEN MIXED — THE HALLMARK OF A BANQUET DISH. This is not a one-pan stir-fry: the carrot, onion, mushrooms and spinach each take their own oil and their own minute, and each is seasoned at its turn. The separate cooking is what lets every colour and texture survive the final mix. HOLDING THE COMPONENTS APART IS THE WHOLE METHOD.\n\nTHE SAUCE IS A SWEET-SALTY GLOSS, MADE ALL AT ONCE. Whisk the soy, sugar, sesame oil, minced garlic and a pinch of pepper — sweet forward, salty behind, the classic banchan balance. Have it in a cup before the pan goes on.\n\nSEAR THE BEEF FIRST IN ITS OWN MINUTE. Season it lightly, sear it hard and brief in a hot pan until just cooked — it should be pink to medium, never grey.\n\nNOW THE FINAL TOSS: ADD THE NOODLES, THE VEGETABLES, THE BEEF, THE SAUCE — TOSS ONCE, COVERED, AND LET EVERYTHING DRINK. Toss over low heat for 2 minutes so the sauce edges into everything, and finish with sesame seeds and a final thread of sesame oil. Serve warm or chilled — this is one of the great make-ahead dishes of Korean cooking and is better on day two.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 20 min","kcal":"~450 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 12g · Carbs 68g · Fat 14g · Sodium ~700mg","storage":"Japchae is a legendary make-ahead — it keeps 3–4 days in the fridge and is genuinely better on day two as the sauce soaks in. Serve it cold or at room temperature, which is how it is traditionally offered at banquets. Freezes well for a month. Do not reheat hard — the noodles are perfect cool.","chefNotes":"Sweet potato glass noodles are non-negotiable. Cook every vegetable in its own pan with its own seasoning, keep them apart until the very end, then toss them all once in the sweet-salty sesame gloss. The separate cooking is what makes japchae japchae. Sesame oil, sesame seeds, soy and a spoon of sugar — the entire Korean binding spell.","pairsWith":"Korean barbecue (the classic banquet pairing) · Pickles · Cold noodles or soup on the side","goesWith":["Bulgogi","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Japchae was born at the royal court in the early 1600s, when a court official served the king a dish of stir-fried vegetables and glass noodles so good it named itself: japchae means 'mixed vegetables'. It became a banquet standard and later went fully royal — King Gojong is said to have raised it to banquet status in the 19th century. Every celebration table in Korea still sets out a bowl.","howThisFeels":"Long, slippery, glassy noodles glistening with sesame oil and studded with bright vegetables — a banquet plate that somehow tastes better the next day.","leftovers":["The day-two move is the unsung one: serve it chilled, straight from the fridge — that is how Korean banquets offer it, and the cold noodles drink the sauce even deeper.","Fold leftover japchae into an omelette or a rice bowl with a fried egg for a five-minute Korean lunch."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Beef","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":36,"howThisFeels":"The banquet original — thin-sliced beef and bright vegetables through the glossy glass noodles.","trivia":"The classic is the royal-era version: thinly sliced beef, shiitake, carrot, spinach and onion, each cooked on its own and finished in the sweet sesame glaze. It is one of the most festive things you can put on a Korean table.","delta":{},"ingredients":"60g sweet potato glass noodles · 40g beef (very thinly sliced, optional) · 30g carrot · 30g onion · 30g spinach · 30g shiitake mushrooms · 15ml soy sauce · 12ml sesame oil · 8g sugar · 2 cloves garlic · sesame seeds · black pepper","method":["Cook the glass noodles 6–8 min until tender; drain and cut with scissors.","Cook each vegetable separately in its own sesame-oiled pan, seasoning as you go.","Sear the beef hard and brief until pink, never grey.","Whisk the soy, sugar, sesame oil, garlic and pepper.","Toss everything once with the sauce over low heat; finish with sesame seeds."]},{"name":"Mushroom (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"All the glassy-slick glory with mushrooms doing the beef's savoury work — the vegan banquet version.","trivia":"Simply removing the beef and browning an extra variety of mushrooms — shiitake, oyster or button — covers the savour beautifully, and a shot of soy in the mushroom pan deepens the whole plate. Japanese and Korean temple kitchens have been making an egg-free, beef-free japchae for centuries.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"40g beef (very thinly sliced, optional)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"40g extra mushrooms (oyster or button), sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Brown the extra mushrooms hard in a dry hot pan until they give up their water and turn golden, then add a splash of soy and a drop of sesame to finish them. Use them exactly where the beef would have sat. The mushrooms take the sweet-salty glaze beautifully and the plate loses nothing."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-samgyeopsal","name":"Samgyeopsal","nameAlt":"Grilled pork belly — the social dish Korean nights are built around","aliases":["samgyeopsal","grilled pork belly","korean bbq pork belly","ssam"],"course":"main","type":["main","pork","grill","bbq","social","make-your-own"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","social","casual","groups"],"mood":"Social & festive","time":"Prep 15 min · Grill 15 min","health":"Rich and indulgent — balance with raw greens and garlic, which are part of the ritual, not an afterthought.","allergen":"Soy (ssamjang, soy sauce) · sesame (sesame oil in the ssamjang)","budget":"★☆☆ — belly is the treat; the lettuce-garlic-chilli table is cheap","audience":"Perfect for a weekend group — it is a do-it-yourself grill where nobody needs a plate, just lettuce and a lap.","season":"Best year-round; a summer braai where the grill IS the party and rain just moves it indoors","ingredients":"200g pork belly, in thick slices · 40g ssamjang (or mix 20g gochujang with 15g doenjang and a drop of honey) · 8 leaves crisp lettuce or perilla (kkaennip, if you can find it) · 2 cloves raw garlic, sliced thin · 1 green chilli, sliced · 5ml sesame oil · 2g flaky salt · freshly ground black pepper","method":"SAMGYEOPSAL IS NOT A RECIPE, IT IS A RITUAL — AND THE RITUAL IS THE TABLE. The meat arrives sizzling in the middle, and everyone cooks, wraps and eats at their own pace. The first rule is REFUSAL: samgyeopsal is NEVER marinated. The whole point is clean pork fat — the seasoning happens at the table in the wrap, which is why this is the one Korean meat that goes un-doctored onto the grill. The second rule is the grill itself. Lay the thick slices on a hot grill or heavy pan, cut-side down, and let them wallow in their own fat. Turn only when the underside is deeply golden and the edges are crisp — patient char beats constant fidgeting. As each piece cooks, cut it with scissors into bite-size strips right on the grill, letting the cut edges crisp another minute. This scissors-at-the-table move is not theatre; it keeps the juices inside and gives every tongue-sized piece a crunchy edge.\n\nWHILE IT COOKS, BUILD THE SSAM TABLE — this is half the dish. Down the centre goes a bowl of ssamjang (shop-bought is honestly fine; homemade is a 2-minute mix of gochujang, doenjang, a thread of sesame oil and a crush of garlic). Around it: cool lettuce leaves (or perilla if you can find it — its anise-and-mint edge with pork fat is one of the great Korean tastes), raw garlic sliced paper-thin, green chilli rings, and a little dish of seasoned sesame oil — sesame oil with a pinch of salt and black pepper, for a quick dip before the wrap.\n\nNOW THE WRAP THAT NAMES THE DISH: SSAM. Take a leaf, swipe a dab of ssamjang in the middle, lay in a piece of the hot pork with its crisp edge showing, a slice of raw garlic, a ring of chilli, fold the leaf over it like a little parcel and eat it whole in one mouthful. Do not nibble — a ssam is built for a single satisfying bite, and the cold leaf against the hot fat, the raw garlic, the chilli and the salty paste colliding at once is exactly why Koreans eat this nightly.\n\nA bowl of steamed rice is optional but honest: for the last few pieces, grill the cloves of leftover garlic alongside the pork until they are soft and sweet, and tuck them into the final wrap. Wash down with cold soju or beer, and repeat until the grill is empty.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Grill 15 min","kcal":"~550 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 28g · Carbs 6g · Fat 46g · Sodium ~900mg","storage":"Best eaten at the grill, fresh from the pan. Cooked leftover belly keeps 2 days in the fridge — the fat sets and it is superb cold in rice or sliced into fried rice. Do not reheat hard; the belly turns from crisp to wobbly.","chefNotes":"Never marinate it — samgyeopsal is pure pork, and the table is the seasoning. Char it patiently, cut it at the table, and never skimp the garlic and lettuce: they are not garnishes, they are the balance that makes a fat-soaked meal feel light.","pairsWith":"Cold soju or crisp lager · Kimchi · Doenjang-jjigae on the side · More lettuce — always more lettuce","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Doenjang-jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Samgyeopsal means 'three-layered flesh' — and the name is about the cut, not the cooking. Pork belly is one of the most popular foods in Korea precisely because it is unmarinated: while almost every other meat arrives drenched in soy or gochujang, this one is served raw at the table, and the seasoning happens in the lettuce wrap. Korean barbecue restaurants built entirely around samgyeopsal number in the thousands, and the 'samgyeopsal + soju' combo is so iconic it has its own slang name: sam-sam, from the similar syllables. The ritual of one person cooking for the table, cutting the meat with scissors and tucking it into leaves for friends first, is so deeply social that Koreans debate whether it tastes better shared — and the honest answer is yes.","howThisFeels":"The sizzle of unmarinated belly curling at the edges, the bright sting of raw garlic and chilli against cool lettuce, and the whole table reaching for the ssamjang at once — nobody talks much during samgyeopsal, and everyone is happy.","leftovers":["Chop leftover belly small and fry it with kimchi, cold rice and a spoon of gochujang for samgyeopsal kimchi fried rice — the classic next-day move.","Slice it thin and crown a bowl of steaming rice with a rare soft egg for one of Korea's favourite solo comfort lunches.","Use the leftover ssamjang as a rapid stir-fry sauce — a tablespoon with any chop of vegetables becomes an instant Korean bowl."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Grill (Samgyeopsal)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":60,"howThisFeels":"The full ritual — thick belly, patient char, scissors at the table, and the ssam table dressed with raw garlic and chilli.","trivia":"The classic is the complete experience: the unmarinated belly, the ssamjang-and-garlic table, and the lettuce-wrap rule of one full bite per ssam.","delta":{},"ingredients":"200g pork belly, in thick slices · 40g ssamjang · 8 leaves crisp lettuce or perilla · 2 cloves raw garlic, sliced thin · 1 green chilli, sliced · 5ml sesame oil · 2g flaky salt · black pepper","method":["Grill the unmarinated belly slices cut-side down, turning only when deeply golden; cut into strips with scissors at the table.","Set the ssam table: ssamjang, lettuce or perilla, raw garlic, chilli rings, and seasoned sesame oil.","Press each piece into the salt, swipe with ssamjang, and wrap in a leaf with garlic and chilli — one whole bite per wrap.","Repeat until the grill is empty, washing down with cold soju or beer."]},{"name":"Budget & Quick — Pan-Fried Belly Strips","icon":"💰","costPP":38,"howThisFeels":"The same unmarinated pork, but sliced thin and pan-fried fast instead of grilled — the apartment weeknight version that still gets the ssam table.","trivia":"Slicing the belly thin (about 5mm) is the budget-quick trick: it cooks in two minutes a side in a hot pan with no grill required, and because it is thinner it needs less total fat rendered. The ssam table does the rest of the work exactly as the classic.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"200g pork belly, in thick slices","to":"200g pork belly, pre-sliced thin (about 5mm)"}],"addStep":[{"text":"A thin slice needs only a minute or two per side in a hot, dry pan. Let the first side go a full 90 seconds untouched so it develops a proper golden crust, then flip, stack the slices into a little pile, and serve immediately with the ssam table. No scissors needed — they are already bite-sized."}]}},{"name":"Grilled Tofu & Mushroom Ssam (Veg)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The same ssam ritual, with the grill given over to tofu and mushrooms — and honestly, this could be the best version: charred tofu soaks up the ssamjang and the fun of the wrap is unchanged.","trivia":"Korean temple kitchens have always eaten this way — grilling firm tofu until it holds a golden crust and wrapping it in lettuce with ssamjang. The tofu does what the belly cannot: it drinks the paste rather than shedding fat, and the sesame-oil seasoning that replaces pork fat brings a nutty depth of its own.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"200g pork belly, in thick slices"}],"addIng":[{"item":"150g firm tofu, cut into thick slabs"},{"item":"100g shiitake or other mushrooms, thick-sliced"},{"item":"extra sesame oil for brushing"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Press the tofu slabs dry, brush both faces with sesame oil, and grill until deeply golden on both sides — a proper crust, not a pale surface. Brush the mushrooms with the same oil and grill until they release their water and take colour. Build the wraps exactly as the classic: ssamjang, garlic, chilli, leaf. For a fully vegan bowl, make ssamjang from gochujang and doenjang and a drop of maple instead of honey."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-sundubu-jjigae","name":"Sundubu-jjigae","nameAlt":"Soft tofu stew — the bubbling volcano pot Koreans eat on cold nights","aliases":["sundubu","soft tofu stew","sundubu jjigae","tofu stew"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","tofu","spicy","one-pot","comfort"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","comfort","winter","lunch"],"mood":"Comforting & restorative","time":"Prep 10 min · Cook 20 min","health":"High-protein and light — soft tofu is genuinely good for you, and this is one of the lightest comfort stews in Korean cooking.","allergen":"Soy (tofu, soy sauce, gochujang/doenjang in some versions) · seafood in the classic (optional — remove for vegan) · sesame","budget":"★★☆ — tofu and pantry staples; a little pork or seafood stretches the classic, or skip them entirely for the cheapest honest bowl","audience":"Perfect for anyone who needs a gentle, warming bowl — it is Korea's fever-and-cold-night food for good reason.","season":"Winter and rainy days are its home, but Koreans crave it year-round","ingredients":"1 packet soft/silken tofu (about 300g) · 400ml anchovy-kelp stock (or vegetable stock for vegan) · 15g gochugaru (Korean chilli flakes) or 1 tbsp mild chilli flakes + a pinch more heat · 10ml gochujang · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 30g onion, sliced · 1 spring onion, sliced · 1 egg (optional — classic) · 40g pork belly or 50g prawns (optional — classic) · black pepper","method":"THE STEW LIVES IN A STONE POT, AND THE POT IS HALF THE DISH. The classic is cooked in a ttukbaegi — a small earthenware pot that sits on the stove bubbling, and arrives still bubbling at the table, the tofu quivering like it is alive. No ttukbaegi? A small heavy saucepan does the same job; the drama is just quieter.\n\nFIRST, THE STOCK — THIS IS WHERE THE DEPTH COMES FROM. True sundubu is built on anchovy-kelp stock (see the Dashi card for the technique family), and it is what separates this from a box of tofu in chilli water. Simmer the big kelp scrap in the stock base with dried anchovies for 5 minutes, then remove them. For a vegan pot, use vegetable stock with a strip of dried shiitake soaked in for the same savoury depth.\n\n⚖️ THE SPICE LAYER IS BUILT IN THE PAN, NOT DUMPED IN. Heat the sesame oil in the pot, add the garlic and onion, and fry gently for a minute; then the gochugaru and let it bloom in the oil for 30 raw seconds — this is the step that turns flat chilli powder into a rich, red, fragrant base. Stir in the gochujang and soy until the paste dissolves into the oil.\n\nNOW THE STOCK AND THE TOFU. Pour in the stock and bring to a bubble. Add the protein if using — pork belly slices or prawns — and simmer 5 minutes. Then the starring act: spoon the soft tofu in big, impossibly fragile pieces, letting them float rather than stirring them into nothing. The tofu is never cut with a knife and never chopped in a bowl; it is scooped in whole and breaks apart naturally as the stew bubbles — that irregular, patchy texture is the soul of sundubu.\n\n⚖️ THE EGG GOES IN AT THE TABLE, NOT THE STOVE. Crack it into the bubbling centre, turn the heat off, and let the residual bubbling cook the white while the yolk stays molten. The yolk is the finishing sauce of the stew, broken with a spoon right before eating. Serve immediately, straight from the pot, with a bowl of steamed rice — the Korean rule is to alternate bite of stew, bite off plain rice, because the rice is the palette that makes the heat sing, not the thing to be drowned in it.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 20 min","kcal":"~380 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 24g · Carbs 18g · Fat 24g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Sundubu is at its best the moment it is made — the soft tofu is at its silkiest straight from the bubbling pot. Leftover stew keeps a day in the fridge, but the tofu weeps and firms; reheat gently and add a fresh spoon of stock to re-liquefy. The egg is always fresh, cracked at the table.","chefNotes":"Three rules make or break sundubu: a proper kelp-anchovy stock (the depth is in the broth, not the chilli), the spice bloomed in oil before the liquid goes in, and the tofu scooped in whole and never stirred. The egg is the finishing act, always at the table.","pairsWith":"A bowl of plain steamed rice · Kimchi · The same leaves of seasoned seaweed · Cold barley tea between spoonfuls","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Korean Fried Chicken","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Sundubu-jjigae means 'soft tofu stew', and the word 'also' hides real history: sundubu is a Korean-style tofu that is older and firmer than Japanese silken tofu, made the traditional way with natural seawater as the coagulant. The stew became iconic in the 1980s when Korean immigrant grandmothers opened 'sundubu' restaurants in Los Angeles, and the bubbling stone-pot bowl quickly became a Korean-American institution — today it is one of the most-ordered items in Korean food, famous for arriving at the table still boiling, with the raw egg in the diner's control.","howThisFeels":"The whole pot arriving at the table still bubbling, the soft tofu wobbling like a live thing in red broth, and that first warm spoonful of stew followed by a cooling bite of plain rice — pure comfort in liquid form.","leftovers":["Spoon the leftover stew over cold rice with a sesame-oil thread for a warm-cold jjigae donburi — the absorbed broth is the point.","Freeze the stew base (before the tofu and egg) in a tub; a fresh scoop of soft tofu and 5 minutes rebuilds the whole pot.","Drain the stew solids and stir them into an omelette with a little extra sesame — the captured umami disappears into the eggs."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Pork & Soft Tofu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42,"howThisFeels":"The classic balance — a few slices of pork belly give the broth a gentle richness that the soft tofu then cuts right through.","trivia":"Pork and tofu are a classic Korean pairing — the meat adds a little fat and a little sweetness to the otherwise clean, fiery broth, and the soft tofu swallows it all. This is the everyday home version, not the seafood restaurant one.","delta":{},"ingredients":"1 packet soft tofu · 400ml anchovy-kelp stock · 15g gochugaru · 10ml gochujang · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic · 30g onion · 1 spring onion · 1 egg · 40g pork belly, sliced thin · black pepper","method":["Simmer kelp and anchovies in the stock base 5 minutes; strain.","Bloom the garlic and gochugaru in sesame oil, then the gochujang and soy.","Add the stock, then the pork belly; simmer 5 minutes.","Spoon in the tofu in big fragile pieces — never stir it.","Bubble 3 minutes, crack the egg in at the table, serve with rice."]},{"name":"Budget — Egg & Kimchi Only (Vegan-friendly)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan","vegetarian","omnivore"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"No pork, no seafood — just good stock, bloomed chilli and the star of the show, soft tofu. The cheapest honest bowl and genuinely the nearest to the soul of the dish.","trivia":"The temple version of sundubu is exactly this — no meat, no fish broth, just depth from kelp and shiitake, and this is how many Koreans actually eat it at home. The egg is optional; skip it for a fully vegan pot.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"40g pork belly, sliced thin"},{"item":"1 egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"60g kimchi, chopped"},{"item":"extra shiitake for the stock"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add the chopped kimchi with the stock for its fermented tang and a deeper colour. Use a vegetable stock properly deepened with dried shiitake and kelp — the umami carries the whole bowl with no meat at all. Skip the egg for the fully vegan version, or keep it for the classic yolk-as-sauce finish."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 15-Minute Sundubu","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"Bottled anchovy stock and a supermarket packet of soft tofu — the quickest honest sundubu, still bubbling, still with the egg at the table.","trivia":"Even in Korea, the weekday shortcut is a good bottled stock and a packet of soft tofu from the convenience store. The depth that matters is the spice bloom and the never-stir tofu rule — those are the techniques, not the homemade stock.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use a quality bottled anchovy-kelp stock (or instant dashi stock powder, the legitimate Korean shortcut) in place of the homemade simmer. Everything else identical — the bloom, the tofu spooned whole, the egg at the table. The whole pot comes together in about 15 minutes."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-fried-chicken","name":"Yangnyeom Korean Fried Chicken","nameAlt":"Double-fried chicken in a glossy gochujang glaze — chimaek's other half","aliases":["yangnyeom chicken","korean fried chicken","kfc korean","double fried chicken","chimaek"],"course":"main","type":["main","chicken","fried","party","comfort"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","comfort","sports","everyday"],"mood":"High-energy & celebratory","time":"Prep 20 min · Cook 25 min (including the double fry)","health":"Fried — this is an indulgence, but the double fry actually renders MORE fat out of the skin, leaving it crisper AND slightly leaner than a single fry.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce, gochujang) · sesame (sesame oil, sesame seeds) · gluten (flour in the coating)","budget":"★★☆ — chicken pieces are cheap; the sauce is pantry staples","audience":"The party platter — made for sharing with cold beer (chimaek) and fingers.","season":"Game night and celebration food, year-round — but the craving strikes hardest with cold beer in hand","ingredients":"300g chicken pieces (wings or thighs) · 40g cornflour + 20g plain flour for the coating · 1 egg (optional, for a thicker crust) · 20g gochujang · 15g gochugaru or 1 tbsp sweet chilli flakes · 10ml soy sauce · 15ml honey or brown sugar · 5ml sesame oil · 10ml tomato sauce (the Korean fusion nod) · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 1 spring onion, sliced · toasted sesame seeds · oil for frying","method":"THE DOUBLE FRY IS NOT A TECHNIQUE, IT IS THE RECIPE — and it is the entire reason Korean fried chicken eats like a different animal from any other fried chicken. The first fry at a lower heat cooks the chicken through and pushes moisture and fat out; a 5-minute rest; then a SECOND fry at high heat in the same oil, which turns the already-set crust glassy and shatter-crisp. That second crunch is what survives the glaze — a single-fried chicken goes soggy the instant the sauce touches it; a double-fried one stays crackly for an hour. The cornflour in the coating is the second secret: cornflour has no gluten, so it fries into a light, crisp shell instead of a chewy wheat one.\n\nBEFORE YOU FRY, THE COATING. Season the chicken and toss it in a dry mix of 2 parts cornflour to 1 part plain flour. The dry-coat matters: a dry, powdery coat is what blisters into that airy crackle. Rest the coated pieces 10 minutes so the coating sets and grips.\n\nTHE FIRST FRY: 160–165°C, about 7 minutes, until pale gold and just cooked through. The oil should be at a steady gentle bubble — too hot and the outside scorches before the middle cooks. Lift out onto a rack (never paper towel, which steams the bottom), and rest 5 minutes.\n\nTHE SECOND FRY: crank the oil to 185–190°C and fry again 3–4 minutes until deep golden and rock-hard crisp. Drain on the rack again.\n\n⚖️ THE GLAZE IS A PAN, NOT A BOIL. In a separate pan over medium heat, soften the gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, tomato sauce, sesame oil and garlic together for a minute until glossy and warm — the gochujang is the soul: a fermented chilli paste whose slow umami and gentle heat is why this tastes deep, not just spicy-sweet. Toss the hot chicken through the warm glaze just enough to coat, then out. (For the absolute crispiest version, brush the glaze on instead of tossing — this is the wing-specialist's move.)\n\nFINISH with sesame seeds and sliced spring onion, and eat immediately with cold beer — the pairing is so sacred in Korea it has its own name: chimaek (chi from chicken, maek from beer).","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~600 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 34g · Carbs 48g · Fat 30g · Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"Best straight from the fryer while the crunch sings. Keeps 2 days in the fridge — but re-crisp in a hot oven for 5 minutes, never the microwave, which ruins the glassy crust on contact. The glaze, refrigerated separately, keeps a week and vanishes over noodles.","chefNotes":"Double-fry or don't bother — the second fry is the entire dish. Dry-coat with cornflour for a light glassy shell. Glaze from a pan, not a boil. And brush the glaze if you want maximum crunch; toss it if you want maximum clove-on-garlic coating.","pairsWith":"Cold beer (non-negotiable — it IS chimaek) · Pickled radish (the classic yellow cubes, or a sharp local pickle) · More cold beer","goesWith":["Bibimbap","Kimchi Fried Rice","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Korean fried chicken's rise is one of the great fried-food stories. American soldiers brought Southern fried chicken to Korea after the Korean War, and Korean cooks immediately saw the improvement: add a double fry and a sticky-sweet-spicy glaze, and the dish became its own creature — Korea's most-loved delivery food, with fried-chicken restaurants numbering in the tens of thousands. The drink pairing (chimaek) is so culturally wired that marriage-hungry singles in Korea are said to have tried 'chimaek blind dates' — first dates built entirely on chicken and beer. And the glaze's secret ingredient is often a whisper of tomato sauce: not a Western intrusion but a Korean fusion that has been standard since the 1980s, balancing the gochujang's savour with a touch of sweetness.","howThisFeels":"The impossible crunch of the second fry, then the glossy red glaze hitting hot chicken — the smell of garlic and gochujang filling the kitchen, and someone already twisting the cap off a cold beer.","leftovers":["Chop cold leftover pieces small and toss them through rice with the glaze and sesame for a 5-minute yangnyeom fried-rice bowl.","Cold chicken, pulled off the bone, is the star of a kimchi fried-rice with a fried egg the next morning.","The leftover glaze is a sauce for anything — noodles, tofu, or just a spoon (nobody is judging at home)."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Yangnyeom (Gochujang Glaze)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42,"howThisFeels":"The full double-fry with the glossy sweet-spicy-gochujang glaze — the chimaek centrepiece.","trivia":"The classic is the 1980s Seoul formulation — double-fried wings in a gochujang-honey-garlic-tomato glaze. It is the dish that made Korean fried chicken famous worldwide.","delta":{},"ingredients":"300g chicken pieces · 40g cornflour · 20g plain flour · 20g gochujang · 15g gochugaru · 10ml soy sauce · 15ml honey · 5ml sesame oil · 10ml tomato sauce · 2 cloves garlic · spring onion · sesame seeds · oil","method":["Dry-coat seasoned chicken in cornflour-flour mix; rest 10 minutes.","First fry at 160°C, 7 minutes — pale gold, just cooked; rest on a rack 5 minutes.","Second fry at 190°C, 3-4 minutes — deep gold and rock-crisp.","Glaze in a warm pan: gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, tomato sauce, sesame, garlic.","Toss hot chicken through the warm glaze; finish with sesame and spring onion. Serve with cold beer — it is chimaek."]},{"name":"Budget — Single-Fry Soy-Garlic Sticky","icon":"💰","costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"No double fry, a simpler soy-garlic-sugar glaze — still properly crisped and sticky, just without the glass-shatter of the full method.","trivia":"The budget fork trades the second fry for a longer single fry at a steady medium heat, which gets most of the crunch — and swaps the gochujang glaze for a classic soy-garlic-sugar sticky sauce, which is arguably the more traditional Korean flavour (the gochujang glaze is the newer star).","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Fry the coated chicken in a single batch at a steady 175°C for 10-12 minutes until deep golden and fully cooked, then drain on a rack. For the glaze, simmer soy, brown sugar and crushed garlic to a shine — a spoon of gochujang optional. Toss and serve. Most of the crunch, all of the flavour, about half the oil temperature juggling."}]}},{"name":"Quick — Crispy Oven Wings with Yangnyeom","icon":"⚡","costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"The weeknight recipe — dry-baked wings got properly crisp in a hot oven, then the same glossy gochujang glaze.","trivia":"A sprinkle of baking powder over the dry-coated wings, baked at a high heat, crisps them surprisingly close to a fry — the baking powder breaks down into tiny bubbles on the skin. It is the classic Korean-American home shortcut.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Toss the coated wings with ½ tsp baking powder, spread on a rack over a tray, and bake at 220°C for 40 minutes, turning once, until deeply golden and crisp. Warm the glaze, coat, and serve — the oven does the double-fry's job from the outside in."}]}},{"name":"Sticky Crispy Tofu (Veg)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The impossible-crunch tofu version — the same glossy gochujang glaze, and tofu that cracks like it belongs in the double-fry tray.","trivia":"Firm tofu pressed, coated in cornflour and double-fried gives a glassy shell that genuinely holds the glaze — the Korean vegan-fried-chicken answer, and it is better than it has any right to be.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"300g chicken pieces"}],"addIng":[{"item":"250g firm tofu, pressed and cut into thick slabs"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Press the tofu 20 minutes, cut into slabs, coat in cornflour and double-fry exactly as the classic. The cornflour crust is what gives the crackle. For a fully vegan glaze, swap the honey for brown sugar — everything else is naturally vegan (gochujang, soy, tomato sauce)."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-kimchi-jjigae","name":"Kimchi Jjigae","nameAlt":"Kimchi stew — the fridge song that uses up OLD kimchi","aliases":["kimchi jjigae","kimchi stew","doenjang kimchi stew"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","kimchi","one-pot","comfort","lunch"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","winter","comfort","leftover"],"mood":"Warm, tangy & soulful","time":"Prep 10 min · Cook 25 min","health":"Fermented and probiotic — the older kimchi used here is richest in lactobacilli, and this is one of Korea's most comforting fermented meals.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce, gochujang/doenjang, tofu) · seafood in the classic tuna version (swap for 'tofu-only' for vegan) · sesame","budget":"★★★ — one of the cheapest comfort stews there is, built on the kimchi already in the fridge","audience":"The everyday home stew — a bowl of kimchi jjigae is Korea's answer to a grilled-cheese-and-soup night.","season":"Winter is its throne — the colder the day, the better the jjigae — but it rescues nothing-but-old-kimchi nights year-round","ingredients":"100g OLD kimchi (the older, the deeper — days to weeks old is ideal) · 1 knob pork belly or a small tin of tuna (optional, the richness) · 200g firm tofu, cubed · 400ml water or anchovy-kelp stock · 15g gochujang · 5ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 1 spring onion, sliced · a pinch of sugar · black pepper","method":"THE STEW IS A FRIDGE-CLEARING SONG, WRITTEN AROUND OLD KIMCHI — and the older the kimchi, the better the jjigae. Fresh kimchi snaps and fizzes like a jar salad; ageing kimchi turns deeply sour, effervescent and mellow, and it is that aged sourness that carries a whole stew. If your kimchi is young and bright, the honest fix is a splash of vinegar and a pinch of sugar to fake the fermentation curve, and let it cook longer.\n\n⚖️ THE KIMCHI IS SEARED BEFORE IT MEETS ANY LIQUID. This is the step nobody skips in a proper Korean kitchen: heat the sesame oil in the pot, add the drained, roughly chopped kimchi, and fry it HARD until its edges char and the whole pan smells intensely of fermented chilli — about 3 minutes. Raw kimchi dropped into broth tastes like soup with kimchi in it; seared kimchi flavours the entire stew from within. Save the kimchi juice too — a spoon of it goes in with the liquid for extra tang.\n\nNOW THE PROTEIN. Press the pork belly slices (or drained tuna) into the seared kimchi and let them brown with it — this is the 1-minute step that loans the whole pot its deep, savoury backbone. If you are keeping it vegan, skip straight to the broth.\n\nIN WITH THE LIQUID AND THE SAVOURY PASTE. Add the water (or stock), the gochujang, soy, garlic and that pinch of sugar (the sugar bridges the ferment's sourness and the gochujang's funk). Simmer 15 minutes until the kimchi is soft and the broth is red and glossy. The stew can be thin — jjigae is brothier than a curry — but it should coat the spoon.\n\n⚖️ THE TOFU GOES IN LAST, AND STAYS A QUIET FOOTNOTE. Cube firm tofu — never soft, in this stew — add it in the last 5 minutes, and let the heat soak it without stirring it to breaking. Serve bubbling hot with a bowl of steamed rice beside, and alternate bites: stew, then plain rice, then stew. The plain rice is not a side in Korea; it is the cooling palate that lets the heat keep singing.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~320 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 18g · Carbs 16g · Fat 22g · Sodium ~1400mg","storage":"Kimchi jjigae is famously better on day two — the kimchi keeps fermenting and the flavour rounds. Keeps 4 days in the fridge, reheated gently with a splash of water. Freezes well in portions for a month. The tofu is best added fresh each time.","chefNotes":"The three rules: USE OLD KIMCHI (the sourness is the heart), SEAR THE KIMCHI hard before the liquid goes in (this is where the flavour builds), and ADD THE TOFU LAST so it stays intact. A spoon of kimchi juice in the broth is the classic extra tang.","pairsWith":"A bowl of steamed rice · More kimchi (the same pickle, this time raw) · Doenjang-jjigae or a quick side of cucumber banchan","goesWith":["Sundubu Jjigae","Kimchi Fried Rice","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Kimchi jjigae is the true kitchen-sink stew of Korea — the opposite of the fresh, careful banchan. It exists entirely as a use for OLD kimchi: the jars that have been fermenting in the fridge for days and weeks, too sour and mellow to eat raw as banchan, are exactly right for this pot. Every Korean household has a version, and the family argument is usually about pork vs tuna — the pork version is richer, and a small tin of tuna (a beloved budget addition) brings a mellow fishiness that pairs shockingly well with the sour kimchi. In fact, the tuna version is so common in Korean kitchens that tinned tuna and kimchi are considered a match made in the pantry. In Korea the stew is often the winter comfort food that marks the return of cold weather — the first kimchi-jjigae night of autumn is practically a seasonal ritual.","howThisFeels":"The smell of kimchi searing hard in sesame oil before the liquid even goes in — then a bubbling red pot on the table, the tofu bobbing, and the first spoonful that is somehow both sour and comforting all the way down.","leftovers":["The day-two jjigae is a matter of pride — reheat gently, add a splash of water, and the fermented broth is even deeper.","Spoon it over cold rice and top with an egg for a lazy jjigae donburi.","Stir a spoonful into fried rice or noodles for an instant flavour bomb."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Pork Belly Kimchi Jjigae","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The classic — seared kimchi, browned pork slices, and a long simmer that turns the broth red, glossy and deeply savoury.","trivia":"Pork and kimchi are one of Korea's most classic pairings — the pork's fat rounds out the fierce sourness of old kimchi, and the searing step melds them into one flavour before the broth even enters.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g old kimchi · 1 knob pork belly (about 50g), sliced · 200g firm tofu, cubed · 400ml water or stock · 15g gochujang · 5ml soy · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic · 1 spring onion · pinch of sugar · pepper","method":["Sear the drained old kimchi hard in hot sesame oil, 3 minutes, until its edges char.","Press the pork slices in and brown with it, 1 minute.","Add the stock, gochujang, soy, garlic and sugar; simmer 15 minutes until red and glossy.","Add the firm tofu in cubes for the last 5 minutes, never stirring it apart.","Serve bubbling hot with steamed rice — alternate stew and plain rice in every bite."]},{"name":"Budget — Tuna Kimchi Jjigae (Bonus: Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"A small tin of tuna replaces the pork — the beloved budget version that turns out to be the pantry match of the century. Skip the tuna and keep the kelp stock for a fully vegan pot.","trivia":"Tinned tuna in kimchi jjigae is not a poverty substitute — it is a beloved Korean classic in its own right. The drained tuna gets browned with the seared kimchi like the pork would, and its mellow fishiness against the sour fermented chilli is one of those pairings that makes no sense until you taste it.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"1 knob pork belly (about 50g), sliced","to":"1 small tin of tuna, drained (or skip entirely for vegan)"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Drain the tuna and press it into the seared kimchi in place of the pork, letting it brown with the pan — the flakes soak up the fermented oil and carry it through the stew. For the vegan version, skip the tuna and use a deep kelp-and-shiitake stock; gochujang carries the body."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 15-Minute Kimchi Stew","icon":"⚡","costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"Bottled stock and pre-cubed tofu turn the classic into a genuine 15-minute throw-together — the recipe exists for the night the fridge offers nothing but old kimchi, tofu and a busy day.","trivia":"Even fast, the two rules that matter survive: the kimchi gets seared hard before the liquid goes in, and the tofu is added at the end. The stew is as good as the kimchi, and the kimchi is the whole recipe in miniature.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use a strong bottled anchovy or vegetable stock, cube the tofu, and skip any browning beyond the kimchi's essential sear. With the kimchi seared and everything else poured together, the pot is on the table in a quarter-hour. The garlic and a pinch of sugar are still non-negotiable."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-gimbap","name":"Gimbap","nameAlt":"Seaweed rice rolls — the Korean picnic and road-trip food","aliases":["gimbap","kimbap","korean sushi roll","seaweed rice roll"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","rolls","picnic","lunchbox","no-cook"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["picnic","lunchbox","road-trip","lunch","everyday"],"mood":"Fresh, cheerful & portable","time":"Prep 25 min · Roll 10 min (no cooking of the filling beyond what you prep)","health":"Remarkably balanced — rice, lean protein, bright vegetables and sesame oil, with far less sugar or sauce than most lunch options.","allergen":"Sesame (sesame oil in the rice and filling) · soy (soy sauce in some fillings) · gluten (optional ham/nori)","budget":"★★★ — rice, a few vegetables, and one sheet of nori; it is Korea's classic cheap portable lunch","audience":"The lunchbox and picnic hero — children love it, adults find it satisfying, and it travels better than any sandwich.","season":"Year-round; spring picnics and summer holidays are its natural home","ingredients":"150g cooked short-grain rice (freshly steamed, warm) · 1 sheet nori (seaweed for sushi rolls) · 1 egg, beaten · 40g carrot, cut into fine matchsticks and stir-fried briefly · 40g spinach, wilted and seasoned · 30g pickled radish (danmuji, or a sharp pickle) · 1 slice ham OR 40g bulgogi beef (optional) · 5ml sesame oil · 2g salt · a few drops of rice vinegar · toasted sesame seeds","method":"GIMBAP IS KOREA'S ROAD-TRIP FOOD — the rolled seaweed-rice snack that travels in the boot (or picnic basket) and gets eaten by hand beside the highway. It is not sushi; it is Korean through and through, seasoned with sesame oil rather than sushi vinegar's sharpness. The two rules that make it: WARM, SEASONED RICE and a ROLL CLOSED TIGHT.\n\n⚖️ THE RICE IS THE WHOLE TRICK — WARM, NOT HOT, AND SEASONED, NOT PLAIN. Cook the rice, and while it is still warm (too hot steams the nori soggy; too cold and the roll falls apart), season with sesame oil and a pinch of salt — and just a few drops of rice vinegar, the lightest echo of near-sushi. Freshly steamed rice is essential: day-old rice is dry and won't stick together in a roll.\n\n⚖️ EVERY FILLING IS PREPPED SEPARATELY, KOREAN-BANCHAN-STYLE. Cut the carrot into fine matchsticks and stir-fry them in a little sesame oil with a pinch of salt until just tender; wilt the spinach, squeeze it DRY (wet filling is the top gimbap killer) and season with sesame oil and a pinch of salt. The pickled radish (danmuji) — if you can find it, it is the bright-yellow punch that defines gimbap — is used raw. The egg: make a thin omelette sheet and slice it into ribbons.\n\nNOW THE ROLL. Lay the nori shiny-side down on a bamboo mat (or, honestly, a clean tea towel works). Spread the warm seasoned rice in an EVEN, THIN layer across the whole sheet, leaving a bare 2cm strip along the far edge — that bare edge is the glue, and a thin rice layer is the difference between a delicate roll and a bready one. Line the fillings in a row just below the centre: the colourful stripes of carrot, spinach, egg, radish and the ham or beef.\n\n⚖️ ROLL IT TIGHT, LIKE A CIGAR, AND SEAL WITH WATER. The signature move of good gimbap is the tight, even roll — pull the mat's near edge up and over, press the filling into a cylinder, and roll forward firmly, compressing as you go. Dip your finger in water, run it along the bare nori edge, and seal the seam. Rest the sealed roll seam-down for a minute.\n\nSLICE with a very sharp, slightly wet knife — sawing drags the rice out. Brush the finished roll lightly with sesame oil and scatter toasted sesame seeds over it if you want the classic glistening finish. Slice into bite-size rounds and pack them for the journey.\n\nREGIONAL NOTE FOR THE GLOBAL READER: danmuji (the yellow pickled radish) is gimbap's secret weapon and almost defines the taste — it is a bright, sweet-tangy pickle sold at Asian grocers. A sharp pickled cucumber or a good chow-chow-style pickle stands in honestly if you cannot find it.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Roll 10 min","kcal":"~450 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 14g · Carbs 68g · Fat 12g · Sodium ~700mg","storage":"Gimbap is made for the road but is best eaten the same day — the nori softens and the rice dries with time. Keeps a day wrapped tightly in plastic, or up to 2 days in the fridge if the roll is very tight. To revive, brush with sesame oil and wrap in a damp cloth for an hour — the classic picnic trick.","chefNotes":"The two make-or-break rules: warm, sesame-oiled rice (never dry day-old) and a tight roll. Wet filling is the silent killer — squeeze every filling dry. The bare nori edge sealed with water, and slicing with a wet knife, are the two professional moves that turn a roll into gimbap.","pairsWith":"A can of cold cola or barley tea · Kimchi on the side · A crisp soup (many Koreans eat gimbap with a side of mandu-guk or a simple egg drop soup)","goesWith":["Kimchi Fried Rice","Sundubu Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Gimbap evolved from Japanese nori-maki during the Japanese occupation, but it became entirely its own: Korean gimbap uses sesame-oil-seasoned rice (not vinegar-heavy sushi rice), a wider range of cooked vegetables, and is eaten as everyday portable food rather than ceremony. The word gimbap means simply 'seaweed-rice', gim being the Korean word for the nori sheet. It is so tied to travel that Koreans call it 'the official food of the Korean road trip' — sold in bulk at rest stops and gas-station convenience stores, picked up on the way to both the beach and the mountains. The yellow danmuji pickle inside is so essential that any Korean grocery sells tubs of it specifically for gimbap making, and the number of gimbap franchises in Korea runs into the thousands.","howThisFeels":"Unrolling the mat to reveal a perfect glossy cylinder, slicing it into neat rounds with a wet knife — and the small triumph of a bite that is rice, egg, sesame and pickle all at once, eaten warm by the side of the road or cool from the lunchbox.","leftovers":["Fried gimbap (savoury leftover move): slice the day-old rolls thick and pan-fry them in a little oil until golden — the Korean way to make yesterday's gimbap exciting again.","Wrap leftover gimbap in a damp cloth for an hour to revive the nori before eating.","Dice it into a rice bowl with a fried egg for gimbap-style fried rice."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Bulgogi Gimbap","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The classic — seasoned rice, carrot, spinach, egg ribbon, pickled radish and a strip of the marinated beef (or ham). The full gimbap experience.","trivia":"Bulgogi gimbap is the upmarket home version — strips of the pear-marinated beef add a savoury-sweet depth that plain ham cannot match. In Korea the convenience-store version is the daily standard; the home version with good beef is for guests.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g warm short-grain rice · 1 sheet nori · 1 egg · 40g carrot · 40g spinach · 30g pickled radish (danmuji) · 1 slice ham or 40g bulgogi beef · 5ml sesame oil · 2g salt · rice vinegar · sesame seeds","method":["Season the warm rice with sesame oil, salt and a few drops of vinegar.","Prep the fillings separately: stir-fried carrot, wilted-and-squeezed spinach, egg ribbon (thin omelette sheet sliced), pickled radish.","Spread rice in a thin even layer on the nori, leaving a 2cm bare edge.","Line the colourful fillings just below centre, with the beef or ham as the anchor.","Roll TIGHT with a mat, seal the bare edge with water, rest seam-down.","Slice with a wet knife; brush with sesame oil and scatter sesame seeds."]},{"name":"Budget & Quick — Tuna Mayo Gimbap","icon":"💰","costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"Tinned tuna mixed with a little mayo stands in for the pricier beef — the beloved budget filling that the whole country orders from convenience stores.","trivia":"Tuna-mayo gimbap is a genuine Korean favourite, not a compromise one: the creamy tuna against the sesame rice and crunchy pickle is why it is one of the best-selling convenience-store gimbaps in Korea.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"1 slice ham or 40g bulgogi beef","to":"80g tinned tuna, drained, mixed with a little mayo and a pinch of sesame"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Drain the tuna well, mix with a spoonful of mayo, a few drops of sesame oil and a pinch of salt. Use it exactly where the beef would sit. It rolls tighter with a softer filling, so press gently as you roll."}]}},{"name":"Vegetarian (Yachae) Gimbap","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"Every colour the roll can carry — carrot, spinach, egg-free, pickled radish, cucumber — and honestly the crispest, freshest version of all.","trivia":"Vegetable gimbap (yachae gimbap) is a standard make in Korean households, and the pickled radish is what carries its bright tang. For a fully vegan roll, simply skip the egg and use seasoned tofu or just more vegetables — the roll loses nothing.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"1 slice ham or 40g bulgogi beef"},{"item":"1 egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"60g extra vegetables (cucumber, seasoned tofu) in place of the protein and egg"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add cucumber matchsticks and a strip of seasoned or smoked tofu for the savoury anchor. Squeeze the cucumber dry so the roll stays crisp. The sesame-oiled rice and the pickle do the rest — this is the freshest, most colourful gimbap of the set."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-haemul-pajeon","name":"Haemul Pajeon","nameAlt":"Seafood & spring onion pancake — Korea's rain-day comfort","aliases":["haemul pajeon","seafood pancake","korean pancake","pajeon"],"course":"main","type":["main","pancake","seafood","street-food","sharing"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","street-food","comfort","autumn"],"mood":"Comforting & casual","time":"Prep 15 min · Cook 15 min","health":"Moderately rich (pan-fried) but genuinely vegetable-forward — the spring onions are the base, not a garnish.","allergen":"Seafood (prawn, squid — skip for vegan) · gluten (wheat flour) · egg (optional batter) · sesame","budget":"★★☆ — flour and spring onions are cheap; a little seafood stretches a big sharing pancake","audience":"The sharing starter or rain-day supper — cooked whole at the table for a crowd, or quartered as a snack.","season":"A Korean rain-day tradition — the sizzle is the sound of rain, and the pancake is made to be shared","ingredients":"100g spring onions (about 5-6, whole stems) · 50g prawns, chopped · 50g squid or calamari rings, chopped · 80g cake flour · 20g cornflour · 1 egg (optional, for a crisper batter) · 180ml cold water (or light stock) · 5ml soy sauce · 10ml dipping sauce: soy + a splash of vinegar + a pinch of chilli flakes + a drop of sesame oil · pinch of salt · black pepper · oil for frying","method":"PAJEON IS THE PAN-CAKE OF RAIN DAYS — Koreans reach for it the moment the first drops fall, because the sizzle of batter in the pan sounds exactly like rain, and it is a dish built for sharing straight from the pan. THE PRINCIPLE: the spring onions are not a topping, they are the STRUCTURE. You want long, whole-to-halved green stems laid flat like a raft in the pan, not chopped through the batter. Noodle-like strands of onion in a thin, crispy pancake is the look and the mouthfeel of a proper pajeon.\n\nTHE BATTER IS THIN, AND THE SECRET IS COLD WATER. Whisk the flour, cornflour, a pinch of salt, pepper and the cold water (or light stock) into a loose, lump-free batter about the thickness of double cream — noticeably thinner than a Western pancake. Cold liquid is the trick: a cold batter lets the outside crisp while the inside steams, giving that crispy-outside, soft-inside classic pajeon texture. Add the optional egg for a slightly richer, more tender crust.\n\nTHE PAN IS THE SECOND SECRET — FILM OF OIL, THEN THE SHOW. Heat a wide, non-stick pan and coat it with a good slick of oil. Lay the spring onion stems across the pan in a tight log — a thick bundle, thicker than feels right. Pour the batter over the top so it seeps through and around the onions and spreads to the pan's edge: the pancake should look like a golden disc with green onion veins showing through.\n\nDOT THE SEAFOOD ON TOP, PRESS IT IN, AND DO NOT TOUCH IT. The prawns and squid get pressed into the batter surface BEFORE the flip. Then patience: leave the pancake completely alone until the edges are crisp and golden and the base lifts cleanly — the single biggest pajeon mistake is flipping too early and tearing the whole thing. When a spatula slides under easily, flip in one confident, whole-pancake motion.\n\nTHE DIPPING SAUCE IS HALF THE FLAVOUR. While the second side cooks, stir the soy + vinegar + chilli flakes + sesame oil together. Slice the cooked pancake into rough diamonds at the table — the traditional way it arrives — and dip every piece into the sauce. Eat it hot, straight from the pan, because pajeon is the rare dish that genuinely only works fresh: the crispness is the entire point, and it fades in minutes.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 15 min","kcal":"~420 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 20g · Carbs 44g · Fat 18g · Sodium ~900mg","storage":"Best straight from the pan while shatter-crisp. Leftover pajeon keeps a day in the fridge but goes soft; re-crisp in a hot dry pan, never the microwave. The dipping sauce keeps a week in a jar.","chefNotes":"Two rules are everything: a THIN cold-water batter (crisp outside, soft in) and NO TOUCHING until the base is golden and lifts cleanly. Lay the spring onions in long strands, not chopped through the batter — they are the structure, not a filling.","pairsWith":"Cold beer or makgeolli (the classic milky rice wine) · Kimchi · A light soup alongside","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Samgyeopsal","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Pajeon literally means 'spring-onion pancake' — haemul just adds the seafood. It is one of Korea's most famous rain-day foods, based on an old folk superstition that pancakes sizzling in oil 'drive away the rain'. Makgeolli, the traditional pale rice wine, is the classic pairing to the point that the combination has its own word: makgeolli-with-pajeon. In Korea the most famous pajeon is the whole-spring-onion version from Jeonju and the coast — and the batter, in an irony the world enjoys, often includes some rice flour, giving it a satisfying chew.","howThisFeels":"The furious sizzle of batter in a hot, oiled pan on a rainy evening — then the first crisp-edged golden diamond, dipped in the tart-soy sauce, shared between friends who all agree the rain can stay.","leftovers":["Re-crisp leftover pajeon slices in a hot dry pan for the crispness to return — then cut into thin strips and toss them through a quick fried rice.","Use leftover pajeon strips as the crunchy topping for a bibimbap or salad, like a Korean-style cracker."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Haemul Pajeon","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The seafood classic — prawns, squid and whole spring onions in a crispy cold-batter pancake, with the mandatory soy-vinegar chilli dip.","trivia":"The classic is the coastal version: whatever is freshly caught, chopped and dotted over the onions. Prawns and squid are the standard pair; clams are a beloved regional swap.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g spring onions · 50g prawns · 50g squid · 80g cake flour · 20g cornflour · 1 egg · 180ml cold water · soy sauce, vinegar, chilli, sesame for dip · salt · pepper · oil","method":["Lay whole-to-halved spring onion stems in a tight log across a hot oiled pan.","Pour the thin cold-water batter over to seep through and spread to the edges.","Press chopped prawns and squid into the surface; leave completely alone until the base is golden and lifts cleanly.","Flip whole, cook the second side, slice into diamonds.","Dip in the soy-vinegar-chilli-sesame sauce and eat immediately."]},{"name":"Kimchi Pajeon (Budget & Veg)","icon":"💰","diet":["veg"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"The budget classic — just kimchi and spring onions, no seafood — and the ferment's tang makes it possibly the most moreish pajeon of all.","trivia":"Kimchi pajeon is the home-standard cheap version: a splash of the kimchi juice goes straight into the batter, and the fermented tang against the crispy onion pancake is a Korean pantry legend.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"50g prawns, chopped"},{"item":"50g squid or calamari rings, chopped"}],"addIng":[{"item":"80g kimchi, chopped, plus a splash of its juice"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fold the chopped kimchi and a splash of its brine straight into the batter — the juice turns the whole pancake tangy and pink. Lay the spring onion raft, pour the kimchi batter over, and cook exactly as the classic. Skip the egg and use a light vegetable stock for the batter for a fully vegan pancake; the dipping sauce is naturally vegan (soy + vinegar + chilli + sesame)."}]}},{"name":"Quick — The 15-Minute Pan Squared","icon":"⚡","costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The weeknight version — pre-cut fritters instead of one big pancake, so it cooks in half the time and nobody fights over the diamonds.","trivia":"Baking the pajeon as small individual fritters pans faster and needs no flip-courage — the technique is identical at a smaller scale.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Instead of one giant pancake, ladle the batter into 4-5 small rounds in the pan, pressing the onion strands and seafood into each. They cook in about 3 minutes a side and flip with zero risk. Serve the little fritters stacked with the same dipping sauce — same crispness, quarter the flip-nerves."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-doenjang-jjigae","name":"Doenjang Jjigae","nameAlt":"Soybean paste stew — the earthy soul of the Korean table","aliases":["doenjang jjigae","soybean paste stew","doenjang stew","Korean miso stew"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","soybean","vegan","comfort","one-pot"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian","omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","comfort","winter","lunch"],"mood":"Grounded & nourishing","time":"Prep 10 min · Cook 25 min","health":"Genuinely nutritious — fermented soybean paste, vegetables and tofu make this one of the healthiest and most comforting stews in Korean cooking.","allergen":"Soy (doenjang, tofu) · sesame (sesame oil)","budget":"★★★ — soybean paste, a zucchini, a potato and tofu; among the cheapest comfort stews there is","audience":"The everyday home stew that Koreans eat for breakfast, lunch or a cold night — the Japanese-umami equivalent of a bowl of miso soup, grown into a full dinner.","season":"A year-round table stead; at its most comforting in winter","ingredients":"30g doenjang (Korean soybean paste — a miso-like fermented paste, darker and earthier than Japanese miso) · 1 knob zucchini (about 80g), cubed · 1 medium potato (about 100g), cubed · 100g firm tofu, cubed · 300ml kelp stock (or water deepened with a strip of kelp) · 1 clove garlic, minced · 30g onion, sliced · 1 spring onion, sliced · 5ml sesame oil · black pepper","method":"DOENJANG IS THE EARTHY SOUL OF THE KOREAN TABLE — the fermented soybean paste that sits beside gochujang in every home, and is the everyday answer to a pot of miso. Where gochujang is fiery and sweet, doenjang is deep, savoury and slightly funky, and a jjigae made from it is Korean comfort at its plainest and most honest. Do not substitute ordinary miso for it without knowing the difference: miso is lighter and sweeter; doenjang is dark, coarse, more assertive — the authentic version is worth a trip to the Asian grocer.\n\n⛔ THE ONE RULE THAT MAKES OR BREAKS IT: DOENJANG IS NEVER BOILED INTO A STEW FROM THE START. Boiling hard destroys its delicate fermented aroma — Koreans say it 'kills' the paste. The correct method is to dissolve the paste into the simmering stock AFTER the other ingredients have cooked, and never let the pot RAPIDLY BOIL again.\n\nBUILD THE VEGETABLE BASE FIRST. In a small pot, heat the sesame oil and fry the garlic and onion for a minute; add the cubed potato and a splash of the stock, and cook gently 5 minutes until the potato begins to soften. Then add the rest of the stock (or the kelp-deepened water) and bring to a GENTLE simmer — bubbling lazily, not boiling hard.\n\n⚖️ NOW THE DOENJANG — DISSOLVED IN A LADLE, NEVER DUMPED IN. Spoon the paste into a ladle or a small sieve, hold it in the simmering broth, and stir until it dissolves fully. Dissolving it this way — rather than plopping it in a lump — is the professional move that makes it smooth and integrated rather than flaky. Taste and adjust: the paste is salty before you add a pinch of anything, so season almost entirely through it.\n\nADD THE ZUCCHINI AND TOFU, AND STOP. The zucchini goes in for the last 8 minutes so it just softens, and the tofu in cubes for the last 5, so it warms but stays intact. After the paste goes in and the zucchini and tofu join, do not let it return to a hard boil — the aroma of the paste is what this dish is about.\n\nFINISH with the spring onion and a thread of sesame oil off the heat. Serve immediately with a bowl of steamed rice — the classic Korean alternation of a spoonful of stew, then a bite of plain rice, keeps the earthy depth singing.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~320 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 16g · Carbs 40g · Fat 12g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Doenjang jjigae keeps 3 days in the fridge and genuinely deepens as it rests — reheat gently, never boil. Freezes well for a month. The tofu and zucchini soften further, so refresh them when reheating a frozen portion.","chefNotes":"The one rule to remember: do not boil the paste hard. The vegetable base cooks first, the paste dissolves in the simmering liquid through a ladle, and the pot never returns to a hard boil after — that gentle treatment is what keeps the deep fermented aroma that defines the dish.","pairsWith":"A bowl of steamed rice · Fresh kimchi · A quick cucumber or radish banchan · Cold barley tea","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Sundubu Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Doenjang is Korea's everyday fermented soybean paste — a sibling of Japanese miso, but darker, chunkier and more intensely savoury, fermented in the traditional way in clay jars that bury the block in brine. It is one of the pillars of the Korean banchan table, and doenjang-jjigae is widely regarded as the Korean 'miso soup grown into a meal'. The family jjigae pot is such a fixture of Korean home cooking that it has spawned a national saying: 'the soup pot is the heart of the home'.","howThisFeels":"A rustic, earthy, deeply comforting bowl — the dark broth glistening, the zucchini just giving, and the subtle fermented depth that tastes like Korea itself rather than any single ingredient.","leftovers":["The day-two jjigae is even deeper — reheat gently and it is the classic Korean cold-weather lunch.","Spoon the leftover stew over freshly steamed rice topped with a fried egg for an honest jjigae-donburi.","Freeze on its own; the paste-based broth is very freezable and 5 minutes of reheating with fresh tofu rebuilds the pot."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Doenjang Jjigae (Vegan)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"The classic bowl — potato, zucchini and tofu in the earthy paste broth, naturally fully vegan and one of Korea's healthiest comfort stews.","trivia":"The classic is vegan even in Korea — tofu, vegetables and soybean paste, no meat needed. The deep umami comes entirely from the fermentation of the paste and the kelp stock.","delta":{},"ingredients":"30g doenjang · 80g zucchini · 100g potato · 100g firm tofu · 300ml kelp stock · 1 clove garlic · 30g onion · 1 spring onion · 5ml sesame oil · pepper","method":["Fry garlic and onion in sesame oil; add cubed potato and a splash of stock; cook 5 minutes.","Add the rest of the stock; bring to a gentle simmer.","Dissolve the doenjang through a ladle into the simmering broth — never let the pot boil hard after.","Add the zucchini and tofu for the last 8 minutes and 5 minutes respectively.","Finish with spring onion and sesame off the heat; serve with rice."]},{"name":"Doenjang Jjigae with Clams (The Classic in Korea)","icon":"🍲","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"The most common Korean home version — clams add a clean mineral sweetness that the earthy paste loves.","trivia":"Clams (or mussels) are the most traditional add-in to doenjang-jjigae in Korean homes — the clean ocean sweetness against the deep soybean funk is a classic matchup. A little seafood stock with the clams deepens it further.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"100g fresh clams or mussels, well-scrubbed"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add the scrubbed clams with the stock, after the paste has dissolved. Simmer until they just open — 5-7 minutes — then lift them out to serve on top, or leave them in the pot. The broth they release is one of the best things about this version. Discard any that do not open."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 15-Minute Doenjang","icon":"⚡","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":18,"howThisFeels":"The fastest honest version — pre-tinned or frozen vegetables and bottled stock shrink the classic to a genuine quarter-hour bowl.","trivia":"A quality bottled kelp stock and pre-cubed vegetables make the authentic dish nearly instant — the only rule that must survive any shortcut is the no-hard-boil treatment of the paste.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use a good bottled kelp or vegetable stock, tinned (drained) beans or frozen zucchini, and pre-cubed tofu. With the vegetables already cooked, the whole bowl is: gentle simmer, dissolve the paste through a ladle, add the tofu, off the heat. Fifteen minutes, complete, and the paste's aroma intact."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-galbi","name":"Galbi (Korean BBQ Short Ribs)","nameAlt":"Charcoal-grilled marinated short ribs — the heart of Korean barbecue","aliases":["galbi","korean short ribs","kalbi","la galbi","beef short ribs"],"course":"main","type":["main","beef","grill","bbq","celebratory"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["celebration","family","bbq","special"],"mood":"Festive & celebratory","time":"Prep 20 min · Marinate at least 3 h (ideally overnight) · Grill 5-6 min total","health":"Special-occasion rich — short ribs are not an everyday cut, and the marinade is sweet. Savour the centrepiece rather than scale it down.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce) · sesame (sesame oil, sesame seeds) · pear (optional tenderiser)","budget":"★☆☆ — short ribs are the special-tier cut; the rest of the marinade is pantry staples","audience":"The celebration platter — the dish Koreans serve when it must be good, at holidays, birthdays and gatherings.","season":"Any celebration; autumn and winter gatherings are the classic home","ingredients":"400g beef short ribs (flanken-cut, LA-style: thin slices across the bones — ask your butcher) · 40ml soy sauce · 20ml pear or apple juice (the quiet tenderiser) · 15ml honey or brown sugar · 15ml mirin or Korean cooking wine · 10ml sesame oil · 1 tbsp minced garlic · 1 tsp grated ginger · 1 pear halved (for the pan-side char — optional) · black pepper","method":"⚖️ GALBI IS THE KING OF KOREAN BARBECUE — and the cut is everything. The classic uses LA-style (flanken) short ribs: thin strips cut ACROSS the bones, each piece a ribbon of meat with three or four little round bone discs showing. This cut matters because the thin, bone-with-meat slices cook fast, char beautifully, and are eaten by wrapping small bundles in lettuce or eating as they come. Ask your butcher to cut the ribs flanken-style (about 1cm thick, across the bone) — getting the right cut is 70% of a great galbi.\n\nTHE MARINADE IS THE SECOND HALF — AND THE PEAR IS THE SECRET. Whisk the soy, pear juice, honey, mirin, sesame oil, garlic and ginger together. The pear's enzymes (papain-family) tenderise the meat exactly like they do in bulgogi, and its natural sugars are what caramelise into that lacquered, glossy char. Thirty minutes of marinating is the floor; overnight is the peak — galbi is one of the few dishes where the difference between 30 minutes and 8 hours is genuinely night and day.\n\n⚖️ THE GRILL, AND THE GRILL'S ONE RULE: DO NOT LET THE SUGAR BURN. The glaze's sugars are what make galbi taste so good and what make it burn so easily. Grill over MEDIUM heat — glowing coals rather than open flame — and turn the ribs frequently, around every 30 seconds, so the sugars caramelise into lacquer rather than scorch into bitter char. Watch the drips: if the fat flares and billows black smoke, move the ribs to a cooler zone immediately.\n\nTHE TRADITIONAL FLIP: many Korean cooks place a halved pear, cut-side down, on the grill beside the ribs and use it to press the meat — the pear's juice keeps the surface moist and adds a whisper of fruit. It is old-school theatre and genuinely good.\n\nREST THE RIB RIBBONS ONE MINUTE, CUT ONCE, AND EAT. Cut each ribbon into two pieces at the table (scissors, the Korean way), so each piece keeps its bone handle. Serve with lettuce or perilla leaves, ssamjang, raw garlic and sliced chilli for the classic wrap — and steamed rice on the side, because galbi without rice is a smile without the reason.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Marinate 3 h–overnight · Grill 5-6 min total","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 30g · Carbs 20g · Fat 36g · Sodium ~900mg","storage":"The uncooked marinated ribs keep 2 days in the fridge and freeze beautifully for a month — the marinade protects the meat. Cooked galbi keeps 3 days, is superb cold, and famously improves into tomorrow's fried rice or a galbi-jjim-style braise.","chefNotes":"The flanken cross-bone cut is non-negotiable, the pear is the quiet star, and the grill must run MEDIUM with frequent turns — the sugar glaze is the flavour and the enemy. Rest the ribbons, cut with scissors, wrap in lettuce.","pairsWith":"Lettuce or perilla wraps with ssamjang, raw garlic and chilli · Steamed rice · Cold soju or beer","goesWith":["Samgyeopsal","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Galbi means 'rib' — and the LA-style cut owes its existence to Korean immigrants in Los Angeles, who adapted the traditional long Korean cut to the flanken style butchers there already knew. It was the dish that built many a Korean-American restaurant. At home in Korea, galbi is the celebratory meat of Korean barbecue: often served as a whole long bone at the grill and cut at the table with scissors, and so tied to gathering that a 'galbi day' with family is considered the perfect weekend.","howThisFeels":"The lacquered, glossy sheen of a properly grilled rib, the smoke rising off the coals, and the first bite — charred and caramelised at the edge, pear-tender in the middle — wrapped in cool lettuce with a sting of garlic.","leftovers":["Chop leftover galbi off the bones and stir it through cold-day-old rice with a spoon of gochujang — galbi fried rice, the family day-two favourite.","Make galbi-jjim: braise leftover ribs with soy-sweet broth, potato and carrot for a slow-cooked second meal.","Freeze cooked galbi in the marinade-juice; thaw and reheat gently in a pan."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"LA Galbi (Flanken Short Ribs)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":62,"howThisFeels":"The classic — flanken-cut short ribs, pear-soy marinade, lacquered over a medium grill, eaten in lettuce wraps.","trivia":"The LA-style flanken cut across the bones is the modern standard and the reason the dish cooks so fast: each thin slice carries its own little bone handles and chars beautifully.","delta":{},"ingredients":"400g flanken-cut short ribs · 40ml soy sauce · 20ml pear juice · 15ml honey · 15ml mirin · 10ml sesame oil · 1 tbsp garlic · 1 tsp ginger · pear half for the grill (optional) · pepper","method":["Whisk the soy, pear, honey, mirin, sesame, garlic and ginger; marinate the flanken ribs 3 hours to overnight.","Grill over MEDIUM heat, turning every 30 seconds so the sugars lacquer rather than burn.","Press the cut-side of a halved pear onto the meat as it grills (traditional and good).","Rest 1 minute, cut each ribbon once with scissors, wrap in lettuce with ssamjang and garlic.","Serve with steamed rice and cold soju."]},{"name":"Chicken Galbi (Dak Galbi — Budget)","icon":"🐔","costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The same pear-soy lacquer on chicken thigh — juicier, cheaper, and the most popular weeknight barbecue substitute in Korea itself.","trivia":"Dak galbi (chicken ribs) uses boneless chicken thigh with the same marinade — the thigh's fat keeps it silky where breast dries out. It is the budget barbecue hero.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"400g beef short ribs, flanken-cut","to":"400g chicken thigh, boneless, in thin medallions"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Chicken needs a full grill to cook through — give each medal 2 minutes a side over medium heat rather than the fast beef char. Same marinade, same lacquer, same lettuce-wrap table."}]}},{"name":"Grilled Mushroom & Pear Galbi-Ssam (Veg)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The galbi ritual, given over to the grill — big mushroom caps basted in the same pear-soy glaze, wrapped with raw garlic in lettuce. A vegan centrepiece the table argues over.","trivia":"The ssam ritual was never about the meat — char-grilled mushrooms with the galbi marinade, wrapped with garlic and chilli, are a beloved Korean barbecue vegetable order, and the marinade's pear sweetness becomes a glaze on the mushrooms.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"400g beef short ribs, flanken-cut"}],"addIng":[{"item":"250g large portobello or shiitake mushrooms, thicksliced"},{"item":"extra sesame oil for the marinade brush"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Marinate the thick-sliced mushrooms in the same galbi soy-pear glaze for 20 minutes, then grill over medium heat until deeply lacquered and tender — about 3 minutes a side. Serve exactly as the classic: lettuce wraps, ssamjang, raw garlic, chilli. The marinade is naturally vegan; it just needs mushrooms to star."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-bossam","name":"Bossam","nameAlt":"Boiled pork belly wraps — the laid-back party feast","aliases":["bossam","boiled pork belly","korean pork wraps","suyuk"],"course":"main","type":["main","pork","no-fry","slow","celebratory","sharing"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","celebration","casual","family"],"mood":"Relaxed & festive","time":"Prep 15 min · Boil & rest 2 h 30 min","health":"Far leaner than a fried pork — boiled belly sheds much of its fat in the cooking liquid; the garlic and greens keep it balanced.","allergen":"Soy (ssamjang) · sesame · mustard (in the classic ssam table) · gluten (soy sauce)","budget":"★★☆ — a belly roast is affordable for a crowd; it is one of Korea's great budget-feast centrepieces","audience":"The no-fuss party dish — slow-boiled, sliced, and everyone wraps their own at the table.","season":"Any gathering; a favourite at holidays and family dinners","ingredients":"400g pork belly, in one solid slab · 4 cloves garlic · 1-2 slices ginger · 2 spring onions, chopped · 1 tbsp doenjang or a splash of soju (optional but traditional) · 20g cabbage leaves or lettuce · 20g ssamjang (or mix from gochujang + doenjang + sesame + garlic) · 8 fresh oysters (optional, the classic luxury) · 2 cloves raw garlic, sliced · 1 green chilli, sliced · a little radish pickle (danmuji) · black pepper","method":"⚖️ BOSSAM IS THE GREAT UN-FANCY FEAST — a whole slab of pork belly boiled with aromatics until meltingly tender, sliced thick, and served with a live 'ssam table' of pickles, garlic, greens and, classically, raw oysters. It is the party food that asks for almost no attention and delivers a table everyone builds differently.\n\nBOIL THE BELLY LOW AND LONG — THIS IS THE WHOLE TECHNIQUE. Place the whole slab in a pot, cover with cold water, and bring to a bare simmer. Skim the scum that rises in the first ten minutes (it will cloud the broth), then add the garlic, ginger, spring onions and a spoon of doenjang or a splash of soju — both optional but traditional, adding a quiet savour-note to the pork as it cooks. Simmer gently, never boiling hard, for about 2 hours — the pork should be so tender that a chopstick slides through with no resistance. The slow simmer is the secret; a hard boil gives tough, dense belly instead of soft, cake-like slices.\n\nREST 10 MINUTES, THEN SLICE THICK — the rest is as important here as for a roast, giving the juices time to settle before the belly is cut into thick, 1cm slices, each one a little tower of clean pork. Serve the slices in a pile, still warm.\n\n⚖️ THE SSAM TABLE IS THE SECOND HALF — AND THE OYSTER IS THE CLASSIC SURPRISE. Around the pork go: crisp cabbage or lettuce leaves, ssamjang, sliced raw garlic, green chilli rings, the radish pickle, and — if you can — fresh oysters. The classic bossam wrap is a cabbage leaf loaded with pork, cold raw oyster, garlic, a swipe of ssamjang and a chilli ring, folded into one full bite. The briny cold oyster against the warm clean pork is one of Korea's great taste collisions, and why bossam is considered a special-occasion dish.\n\nEVERYONE WRAPS THEIR OWN — that is the form. No plates needed beyond a leaf and a lap, and the table does the work. Steamed rice and a simple cold soup complete the spread.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Boil & rest 2 h 30 min","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 28g · Carbs 8g · Fat 42g · Sodium ~700mg","storage":"Cooked bossam keeps 3 days in the fridge, and is famously spectacular cold — the traditional bossam drinking snack is cold slices with garlic. Reheat gently in a steamer, or eat cold. The boiled belly also freezes for a month.","chefNotes":"Low and slow is the whole technique — a bare simmer, never a boil, with the aromatics lending quiet depth. Rest before slicing, slice thick, and let the oyster-garlic-ssamjang wrap do the rest. The table is the recipe.","pairsWith":"Fresh oysters (the classic luxury) · Ssamjang, raw garlic, chilli · Radish kimchi or pickle · Cold beer or soju","goesWith":["Samgyeopsal","Kimchi Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Bossam means 'wrapped' — from the Korean verb to wrap, capturing the dish's entire character. It is a party dish sometimes translated as 'boiled pork belly wraps', and in Korea it is a favourite at holidays and family tables for being genuinely low-effort: the boil does the work, and the wow is in the table of accompaniments rather than any complex technique. The cooked pork is also the basis of the 'bossam kimchi' specialty — cabbage leaves stuffed with seasoned minced pork, a celebrated Korean side dish in its own right.","howThisFeels":"The long slow simmer filling the kitchen with gentle pork scent, then the reveal: thick, tender slices of white-clean pork in a stack, the oyster-garlic-chilli table around it, and the first wrap that fuses warm clean meat with cold briny oyster — a bite that makes the table go quiet.","leftovers":["Eat the cold slices as a drinking snack with garlic and ssamjang — the most authentic day-two move there is.","Shred leftover bossam through kimchi fried rice or over a bowl of rice with the pickles.","Use the leftover cooking liquid as a rich soup base — add kimchi and you have kimchi jjigae in minutes."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Bossam with Oyster","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"The full spectacle — slow-boiled whole belly, thick slices, the oyster-garlic-ssamjang table.","trivia":"The oyster is the classic that makes it a celebration — the raw cold oyster in the warm pork wrap is one of Korea's great taste moments.","delta":{},"ingredients":"400g pork belly slab · 4 cloves garlic · 2 slices ginger · 2 spring onions · 1 tbsp doenjang · cabbage leaves · 20g ssamjang · 8 fresh oysters (optional) · 2 cloves raw garlic · 1 green chilli · radish pickle · pepper","method":["Simmer the whole belly slab in cold water with garlic, ginger, spring onion and a spoon of doenjang for 2 hours until chopstick-tender.","Rest 10 minutes, slice into thick 1cm towers.","Set the ssam table: cabbage leaves, ssamjang, raw garlic, chilli, radish pickle, fresh oysters.","Wrap: leaf, warm pork, cold raw oyster, garlic, chilli, ssamjang — one full bite.","Repeat until the table is happy."]},{"name":"Budget Bossam — No Oysters & Quicker Boil","icon":"💰","costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The same slow-simmered belly, no oysters, cooking time somewhat shrunk with a faster cut — every bit the party centrepiece without the luxury add-on.","trivia":"The oysters are the luxury, not the essence — bossam without them is still a beloved Korean party dish, with the garlic, chilli and ssamjang wrap doing all the work.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"8 fresh oysters (optional, the classic luxury)"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Without the oysters, the wrap is still complete: warm pork, strong garlic, chilli and the ssamjang is the everyday bossam — and if you are short on time, a 1kg piece cooks fine in 1.5-1.75 hours as long as it stays at a bare simmer."}]}},{"name":"Tofu Bossam (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"Pan-crisped tofu slabs taking the centre of the same ssam table — a vegan bossam that keeps the wrap ritual and its bright table.","trivia":"Korean temple cooking has always done exactly this — firm tofu, crisped in the pan, wrapped with the bright garlic-and-pickle table. The wrap is the point, and tofu wears it beautifully.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"400g pork belly, in one solid slab"}],"addIng":[{"item":"300g extra-firm tofu, pressed and thick-sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Press the tofu, slice into thick slabs, and pan-fry in a little sesame oil until deeply golden and crisp on both sides. Serve as the centre of the same ssam table — cabbage leaves, ssamjang (use a maple-sweetened gochujang-doenjang mix for full vegan), garlic, chilli, pickle. The wrap ritual survives completely healthily."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-naengmyeon","name":"Mul Naengmyeon","nameAlt":"Cold buckwheat noodles in icy broth — Korea's heat-beating summer bowl","aliases":["naengmyeon","mul naengmyeon","cold noodles","korean cold buckwheat noodles"],"course":"main","type":["main","noodles","cold","summer","light"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["summer","everyday","lunch","refresh"],"mood":"Refreshing & clarifying","time":"Prep 10 min (+chill 1 h) · Cook 5 min","health":"Genuinely light and refreshing — buckwheat noodles, icy broth, shredded vegetables and protein; a hot-day bowl that is a full meal without the heaviness.","allergen":"Buckwheat (in the noodles) · soy (soy, gochujang or mustard in the seasoning) · egg (classic garnish) · sesame","budget":"★★★ — noodles and broth, shredded vegetables and an egg; one of the cheapest peaks in Korean summer eating","audience":"The summer-rescue bowl — served icy cold, it is Korea's answer to the heatwave, and the single dish that defines Korean summer food.","season":"SUMMER — unambiguously. It is eaten when the temperature demands ice in the broth, and barely makes sense in winter.","ingredients":"100g mul naengmyeon noodles (Korean cold buckwheat noodles — buckwheat with a little starch; the greyish-brown kind) · 1 egg, hard-boiled · 50g cucumber, cut into fine matchsticks · 40g Korean pear or apple, cut into matchsticks · 1 tsp mustard (or gochujang + vinegar for the red version) · 1 tbsp rice vinegar · 5ml soy sauce · 1 tsp sugar · 10ml ice-cold dongchimi radish water, or iced broth made from kelp + water, well-chilled · ice cubes · toasted sesame seeds","method":"⚖️ NAENGMYEON IS KOREA'S DEFINING SUMMER BOWL — a tangle of cold buckwheat noodles in an ice-cold broth, sweetened and sharpened, eaten when the mercury decides brute force is the only response. The two things that make it work: the broth must be genuinely icy, and the noodles must be chewy, not soft.\n\nTHE BROTH IS THE SOUL. The classic is dongchimi — the clear, tart, chilled brine of winter-radish kimchi — or a light kelp-and-water broth chilled to icy. For the honest home version: steep a strip of kelp in cold water for 30 minutes, add the rice vinegar, soy, sugar, and a little mustard (or a spoon of gochujang for the red version), and chill it thoroughly. Whatever you use, the broth must be BRISKLY COLD — traditional naengmyeon is served with ice cubes floating in it, and the iciness is not decorative, it is the dish.\n\nTHE NOODLES ARE CHEWY — because they are buckwheat and starch, and that pairing is the whole texture. Cook the noodles per the packet (usually 3-4 minutes for the thin grey-brown mul naengmyeon kind), then the secret step: rinse them under cold running water while rubbing gently with your hands, then drain well — this stops the cooking instantly AND washes off the excess starch, leaving each strand slippery and separate, not clumped. Then give them one final swish in iced water to chill through.\n\nTANGLE, GARNISH, AND FREEZE AS YOU EAT. Coil the cold noodles high in the middle of the bowl like a nest, pour the icy broth around, and crown with the cucumber and pear matchsticks, half the boiled egg, a thread of mustard (or gochujang-vinegar), and sesame seeds. Add the ice cubes. Eat immediately, using scissors to cut the long noodles if they resist — cold noodles always fight back a little, and Korean tables keep scissors on hand for exactly this.\n\n⚖️ THE KOREAN RULE: EAT IT FAST. A naengmyeon is at its peak for about fifteen minutes, before the broth warms and the noodles slacken. It is a dish to be devoured, not savoured slowly.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min (+chill 1 h) · Cook 5 min","kcal":"~380 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 16g · Carbs 62g · Fat 8g · Sodium ~800mg","storage":"Best eaten immediately at its icy peak. The chilled seasoned broth keeps 2 days refrigerated — re-chill it hard before use. Cooked noodles keep a day rinsed in cold water and drained; they are served cold the next day, no reheating, with a fresh pour of icy broth.","chefNotes":"The broth must be BRISKLY cold — ice cubes are part of the serving, not a garnish. Rinse the cooked noodles under cold water and give them a final iced swish so each strand stays separate and chewy. Eat immediately at its icy peak.","pairsWith":"A little mustard or gochujang at the table · Hard-boiled egg and pear for sweetness · The coldest room in the house","goesWith":["Gimbap","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Naengmyeon means 'cold noodles', and in Korea it is the un-negotiable food of summer — restaurants advertise 'mul naengmyeon' with the kind of chill-marketing other nations give ice cream. It originated in the northern Korean provinces of Pyeongyang and Hamhung, where the cold climate and the buckwheat fields made cold noodle broths a regional staple long before they became the nationwide heat-beater. The tradition of eating long noodles is also tied to birthday wishes for long life, which is why naengmyeon, despite being the lightest dish of the Korean table, is one of the foods most associated with celebration.","howThisFeels":"The first shock of genuinely icy broth, the chewy-slippery tangle of buckwheat noodles, and the sweet clean crunch of pear and cucumber between mouthfuls — the single most refreshing thing on the Korean table on a brutal summer day.","leftovers":["The cooked, rinsed noodles keep a day cold — serve tomorrow with a fresh icy broth pour, no reheating.","The seasoned broth keeps 2 days chilled; it is the base for a fast second bowl with fresh noodles.","Leftover garnishes (cucumber, egg, pear) become a fresh salad bowl with sesame."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Mul Naengmyeon (Icy Broth)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The classic — buckwheat noodle nest in genuinely icy broth, with cucumber, pear and a boiled egg, mustard on the side.","trivia":"The classic is the Pyeongyang-style mul naengmyeon: icy broth, chewy buckwheat noodles, and the sweet-salty balance of pear and pickle.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g mul naengmyeon noodles · 1 egg · 50g cucumber · 40g pear · mustard · rice vinegar · soy · sugar · ice-cold kelp/radish broth · ice cubes · sesame seeds","method":["Chill the seasoned kelp broth hard (ice cubes in the bowl at the end).","Cook the noodles 3-4 min, rinse in cold water rubbing gently, drain, and swish in iced water.","Coil the cold noodles high like a nest, pour icy broth around, garnish with cucumber, pear, halved boiled egg, mustard, sesame.","Add ice cubes and eat immediately — a naengmyeon lives about fifteen minutes."]},{"name":"Bibim Naengmyeon (Spicy Red, No Broth)","icon":"🌶️","diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"The Hamhung-style fiery twin — the same chewy cold noodles tossed in a cold gochujang sauce with no broth, more pike, more kick.","trivia":"Hamhung-style bibim naengmyeon is famously spicy — seasoned with gochujang, garlic and a sharp cold sauce, and unlike its mul cousin, served without broth. It is Korea's heat-on-heat answer to a summer day.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"For the red broth-less version: toss the cold rinsed noodles in a cold sauce of gochujang, rice vinegar, soy, sugar, garlic and a thread of sesame oil, then garnish with the cucumber, pear, egg and sesame. Serve with a small side cup of cold water or broth to sip — the classic bibim naengmyeon side. For a fully vegan bowl, skip the egg and use a gochujang-sugar sauce; the rest is naturally plant-based."}]}}]},{"id":"korea-yukgaejang","name":"Yukgaejang","nameAlt":"Spicy beef & vegetable soup — the bone-cleansing fire bowl","aliases":["yukgaejang","spicy beef soup","korean beef vegetable soup","yukkaejang"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","beef","spicy","comfort","winter","restorative"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","comfort","winter","restorative"],"mood":"Fiery & restorative","time":"Prep 15 min · Simmer 1 h 45 min","health":"A genuinely restorative bowl — lean beef and vegetables in a fiery, garlicky broth. The heat is real purpose: this is the dish Koreans reach for when they feel a cold arriving.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce) · sesame (sesame oil)","budget":"★★☆ — beef brisket is the main cost; the vegetables are cheap and the broth is pantry-built","audience":"The restorative weekday soup — Korea's answer to the pot of soup that heals whatever has you down.","season":"Winter, cold nights, and the first shiver of a cold — though its fans will eat it in summer rain without blinking","ingredients":"150g beef brisket or shin, in a chunk · 30g gosari (dried fernbrake — see note; or rehydrated mushrooms) · 25g doraji (dried bellflower root — optional; or extra bean sprouts) · 60g bean sprouts · 100g spring onions (a whole generous bunch — this soup is a bed of them) · 15g gochugaru (Korean chilli flakes) · 10ml soy sauce · 10ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 1 slice ginger · 1 egg (for the classic yolk-and-white ribbons) · black pepper · salt","method":"YUKGAEJANG IS KOREA'S FIREY RESTORATIVE — a bowl that tastes the way a thermos feels before a long winter walk. Its literal name carries its purpose: yukgaejang is said to mean 'beef soup served to someone who has suffered', and it is the soup Koreans cook for themselves when a cold is circling. IT IS BUILT IN TWO CHAPTERS — a long quiet simmer that makes the beef shreddable, then a fiery 30-minute finish that builds the broth.\n\nFIRST, THE DRIED MOUNTAIN VEGETABLES. Traditional yukgaejang is defined by two wild ingredients the global reader may not know: gosari (dried fernbrake — rehydrated it is chewy and earthy, the soul of the soup's texture) and doraji (dried bellflower root — a crisp, slightly bitter root like a wild parsnip). Both are sold at Asian grocers and are worth hunting for once. The honest global swaps: gosari → any rehydrated mushroom (shiitake or wood ear); doraji → an extra handful of bean sprouts. Soak the gosari and doraji in cold water 2 hours (or quick-soak in hot water 30 minutes), then simmer them 20 minutes in fresh water to soften before they join the soup.\n\n⚖️ CHAPTER ONE — THE QUIET SIMMER THAT MAKES THE BEEF. Put the beef in a pot with cold water to cover, the ginger, a few of the spring onion whites, and bring to a boil. Skim the scum that rises, then lower to a bare simmer for 1 hour 15 minutes — the beef is done when a chopstick slides through and it can be shredded by hand. Lift the beef out, let it cool, and SHRED it into rough strands — the shredded beef is the texture of the finished bowl, never cubed meat. Strain the broth and keep every drop.\n\n⚖️ CHAPTER TWO — THE FIRE. Heat the sesame oil in the same pot, add the gochugaru and garlic, and stir 30 seconds until the flakes bloom into a deep red oil. Add the shredded beef, the softened gosari and doraji (or their swaps), the soy, and a good splash of the broth, and let the whole thing come together over medium heat — this is where the soup gains its colour and its bones.\n\nADD THE REMAINING BROTH AND THE BEAN SPROUTS, and simmer 25-30 minutes until the broth is deeply red and glossy. Season with salt and black pepper — the soup should be savoury and fiery, with the beef tasting of itself.\n\n⚖️ THE SIGNATURE: THE SPRING ONION BED AND THE EGG RIBBONS. A proper yukgaejang carries a MOUND of spring onions — sliced thick and stirred through for the last 5 minutes so they stay a little toothsome. And the classic garnish is a separate pan-beaten egg, cooked into a thin sheet and sliced into white and yellow ribbons, scattered over the top like a ribbon in a gift. Serve with steamed rice, and alternate spoonfuls of soup and bites of plain rice as the heat demands.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Simmer 1 h 45 min","kcal":"~420 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 34g · Carbs 12g · Fat 26g · Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"Famously better on day two — the beef drinks the broth and stays tender. Keeps 4 days in the fridge and freezes perfectly for 2 months; it is one of the best freezers in Korean cooking. Reheat gently — the beef can go from silky to stringy with a hard boil.","chefNotes":"Two chapters, never one: the long quiet simmer that makes the beef shreddable, then the fiery finish that builds the broth. The gochugaru is bloomed in oil first — that step is the difference between a fire bowl and a watery chilli soup. And the spring onions are not a garnish; they are a whole bed of texture.","pairsWith":"Steamed rice · Kimchi · A cold pickled radish side · Cold barley tea between spoonfuls","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Doenjang Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Yukgaejang's name is its story — 'beef soup served to someone who has suffered', which is why it is Korea's designated restorative. The dried mountain vegetables give it its particular chewiness: gosari (fernbrake) and doraji (bellflower root) are foraged wild plants, dried for the pantry, and they are the reason the soup is as much about texture as taste. In Korean restaurants it is served in a sizzling stone bowl that keeps bubbling when it reaches the table, and it has a long-standing reputation as the hangover cure and the cold-season fix in equal measure.","howThisFeels":"The deep red glossy broth, the shreds of tender beef, and that first fiery spoonful that warms from the stomach outwards — the spring onion and egg ribbon finish making it a bowl that somehow tastes both powerful and comforting.","leftovers":["Day-two yukgaejang is a matter of pride — reheat gently and the broth is deeper, the beef softer.","Serve the leftover soup over freshly cooked noodles for a fiery yukgaejang-guksu the next day.","The leftover broth alone is gold: freeze it and use it as the stock base for a kimchi-jjigae."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic with Gosari & Doraji","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":38,"howThisFeels":"The complete restorative — shredded beef, chewy fernbrake, crisp root, and the fire bowl of spring onions and egg ribbons.","trivia":"The classic is the foraged-vegetable version — gosari and doraji give the soup its signature chew, and the egg ribbons the restaurant finish.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g beef brisket · 30g gosari (soaked) · 25g doraji (soaked) · 60g bean sprouts · 100g spring onions · 15g gochugaru · 10ml soy · 10ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic · 1 slice ginger · 1 egg · pepper · salt","method":["Soak the gosari and doraji; simmer 20 minutes in fresh water until softened.","Simmer the beef in cold water with ginger and spring onion whites for 1 h 15 min until shreddable; shred it; keep the broth.","Bloom gochugaru and garlic in sesame oil 30 seconds; add beef, vegetables, soy and broth.","Simmer 25-30 min until deep red and glossy; season.","Stir in the spring onion mound for the last 5 min; finish with egg ribbons. Serve with rice."]},{"name":"Quick Pressure-Cooker Yukgaejang","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The same fire bowl in roughly half the time — a pressure cooker does Chapter One in 30 minutes instead of 75.","trivia":"A pressure cooker genuinely transforms this soup: the beef reaches shreddable tenderness in 30 minutes, and the broth gains depth faster because the pressure pushes flavour in. Everything after the simmer is identical.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Pressure-cook the beef with the ginger, aromatics and water for 30-40 minutes until fork-tender, then shred. Bloom the gochugaru in the open pot and follow Chapter Two exactly — the whole bowl comes together in about 1 hour with no sacrifice. Pre-sliced stir-fry beef can even skip the long simmer entirely: use it shreddable-cooked in 5 minutes, at the cost of a lighter broth."}]}},{"name":"Vegetarian Yukgaejang (Mushroom)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"The same fiery red broth and the same mountain-vegetable chew, with mushrooms and tofu standing in for the beef — a vegetarian soup that keeps the fire alive.","trivia":"The gosari and doraji are vegetables already — a vegetarian yukgaejang simply swaps the beef for a mix of shiitake, oyster mushrooms and tofu, and deepens the broth with kelp and mushroom stock. The temple kitchen tradition of Korean vegetarian cooking has always kept the spice.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"150g beef brisket or shin, in a chunk"}],"addIng":[{"item":"120g mixed mushrooms (shiitake, oyster, button), sliced"},{"item":"100g firm tofu, cubed"},{"item":"kelp and shiitake stock in place of beef broth"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Brown the sliced mushrooms hard in the sesame oil before the gochugaru blooms — they carry the savour the beef would have lent. Use a deep kelp-and-shiitake stock for the broth, skip the ginger beef simmer, and add the tofu in the last 5 minutes so it warms through. Everything else — the fire, the spring onions, the egg ribbons (skip for vegan) — stands exactly as the classic."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-samgyetang","name":"Samgyetang","nameAlt":"Ginseng chicken soup — the whole-chicken summer health bowl","aliases":["samgyetang","ginseng chicken soup","korean ginseng soup","samgye-tang"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","chicken","whole-chicken","health","celebration"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["health","summer","celebration","special"],"mood":"Restorative & nurturing","time":"Prep 20 min · Simmer 1 h 15 min","health":"The legendary Korean health soup — whole chicken stuffed with glutinous rice and ginseng, eaten as a restorative. The ginseng is the point: warming, grounding, the traditional cure for summer heat-exhaustion.","allergen":"Ginseng (the herb) · chicken · rice","budget":"★★☆ — a small whole chicken is the cost; ginseng is a pantry hero you use a pinch of","audience":"The special health meal — one whole chicken per person at its most traditional, or one big bird shared for two.","season":"The famous summer bowl — eaten on the three sam-bok days (the hottest days of the Korean summer) as a warm-you-in-the-heat cure","ingredients":"1 small whole chicken (about 600-800g — corn-fed or small broiler) · 60g glutinous rice (sweet rice — soaked 30 minutes) · 1 medium ginseng root (or 1 tsp ginseng powder, or 2 sachets ginseng tea) · 4 cloves garlic · 3 red dates (jujube — optional but traditional) · 2 slices ginger · 2 spring onions · 1L water · salt & pepper · optional: a few chestnuts or ginkgo nuts if you find them","method":"⚖️ SAMGYETANG IS KOREA'S SUMMER MEDICINE — a whole small chicken stuffed with glutinous rice, garlic and a ginseng root, simmered until it is so tender it sheds its own meat, served as a milky soup. It is the dish Koreans eat on the hottest three days of summer (sam-bok) — the logic being that heat inside beats heat outside, and the ginseng plus chicken restores what summer drains. It IS medicine, prepared as food.\n\n⚖️ THE STUFFING IS THE SOUL — AND OVERSTUFFING IS THE SIN. Soak the glutinous rice 30 minutes first; it doubles as it cooks, so the cavity takes only about two-thirds full. Into the cavity go the drained rice, the ginseng root (or a generous pinch of ginseng powder — the root's strong earthy-bitter heart), the garlic cloves, the jujube and a slice of ginger. Truss the chicken closed with kitchen string, or simply tuck the legs to hold it — the trussing keeps the rice from boiling out.\n\nSET THE CHICKEN IN A POT, BREAST UP, AND COVER WITH COLD WATER. Add the remaining ginger and a spring onion. Bring to a boil, skim the scum thoroughly, and drop to the gentlest simmer — the pot should just barely tremble. Simmer 1 hour to 1 hour 15 minutes, turning the chicken once at the halfway point. The chicken is done when it is fall-apart tender and the broth has turned milky white — that milky transformation is the collagen emulsifying into the water, and it is the mark of a properly long, gentle simmer.\n\n⚖️ THE BROTH IS THE MEDICINE — SEASON AT THE TABLE, NOT IN THE POT. A samgyetang broth is barely seasoned: a little salt and pepper to taste at the table, because the chicken, rice and ginseng carry the whole flavour. The ritual is to eat the chicken (falling off the bone, lifting clean with chopsticks), then the rice that has cooked inside it into a gentle porridge, then drink the milky broth like a warm tonic.\n\nSERVE IN THE POT OR A LARGE BOWL, WHOLE, WITH THE SALT AND PEPPER BESIDE IT. This is a one-chicken-per-person dish at its most traditional; one large bird shared between two is the honest practical version, with rice on the side.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Simmer 1 h 15 min","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 42g · Carbs 30g · Fat 26g · Sodium ~600mg","storage":"Best the day it is made — the chicken is at its falling-apart peak. Keeps 2 days in the fridge; the broth sets to a natural jelly (a sign of a good long simmer) and re-liquefies on gentle reheating. The stuffed rice swallows the broth and becomes a porridge — reheat gently and add a splash of water.","chefNotes":"Three rules: soak the rice, never overstuff (it doubles), and SKIM, then simmer gently until the broth turns milky white — that milky turn is the collagen, which is the whole point. Season only at the table: the ginseng, chicken and rice are the flavour.","pairsWith":"A side of kimchi (the classic counter to the warm, gentle soup) · Salt & pepper at the table · Cold barley tea","goesWith":["Gimbap","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Samgyetang is Korea's most famous seasonal health food — eaten specifically on the sam-bok days, the three hottest days of the lunar summer calendar, when Koreans flock to 'samgyetang restaurants' that open early and sell out by lunch. The logic is traditional Korean medicine: you fight summer heat with warming food that makes you sweat, expelling the heat from within. The ginseng root in the cavity is the active ingredient — Korea's great medicinal herb, prized for restoring energy — and the milky broth is the proof of hours of patient simmering. In full tradition, one whole chicken IS one serving, and the biggest samgyetang chains serve hundreds of birds a day during the three hot days.","howThisFeels":"The whole chicken arriving in its milky broth, so tender the meat lifts off the bones, with the ginseng-earthy steam rising — the warm, grounding feeling of being fed like medicine, bite after bite.","leftovers":["The next-morning move is the best part in disguise: the broth and the rice-porridge filling reheat into a gentle congee — add a splash of water and a little sesame oil.","Shred any leftover chicken into the reheated broth with the remaining porridge for a chicken-juk (congee) breakfast.","Freeze the broth alone; it is a gold-standard stock for any Korean soup."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Whole-Chicken Samgyetang","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":52,"howThisFeels":"The full ritual — one whole stuffed chicken, milky ginseng broth, salt and pepper at the table.","trivia":"The classic is one whole small chicken per person — the traditional sam-bok serving. The glutinous rice cooks inside into a porridge, and the ginseng root is the dash of medicine at the heart of it.","delta":{},"ingredients":"1 small whole chicken (600-800g) · 60g glutinous rice, soaked · 1 ginseng root · 4 garlic cloves · 3 jujube (dates) · 2 slices ginger · 2 spring onions · 1L water · salt & pepper","method":["Soak the rice 30 minutes; it doubles as it cooks.","Stuff the cavity two-thirds full: rice, ginseng, garlic, jujube, ginger. Truss closed.","Cover with cold water, bring to a boil, skim thoroughly.","Simmer at a bare tremble 1 h 15 min, turning once, until the broth turns milky white.","Season with salt and pepper at the table only; eat the chicken, the porridge, then the broth."]},{"name":"Budget — Samgyetang for Two from One Bird","icon":"💰","costPP":36,"howThisFeels":"One big bird, two bowls, the same milky ginseng broth — the practical family version of a dish that is traditionally one-chicken-each.","trivia":"Feeding two from one larger bird is the honest home version — the broth and the stuffed-rice porridge stretch perfectly, and a pot of rice on the side closes the gap.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use one larger chicken (about 1.2kg) for two servings. Same stuffing, same gentle simmer, but add an extra 20-30 minutes for the bigger bird. Serve the shredded chicken and porridge into two bowls over steamed rice, and pour the milky broth over both — the broth is easy to share, and the pot of rice makes it a full meal for two."}]}},{"name":"Quick — Samgyetang in the Pressure Cooker","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"The same milky restorative in 40 minutes — pressure cooking does in minutes what the stove does in an hour, and the broth is honestly just as milky.","trivia":"A pressure cooker genuinely works for samgyetang: the chicken and rice cook together in about 35-40 minutes, and the high pressure emulsifies the collagen just as well as a long open simmer. It is the modern Korean home shortcut for a school-night tonic.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Stuff and truss the chicken exactly as the classic, then pressure-cook on high for 30-35 minutes with the water and aromatics, following the cooker's natural release. The broth comes out milky and the chicken fall-apart — add the spring onion and a minute on the sauté setting to finish, then serve exactly as the classic with salt and pepper at the table."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-galbi-jjim","name":"Galbi-jjim","nameAlt":"Korean braised short ribs — the party centrepiece that tastes like a hug","aliases":["galbi jjim","braised short ribs","korean braised beef ribs","kalbi jjim"],"course":"main","type":["main","beef","braise","celebration","party","comfort"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["celebration","party","family","special","new-year"],"mood":"Celebratory & comforting","time":"Prep 25 min · Braise 1 h 45 min","health":"A special-occasion rich dish — the sauce is sweet and the ribs rich, but the long braise renders much of the fat away. This is the celebration plate, not the daily one.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce) · sesame (sesame oil, sesame seeds) · pear (the tenderiser)","budget":"★☆☆ — short ribs are the special-tier cut; everything else is pantry","audience":"The celebration centrepiece — served at holidays, birthdays and the big family Sunday table, where it sits next to the galbi on the grill as the slow answer to the fast.","season":"Any special gathering; the holidays and cold-season Sunday tables are its natural home","ingredients":"400g beef short ribs (English or flanken cut — ask your butcher) · 40ml soy sauce · 20ml pear or apple juice (the quiet tenderiser) · 20ml mirin or Korean cooking wine · 15ml honey or brown sugar · 10ml sesame oil · 1 tbsp minced garlic · 1 tsp grated ginger · 1 potato (about 120g), cubed · 1 carrot, in thick chunks · 5 shiitake mushrooms (optional) · 6 chestnuts (optional, traditional) · 100ml water or stock · black pepper · spring onion & sesame seeds for the finish","method":"⚖️ GALBI-JJIM IS THE SLOW SIBLING OF GALBI — where galbi is the fast, caramelised grill, galbi-jjim is the long, glossy braise, and together they are the twin pillars of a Korean celebration table. 'Jjim' means braised or steamed, and this is the dish served when the table must impress: short ribs cooked until they are falling-apart tender in a sweet-salty soy glaze, with potato and carrot soaking it all up. THE TWO JOBS ARE THE SOAK AND THE BRAISE.\n\n⚖️ FIRST, THE SOAK — THE QUIET STEP THAT MAKES IT GREAT. Put the ribs in a bowl of cold water for 30 minutes to draw out the blood (this is the traditional Korean step that keeps the finished glaze clean and bright instead of dark and murky), then drain. It takes no effort and changes everything.\n\nBLANCH THE RIBS, THEN THE SAUCE ALL AT ONCE. Bring a pot of water to a boil, add the ribs for 5 minutes, and skim the scum that rises — drain and rinse the ribs clean. Whisk the soy, pear juice, mirin, honey, sesame oil, garlic and ginger into a glossy sauce. The pear does what it always does: tenderises gently and gives the glaze its deep caramel sweetness.\n\n⚖️ THE BRAISE — LOW, SLOW, AND THE REDUCTION IS THE SAUCE. Put the ribs in a pot, pour the sauce and the water (or stock) over, and bring to a gentle bubble. Then simmer LOW, covered, for about 1 hour 15 minutes — the ribs are nearly done when the meat is beginning to pull away from the bone. ADD THE POTATO, CARROT, MUSHROOMS AND CHESTNUTS, and simmer 20-25 minutes more, until the vegetables are tender and the ribs are truly falling-apart.\n\n⚖️ THE FINISH — REDUCE THE SAUCE TO A GLAZE. Lift the ribs and vegetables out, then boil the remaining liquid hard for 5-8 minutes until it thickens into a glossy, lacquer-like sauce — this reduction is the entire difference between a braise and a glaze. Pour it over the ribs, scatter the spring onion and sesame seeds, and serve with steamed rice.\n\nTHE RIBS SHOULD BE SO TENDER THAT A CHOPSTICK SLICES THEM. That is the sole test of a properly done galbi-jjim.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Braise 1 h 45 min","kcal":"~580 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 32g · Carbs 38g · Fat 32g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Galbi-jjim is famously BETTER on day two — the ribs drink the glaze and the sauce deepens. Keeps 4 days in the fridge, reheated gently. Freezes beautifully for a month, in its sauce. The potatoes soften further, so add fresh ones when reheating a frozen portion.","chefNotes":"Three rules: the cold-water SOAK draws the blood that keeps the glaze bright, the braise must be LOW and patient (chopstick-through is the test), and the final REDUCTION is what turns it from a braise into a glossy centrepiece. Do not skip the reduction — it is the whole shine.","pairsWith":"Steamed rice (non-negotiable) · Kimchi · A fresh cucumber side · Cold soju or beer","goesWith":["Galbi","Samgyeopsal","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Galbi-jjim is the slow-cooked sibling in the Korean barbecue family: where galbi is grilled fast over coals, jjim braises low and long, and Korean households typically serve both at a celebration table — the fast and the slow, the charred and the melt. It is a staple of the Korean holiday table, and in the southern tradition chestnuts are the signature addition. The name is the method: jjim means steamed or braised, and the dish is proof of how patient Korean cooking can be when it wants a bone to become silk.","howThisFeels":"A glossy lacquered pile of ribs that surrender at the touch of chopsticks, the sweet-salty glaze clinging, the potato soaked in everything — the dish that makes the whole table go quiet mid-bite and then start planning seconds aloud.","leftovers":["The day-two move: the glazed ribs over steaming rice with a fried egg — the humble bibimbap of a celebration dish.","Use the leftover glaze as a stir-fry sauce — a spoon with any quick vegetables turns them Korean in a minute.","Freeze the ribs in their sauce for a month; reheat gently and add fresh potato and carrot."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Galbi-jjim with Potato & Chestnut","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":56,"howThisFeels":"The full celebration braise — falling-apart ribs, glossy soy-pear glaze, potato, carrot, mushroom and chestnut.","trivia":"The classic is the holiday version — short ribs braised with chestnuts (the southern tradition), potato, carrot and shiitake, finished with a glossy reduction.","delta":{},"ingredients":"400g short ribs · 40ml soy · 20ml pear juice · 20ml mirin · 15ml honey · 10ml sesame oil · 1 tbsp garlic · 1 tsp ginger · 120g potato · 1 carrot · 5 shiitake · 6 chestnuts · 100ml water · pepper · spring onion · sesame seeds","method":["Soak the ribs in cold water 30 minutes; drain. Blanch 5 minutes; skim and rinse.","Whisk soy, pear, mirin, honey, sesame, garlic, ginger. Put ribs, sauce, water in a pot; simmer LOW, covered, 1 h 15 min.","Add potato, carrot, shiitake, chestnuts; simmer 20-25 min until everything is tender and ribs fall apart.","Lift out the solids; boil the liquid hard 5-8 min until it reduces to a glossy glaze.","Pour the glaze over; finish with spring onion and sesame. Serve with rice."]},{"name":"Chicken Galbi-Jjim (Budget)","icon":"🐔","costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"The same glossy braise with chicken drumsticks — a quarter of the cost, all of the comfort.","trivia":"Chicken legs take the same glaze beautifully and cook in half the time — a legitimate budget fork that Korean families love.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"400g beef short ribs (English or flanken cut — ask your butcher)","to":"400g chicken drumsticks or thighs"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the 30-minute soak (chicken has no blood to draw). Braise the chicken in the same sauce at a gentle bubble for 35-40 minutes until fall-apart, then add the vegetables for the last 15 minutes. Reduce the sauce exactly as the classic — the glaze clings to chicken just as lovingly."}]}},{"name":"Quick — Pressure-Cooker Galbi-Jjim","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":54,"howThisFeels":"The same glossy lacquer in 45 minutes — the pressure cooker does the braise in a quarter of the time, and the reduction still delivers the shine.","trivia":"A pressure cooker transforms galbi-jjim: the ribs reach falling-apart in about 30 minutes. The final reduction is the step that must not be skipped — it is what takes the dish from stew to celebration.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Soak and blanch as the classic, then pressure-cook the ribs with the sauce and water on high for 25-30 minutes, natural release. Add the vegetables and cook a further 5 minutes. Then lift everything out and reduce the liquid hard on the stovetop to the glossy glaze — the reduction is non-negotiable, even in the fast lane."}]}},{"name":"Mushroom & Daikon Galbi-Jjim (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The glossy braise, given over to king oyster mushrooms and daikon — and the daikon does its famous trick of tasting like the sauce itself.","trivia":"Daikon radish braised in soy-sweet sauce is a beloved Korean banchan-adjacent classic — it drinks the glaze and becomes startlingly savoury. King oyster mushrooms, thick-sliced, give the closest thing to a meaty bite.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"400g beef short ribs (English or flanken cut — ask your butcher)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"250g king oyster mushrooms, thick-sliced"},{"item":"200g daikon radish, in thick chunks"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the soak and blanch. Simmer the daikon in the sauce and water for 20 minutes first (it needs the head start), then add the king oyster mushrooms, potato and carrot, and simmer 20 minutes. Reduce the glaze exactly as the classic. The mushrooms and daikon drink the soy-pear glaze and carry the celebration plate with honour."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-kongguksu","name":"Kongguksu","nameAlt":"Cold soybean noodle soup — summer's cooling bowl of pureed beans","aliases":["kongguksu","cold soybean soup","soybean noodle soup","korean soybean noodles"],"course":"main","type":["main","noodles","cold","summer","vegan","light"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["summer","everyday","lunch","refresh"],"mood":"Cooling & refreshing","time":"Prep 15 min (+soak overnight) · Cook 25 min","health":"One of the healthiest bowls in Korean cooking — pureed soybeans are a protein-and-fibre powerhouse, and the dish is naturally vegan.","allergen":"Soy (the soybeans) · wheat (the noodles)","budget":"★★★ — dried soybeans are cheap; this is the most affordable summer peak in Korean cooking","audience":"The summer-cooling bowl — the dairy-free, protein-dense answer to a milkshake, served as a meal.","season":"SUMMER ONLY, and proudly so — kongguksu is eaten when the thermometer gives up, and its fans mourn it in winter","ingredients":"100g dried soybeans (about 1 generous handful) · 120g thin wheat noodles (somyeon / thin spaghetti works) · 5ml salt · 5g sugar (just a touch) · 5ml sesame oil · toasted sesame seeds · 1 slice cucumber, in fine matchsticks · 1 slice tomato (the classic garnish) · a few ice cubes · salt for the table","method":"⚖️ KONGGUKSU IS SUMMER IN A BOWL — cold, milky, quietly sweet: pureed soybeans as the soup, thin wheat noodles swimming through it, and a bright cucumber-and-tomato garnish. It is Korea's dairy-free, protein-dense answer to a cold milkshake, and its whole character is one of pure, cooling simplicity. The dish is naturally vegan, one of the few Korean bowls that needs no adaptation.\n\nTHE BEANS — SOAKED OVERNIGHT, THEN TRANSFORMED. This is the day-before part, and it is the whole dish: dried soybeans soak in cold water overnight (at least 8 hours), then are drained, boiled in fresh water 5 minutes, drained, and the skins slipped off by hand or by rubbing them in water — removing the skins is what makes the soup SILKY instead of gritty. This is the classic home method; the honest shortcut is to use 300ml of good unsweetened soya milk plus a handful of cooked beans, blended to boost the milky body.\n\n⚖️ THE BLEND — SLOW, LOTS OF COLD WATER, AND THE STRAIN. Blend the skinned beans with cold water (about 2 cups for the batch), starting slow and working up, until it is completely smooth. Then strain it through a fine sieve or cheesecloth, pressing the pulp hard to extract every drop — then squeeze the pulp once more. The result is a milky, nutty, gently sweet liquid that looks like dairy and tastes like beans in the best possible way. Season it with salt and just a touch of sugar, and chill it thoroughly — ice-cold is not optional.\n\nTHE NOODLES — COOKED, THEN SHOCKED. Cook the thin noodles per the packet, then the indispensably Korean step: rinse them under cold running water, rub them gently, drain, and give them a final swish in iced water. Cold, slippery, separate strands — a warm clump would sink the whole bowl. Drain very well.\n\n⚖️ BUILD THE BOWL — NOODLES BELOW, MILKY SOUP OVER, GARNISH BRIGHT. Coil the cold noodles in the bowl, pour the icy soybean soup over and around, and crown with cucumber matchsticks, a slice of tomato, sesame seeds and a thread of sesame oil. Add an ice cube or two if the day demands it. Serve with kimchi on the side — the sharp fermented spice cutting against the milky sweetness is the pairing the dish was born for.\n\nEAT IT FAST, like the other cold-noodle dishes of Korean summer. Season at the table — every Korean table keeps salt beside the kongguksu, because the soup's seasoning is meant to be tuned by the eater.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min (+soak overnight) · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~450 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 26g · Carbs 62g · Fat 12g · Sodium ~500mg","storage":"The soybean soup itself keeps 2 days refrigerated in a jar — shake before use. The noodles must be freshly cooked and iced; they clump and dull overnight. The dish is a same-day bowl, best eaten at its icy peak.","chefNotes":"The three rules: SOAK the beans overnight and remove the skins (silky, not gritty), STRAIN the blend through a sieve pressing the pulp (the milk is in the squeeze), and RINSE the noodles ice-cold so each strand stays separate. Season at the table — salt is the eater's, not the cook's.","pairsWith":"Kimchi (the essential companion — sharp fire against the milky sweet) · A cold, crisp cucumber side · Chilled barley tea","goesWith":["Naengmyeon","Gimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Kongguksu is one of Korean summer's most personal foods: the milky puree of soybeans is so plain-looking that newcomers often underestimate it, and so beloved that Koreans argue about the details — whole-skin vs skinned, strained vs coarse, sesame oil or not. It hails from the northern tradition, where the soybean was the everyday protein, and it remains the dish that proves a bowl can be almost nothing — cold beans, cold noodles, salt — and still be one of the great refreshments of the Korean table.","howThisFeels":"The first shock of milky-cold soybean soup against warm summer air, the slippery tangle of noodles, the bright crunch of cucumber — and the quiet satisfaction of a bowl that is both a meal and a milkshake.","leftovers":["The soybean liquid keeps 2 days cold — shake well and it is ready for a fresh bowl of noodles the next day.","Stir the leftover soy milk through the next morning's porridge (or add to a bowl of museli) for a Korean-strength start.","The squeezed bean pulp is not waste: toast it dry in a pan with a little sesame and it becomes a crunchy, protein-dense garnish (kong-gom)."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Skinned-Bean Kongguksu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"The full ritual — beans soaked overnight, skinned by hand, blended slow and strained silky, over iced noodles.","trivia":"The classic is the home method: dried soybeans soaked overnight, skins slipped off by hand, blended with cold water and strained through a sieve until silky. The skin-removal is the difference between milky and gritty.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g dried soybeans · 120g thin wheat noodles · 5ml salt · 5g sugar · 5ml sesame oil · sesame seeds · cucumber · tomato · ice cubes","method":["Soak the soybeans in cold water overnight (8+ hours).","Boil 5 minutes, drain, slip off the skins by rubbing in water.","Blend with cold water until completely smooth; strain through a sieve, pressing the pulp for every drop.","Season with salt and a touch of sugar; chill thoroughly until icy.","Cook the noodles, rinse under cold water, swish in iced water, drain well.","Coil noodles in a bowl, pour the icy bean soup over, garnish with cucumber, tomato, sesame. Serve with kimchi."]},{"name":"Quick — Soy-Milk Kongguksu (15 Minutes)","icon":"⚡","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"The honest weeknight shortcut — good unsweetened soya milk boosted with blended cooked beans, no overnight soak and no skin-slipping.","trivia":"A quality unsweetened soya milk plus a handful of cooked (tinned) soybeans, blended together, is a legitimate shortcut — the tinned beans give the milky body and the soya milk gives the base. The noodles and the ice-cold ritual carry the rest.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Blend 300ml unsweetened soya milk with a drained handful of tinned soybeans (about 60g) and a pinch of salt until completely smooth — taste and adjust with more milk for silkier. Stick it in the freezer while the noodles cook, then chill the noodles in iced water and build the bowl exactly as the classic. Fifteen minutes, all of the refreshment."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-budae-jjigae","name":"Budae Jjigae","nameAlt":"Army stew — the extraordinary Korean War fusion born of scarcity","aliases":["budae jjigae","army stew","korean army stew","부대찌개"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","fusion","kimchi","one-pot","comfort","sharing"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","comfort","winter","sharing","leftover"],"mood":"Bold, fun & communal","time":"Prep 15 min · Cook 20 min","health":"Not health food — this is a fun, indulgent fusion pot built on processed meats, noodles and cheese. It is a comfort blowout, eaten socially.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce, gochujang) · gluten (noodles, some sausages) · dairy (the cheese) · sesame","budget":"★★★ — tinned meats are among the cheapest proteins around; this stew is built to feed a crowd for almost nothing","audience":"The communal feast — a bubbling pot in the middle of the table that everyone dips into, built for sharing, gaming nights and cold evenings.","season":"Winter pot-meals and late-night gatherings — but its fans will fire it up any night the craving strikes","ingredients":"100g old kimchi, chopped · 50g SPAM or corned meat, sliced (classic) · 2 hot dog sausages, sliced (classic) · 30g baked beans (the classic American-ration nod) · 100g firm tofu, cubed · 1 packet instant noodles (ramyun noodles — the classic finish) · 15g gochugaru · 10ml gochujang · 1 tsp minced garlic · 10ml soy sauce · 300ml water or chicken stock · 1 slice processed cheese (American-style — the final flourish) · 2 spring onions · black pepper","method":"⚖️ BUDAE JJIGAE IS THE EXTRAORDINARY FUSION THAT SCARCITY BUILT — a bubbling Korean pot of kimchi, SPAM, hot dogs, baked beans, instant noodles and melted cheese, born after the Korean War when Koreans living near US army bases combined the leftover American ration tins with the Korean ingredients they had always used. The result, named 'army stew' (budae jjigae), became one of Korea's most beloved comfort foods and a genuine cultural artefact. It is not haute cuisine — it is the glorious opposite: a communal pot that is unapologetically tinned, processed and joyful.\n\n⚖️ FIRST, THE KOREAN FUNDAMENT: SEAR THE KIMCHI. Everything that is good about this stew starts exactly like kimchi jjigae: chop the old kimchi, and fry it hard in a little oil until its edges darken and the whole kitchen smells of fermented chilli. This is the step that gives the pot its deep, tangy, savoury base.\n\nBUILD THE POT, LAYER BY LAYER. Spread the seared kimchi across the bottom of a wide pot. Arrange the sliced SPAM, hot dogs, tofu, baked beans and any vegetables in neat piles around the edge — the laid-out look is part of the dish's charm, a mosaic of rations and Korean pantry that bubbles together as it cooks.\n\n⚖️ THE SAUCE IS A SPICY MIXTURE, ACTIVE IN THE CENTRE. Into the middle goes the sauce: gochugaru, gochujang, garlic, soy and a splash of the stock, whisked into a red paste. Pour the rest of the stock (or water) around, so the vegetables still peek above the liquid. Bring the whole pot to a furious bubble.\n\nNOW THE ANTHEMIC FINISH — THE NOODLES AND THE CHEESE. When the pot is bubbling hard, lay the instant noodle block in the centre, submerging it gently into the broth, and place the slice of processed cheese on top. As the noodles soften and the cheese melts into a molten island of orange, the pot is ready. Turn the heat off just before the cheese fully vanishes — the half-melted cheese pooling on the noodles is the money shot.\n\n⚖️ SERVE FROM THE POT, EVERYONE DIPS IN. Budae jjigae is eaten communally — everyone reaches in with their chopsticks, fishing out noodles and SPAM and tofu until the broth is nearly gone. The last move, the classic one, is to break some rice into the remaining spicy broth and eat it as a little congee — nothing is wasted.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 20 min","kcal":"~560 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 28g · Carbs 54g · Fat 26g · Sodium ~1600mg","storage":"The broth is gold — keep it a day and reheat with fresh noodles and cheese for an equally glorious round two. The stew itself does not freeze well (the noodles and cheese collapse); the seared-kimchi-and-sauce base freezes for a month.","chefNotes":"Two rules: SEAR the kimchi first (the whole base is in that browning), and WAIT for the noodles and cheese until the pot is already boiling hard — they are the finish, not the beginning. Lay everything out in piles before the liquid goes in; the mosaic is part of the charm.","pairsWith":"Cold beer (the definitive companion) · Steamed rice to finish the broth · Kimchi on the side, always","goesWith":["Kimchi Jjigae","Tteokbokki","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Budae jjigae's story is the reason it is extraordinary: after the Korean War, Koreans in Uijeongbu and near US bases found themselves with American military ration cans — SPAM, hot dogs, baked beans, processed cheese — an irony of hunger turned into invention. Korean cooks married those tins to the pantry they had always used: kimchi, gochugaru, gochujang, garlic, instant noodles. The fusion was so beloved it became a national comfort food, and today 'army stew' is served everywhere from two-dollar street joints to high-end reinterpretations — a dish that turns the memory of scarcity into a feast.","howThisFeels":"The whole pot arriving bubbling, the molten cheese island melting over the noodles, everyone reaching in at once — salty, spicy, tinned, joyful, and somehow deeply Korean.","leftovers":["The broth is the treasure: reheat it and add a fresh noodle block and cheese for round two.","Break cooked rice into the leftover broth and simmer 2 minutes for a spicy congee finish.","Freeze the seared-kimchi-and-sauce base for a month; a fresh pot is then 10 minutes away."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Army Stew (SPAM & Hot Dogs)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The original fusion — SPAM, hot dogs, baked beans, kimchi, instant noodles and a molten cheese island.","trivia":"The classic is the post-war original: SPAM and hot dogs layered with kimchi and gochugaru, finished with instant noodles and one slice of processed cheese — the exact configuration that won the nation.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g old kimchi · 50g SPAM, sliced · 2 hot dogs, sliced · 30g baked beans · 100g tofu, cubed · 1 packet instant noodles · 15g gochugaru · 10ml gochujang · 1 tsp garlic · 10ml soy · 300ml stock · 1 slice processed cheese · 2 spring onions · pepper","method":["Sear the chopped old kimchi hard until its edges darken.","Arrange SPAM, hot dogs, tofu and baked beans in piles around the seared kimchi in a wide pot.","Whisk gochugaru, gochujang, garlic, soy and a splash of stock into a sauce in the centre; pour the rest of the stock around.","Bring to a furious bubble; lay the instant noodle block in and the cheese slice on top.","Eat communally from the pot as the cheese melts; finish with rice broken into the remaining broth."]},{"name":"Budget — Beans-Forward Army Stew (Vegan-friendly)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"The same bubbly fusion pot with the tinned meat scaled right back — more beans, more mushrooms, more tofu, all the flavour.","trivia":"The tinned meat is the flavour-carrying luxury, not the essence — a budget pot with extra baked beans, mushrooms and tofu keeps the whole spirit of the dish, and removing the SPAM and hot dogs entirely makes it genuinely vegan (gochujang and kimchi are naturally plant-based).","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Double the baked beans, add a handful of sliced mushrooms and an extra block of tofu, and halve (or drop entirely, for vegan) the SPAM and hot dogs. The seared kimchi and the spicy sauce do all the work — the bright, bubbling, communal soul of the pot survives completely. Use vegetable stock for the vegan version."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 10-Minute Single-Pot Budae","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"The solo or couple's version — one small pot, everything tossed in together, noodles and cheese on top the instant it bubbles.","trivia":"The quick version simply skips the neat mosaic layering: everything meets in one small pot, the sauce whisked right in, and the noodle-and-cheese finish is unchanged. The seared kimchi still leads — that step is the non-negotiable.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"For one small practical pot, sear the kimchi in a deep saucepan, toss in the tinned and cubed ingredients and the whisked sauce, pour the stock over, and bring to a boil. The instant it bubbles hard, drop in the noodle block and cheese. Single-pot, 10 minutes, the same molten finish."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-ojingeo-bokkeum","name":"Ojingeo-bokkeum","nameAlt":"Spicy stir-fried squid — the diamond-scored Korean pan favourite","aliases":["ojingeo bokkeum","spicy squid stir fry","korean spicy squid","ojingeo"],"course":"main","type":["main","seafood","squid","stir-fry","spicy","quick"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","weeknight","quick","drinking-food"],"mood":"Fiery & fast","time":"Prep 15 min · Cook 6 min","health":"Lean seafood protein in a spicy-sweet glaze — one of the lighter mains on the Korean pan table, as long as the rice bowl doesn't run away with you.","allergen":"Seafood (squid) · soy (soy sauce, gochujang) · sesame (sesame oil, sesame seeds)","budget":"★★☆ — squid is mid-price; the sauce is pantry staples and the vegetable add-ins stretch it","audience":"The fast weeknight pan — this is the 20-minute Korean dinner that outranks takeaway, and a favourite anju (drinking snack) with beer.","season":"Year-round and fast — but its bracket is the quick hungry night, not any particular season","ingredients":"200g squid (tubes and tentacles), cleaned \u00b7 20g gochujang \u00b7 10g gochugaru (or mild chilli flakes + a pinch extra) \u00b7 10ml soy sauce \u00b7 10ml honey or sugar \u00b7 5ml sesame oil \u00b7 1 tbsp minced garlic \u00b7 1 tsp grated ginger \u00b7 30g onion, sliced \u00b7 30g carrot, in matchsticks \u00b7 2 spring onions, in 4cm lengths \u00b7 toasted sesame seeds \u00b7 oil for the pan","method":"⚖️ SQUID COOKS IN 90 SECONDS, OR IT COOKS FOR A LIFETIME. That is the one law of ojingeo-bokkeum, and the only real technique in the dish. Squid turns from tender to rubber the moment it is overcooked, so the whole method is built around salting the pan, scoring the tubes, and getting everything in and out FAST — the sauce does the flavour, the fire does the texture.\n\n⚖️ THE SCORE THAT MAKES IT CURL — THE DIAMOND CROSSHATCH. Cut the squid tubes open flat and score the inner side in a fine crosshatch — diagonal lines one way, diagonal lines the other, about 5mm apart, cutting only halfway through the flesh. When the scored pieces hit the hot pan they curl into those beautiful little pine-cone shapes with diamond pattern showing — the signature look of the dish, and the scoring is also what lets the sauce cling. Cut the scored tubes into 4-5cm pieces, and split the tentacles into pairs.\n\nTHE SAUCE IS ONE BOWL, WAITING. Whisk the gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, sesame oil, garlic and ginger into a sticky red paste. Have it ready beside the stove — once the pan goes on, there is no time to be measuring.\n\n⚖️ THE PAN — SEARING HOT, AND THE ORDER IS EVERYTHING. Heat the pan hard with a little oil. First the onion and carrot, stir-fried 1 minute until just softening; then the scored squid, and the moment it hits the pan it begins to curl. Sear it hard for 60-90 seconds, tossing constantly — it is the briefest, hottest cook of the dish, and nothing else touches the pan until the squid is nearly curled and opaque.\n\n⚖️ THE SAUCE GOES IN LAST, AND RIGHT AFTER IT, OFF THE HEAT. Spoon the sauce over, toss once to glaze everything in a single coat, add the spring onion, and take the pan straight off the heat — the residual heat finishes the glaze without cooking the squid a second too long. First bite of a properly done ojingeo-bokkeum: tender, sweet-spicy, slightly bouncy, and absolutely not rubber. That is the whole achievement.\n\nFINISH with a shower of toasted sesame seeds and eat immediately with rice. If it ever does turn rubbery, the salvage is the classic one: chop it small and stir it through the fried rice — the texture disappears and only the glorious red glaze remains.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 6 min","kcal":"~340 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 30g · Carbs 22g · Fat 10g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Best the second it leaves the pan — squid waits for no one. Leftovers keep 1 day in the fridge, reheated in a flash over high heat, but they are a shadow of the fresh bowl; the honest day-two move is the fried rice salvage below.","chefNotes":"The three rules: SCORE the tubes in a diamond crosshatch so they curl and catch the glaze, cook the squid 60-90 seconds over searing heat (or it turns to rubber), and put the sauce in LAST then off the heat — the glaze must come from residual heat, not more cooking.","pairsWith":"Steamed rice (non-negotiable) · Cold beer (the anju pairing) · Kimchi · A light soup to follow","goesWith":["Kimchi Fried Rice","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Ojingeo-bokkeum means simply 'stir-fried squid', but in Korea it is more than one dish — it is the standard by which home cooks prove their speed. The diamond crosshatch scored into the tubes is the dish's fingerprint: it makes the squid curl dramatically, catches the red glaze, and is one of the first knife skills Korean home cooks teach children. As anju (bar food to eat with alcohol), it is a beloved pairing with beer and soju. Squid is eaten so commonly in Korea that it appears on the coast nearly every way a squid can be cooked — and this spicy stir-fry is the most iconic pan version of them all.","howThisFeels":"The cuttlefish curls tightening in the smoking-hot pan, the red glaze spooned on at the last second — and the first chewy-sweet-spicy bite mopped through rice, the kind of fast weeknight dish that makes takeaway feel like a mistake.","leftovers":["The classic salvage: chop any leftover squid small and stir it through day-old rice with the extra glaze for spicy squid fried rice — the texture disappears and the red shines.","Slice leftover ojingeo over cold noodles for a spicy Korean squid salad lunch.","A spoon of the leftover glaze alone glazes any quick stir-fry of vegetables the next night."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Ojingeo-bokkeum","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":36,"howThisFeels":"The genuine article — diamond-scored squid, 90-second sear, one-coat red glaze, sesame finish.","trivia":"The classic is scored-tube squid with the gochujang glaze — the 90-second rule is the whole craft.","delta":{},"ingredients":"200g squid, cleaned · 20g gochujang · 10g gochugaru · 10ml soy · 10ml honey · 5ml sesame oil · 1 tbsp garlic · 1 tsp ginger · 30g onion · 30g carrot · 2 spring onions · sesame seeds · oil","method":["Score the opened tubes in a 5mm diamond crosshatch, cut into 4-5cm pieces; split the tentacles.","Whisk the gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, sesame, garlic and ginger into a paste — ready beside the stove.","Stir-fry onion and carrot 1 minute in a searing pan.","Add the scored squid; sear hard 60-90 seconds, tossing, until it curls and turns opaque.","Glaze with the sauce, toss once, add spring onion, OFF the heat immediately.","Finish with sesame seeds; serve with rice."]},{"name":"Budget — Squid & Prawn Mix","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"Half squid, half prawns, all the red glaze — a smaller squid outlay stretched with cheaper shrimp, and honestly a more colourful plate.","trivia":"Mixing prawns into ojingeo-bokkeum is a common home economy: prawns cook to tenderness on the same timescale, catch the same glaze, and make the pan look generous for less.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use 100g squid and 100g peeled prawns in place of the full 200g squid. Add the prawns to the pan for the final 30 seconds of the squid's 90-second sear — they turn pink and curl at the same pace. Everything else identical, and the mixed bite of squid-meets-prawn in one red glaze is its own reward."}]}},{"name":"Quick — Frozen Squid Rings","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The honest weeknight shortcut — frozen rings skip the scoring, cook in the same fast fire, and take the same glaze.","trivia":"Frozen squid rings are a legitimate ready ingredient — you lose the curled-pinecone look, but the 90-second rule and the glaze do all the flavour work.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Swap the scored tubes for 200g frozen squid rings, thawed and patted dry. No scoring needed — the rings are already the right size. They need about the same 90 seconds in the searing pan, then the same last-second glaze and off the heat. A genuinely 15-minute dinner."}]}},{"name":"Tofu & Mushroom Bokkeum (Veg)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"The same red-glazed pan celebration, given to golden tofu and mushrooms — the glaze is the star, and it shines on anything.","trivia":"The gochujang glaze is naturally vegan, and pan-crisped tofu takes it beautifully — this is the honest plant-based answer that keeps a 90-second pan energy.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"200g squid (tubes and tentacles), cleaned"}],"addIng":[{"item":"200g firm tofu, pressed and cubed"},{"item":"100g mushrooms (shiitake or button), thick-sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Press the tofu, cube it, and sear the cubes hard in the hot oil until deeply golden on each side, then brown the mushrooms alongside for 2-3 minutes until they release their water and take colour. Into the same pan goes the same onion, carrot and spring onion, then the same glaze tossed at the last second and off the heat. The red, sweet-spicy finish is identical — the glaze makes no demands about what it coats."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-dakgalbi","name":"Dakgalbi","nameAlt":"Spicy stir-fried chicken & vegetables — the Chuncheon table-top classic","aliases":["dakgalbi","dak galbi","spicy stir fried chicken","chuncheon dakgalbi"],"course":"main","type":["main","chicken","stir-fry","spicy","table-top","onepot"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","everyday","family","make-your-own"],"mood":"Bold & communal","time":"Prep 20 min · Cook 15 min","health":"A moreish white-meat stir-fry — the gochujang glaze and the cabbage-to-chicken ratio make it lighter than its fiery look suggests.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce, gochujang) · sesame (sesame oil) · gluten (soy sauce)","budget":"★★★ — chicken thigh and cabbage are cheap; this is one of Korea's great crowd-feeders for almost nothing","audience":"The communal pan dish — cooked at the table and eaten straight from the pan with everyone helping themselves; perfect for a hungry group.","season":"A year-round table pan, at its best in the cold months when the bubbling pan and the crowd around it are the whole point","ingredients":"250g chicken thigh, in bite-size pieces \u00b7 100g cabbage, in rough squares \u00b7 60g sweet potato (or potato), in thin half-moons \u00b7 50g tteokbokki rice cakes (optional, the classic add) \u00b7 1 onion, sliced \u00b7 15g gochujang \u00b7 10g gochugaru \u00b7 10ml soy sauce \u00b7 10ml honey or sugar \u00b7 1 tbsp minced garlic \u00b7 5ml sesame oil \u00b7 2 spring onions \u00b7 toasted sesame seeds \u00b7 optional for the finish: 80g cooked rice, a sheet of nori, a little extra sesame oil (for the famous bap)","method":"⚖️ DAKGALBI IS THE CHUNCHEON TABLE-TOP CLASSIC — a wide pan of gochujang-glazed chicken, cabbage, sweet potato and rice cakes, set bubbling in the middle of the table, cooked and stirred live while everyone eats straight from the pan. Like samgyeopsal, half the dish is the format: the pan arrives half-cooked and finishes in front of you, and every fork is a coalition. THIS IS THE SPICY CLOSE COUSIN OF SAMGYEOPSAL'S LETTUCE-WRAP TABLE — and the fried-rice finish is the legend.\n\nTHE SAUCE IS THE KING, AND IT HAS ONE BOWL. Whisk the gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, garlic and a thread of sesame oil into a bright red paste. Chickens takes it eagerly; everything in this pan does — that is dakgalbi.\n\n⚖️ THE ORDER OF THE PAN — CABBAGE GOES FIRST, AND AS A BED. In the wide pan over medium-high, lay the cabbage squares down first, then the chicken on top. The cabbage drinks the released juices and keeps the chicken from sticking scorching to the pan — it is both ingredient and cushion. Add the sweet potato slices and the rice cakes (if using) around the edges, spoon the whole sauce over the mound, and toss to coat everything.\n\n⚖️ COOK AND TOSS THE PAN FOR 10-12 MINUTES — THE CHICKEN MUST COOK THROUGH, THE VEGETABLES MUST SOFTEN. Over a steady medium heat, turn the pan contents every couple of minutes so everything glazes evenly and the chicken cooks all the way. The cabbage collapses, the sweet potato goes tender, the rice cakes turn silky-chewy, and the sauce reduces into a glossy lacquer clinging to every piece. Toss in the spring onion for the last minute.\n\nNOW THE FAMOUS FINISH — THE DAKGALBI BAP. When the pan is nearly empty, push everything to one side, lower the heat, and tip in the cooked rice with a sheet of torn nori and a thread of sesame oil. Fold and fry it for 2-3 minutes in the remaining red sauce until every grain is coated and the bottom crisps — then divide it out. This fried-rice-in-the-sauce finish is so beloved it is the standard way the dish ends, and the last crunchy bits of seasoned rice are fought over.\n\n⚖️ THE CHUNCHEON FLAVOUR: the original dakgalbi is from Chuncheon, eaten with ssam (lettuce wraps) — serve lettuce leaves alongside so everyone can wrap crisp bites of the red chicken, or just eat it straight from the pan with rice.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 15 min","kcal":"~460 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g · Carbs 34g · Fat 18g · Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"The cooked pan keeps 2 days in the fridge and reheats honestly in a hot pan with a splash of water; the rice cakes harden, so add a fresh handful when reheating. The sauce base (before the vegetables) freezes for a month.","chefNotes":"Four rules: LAY the cabbage as a bed (it cushions the chicken and drinks the juices), let the chicken COOK ALL THE WAY in the 10-12 minute toss (never rush it to crispy-pink), let the sauce REDUCE to a lacquer, and NEVER skip the bap — the fried-rice finish is the dish's second act and its most-loved move.","pairsWith":"Lettuce leaves for ssam wraps · Steamed rice (or save it for the bap finish) · Cold beer or soju · Kimchi","goesWith":["Bibimbap","Tteokbokki","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Dakgalbi was born in Chuncheon, the city an hour east of Seoul, where in the 1960s a simple method — marinating chicken thigh in a spicy paste and stir-frying it at the table with cabbage and sweet potato — became a local institution. Chuncheon dakgalbi restaurants sit along 'dakgalbi street', and the dish is so tied to the city that the streets there are paved with its name. The fried-rice finish (bap) — rice fried into the leftover sauce at the end of the meal — is so canon that in Korea it is simply called 'the bap', and the final crunchy layer of seasoned rice is a place of genuine debate: the crisp-coated bottom is the prize.","howThisFeels":"A wide pan of red-glazed chicken and collapsing cabbage bubbling at the table, everyone reaching in at once — then the collective gasp when the rice goes in and the bap begins to crisp in the leftover glory.","leftovers":["The day-two move is the same as the first day: tip cold rice into the leftover pan and fry the bap — the sauce is still all there.","Wrap the leftover chicken in lettuce with rice and kimchi for a quick ssam-style lunch.","Freeze the sauce base (gochujang paste + chicken before the vegetables) for a month — a fresh pan is then 15 minutes away."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Chuncheon Dakgalbi","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The full table-top classic — chicken, cabbage, sweet potato and rice cakes in the red glaze, with the bap finish.","trivia":"The classic is the Chuncheon original — chicken thigh in spicy paste, stir-fried at the table, and the fried rice cooked into the leftover sauce as the canonical ending.","delta":{},"ingredients":"250g chicken thigh · 100g cabbage · 60g sweet potato · 50g rice cakes (optional) · 1 onion · 15g gochujang · 10g gochugaru · 10ml soy · 10ml honey · 1 tbsp garlic · 5ml sesame oil · 2 spring onions · sesame seeds · 80g rice + nori (for the bap)","method":["Whisk gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, garlic and sesame oil into the red paste.","Lay the cabbage as a bed in the pan; add the chicken on top, sweet potato and rice cakes around the edges.","Spoon the sauce over everything and toss to coat.","Stir-fry 10-12 minutes over medium heat, tossing every couple of minutes, until the chicken is cooked through and the sauce reduced to a lacquer.","VERSION BEFORE THE LAST MOVE: for the bap finish, push the pan aside, tip in rice and torn nori with a thread of sesame, and fry until crisped in the sauce. Serve with lettuce for ssam."]},{"name":"Budget — Dakgalbi with More Veg & Noodles","icon":"💰","costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"A more vegetable-forward pan with instant noodles folded in — the sauce stretches to feed a bigger table for less.","trivia":"Adding extra cabbage, carrot and a fistful of instant noodles to the pan is the standard Korean home economy — the noodles drink the glisten and the pan goes further.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Add an extra 100g of cabbage, a sliced carrot, and a square of instant noodles (cooked and drained) tossed in for the last 3 minutes. The noodles pick up the red glaze like they were born for it, and the pan feeds another hungry guest for pennies. The bap finish still works a charm over the extra sauce."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 12-Minute Skillet Dakgalbi","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The midweek version in a normal skillet — no table-top theatre, same red glaze, same bap, just faster.","trivia":"A home skillet does the Chuncheon pan's job perfectly well — the fun of the table-top is the format, not the pan, and the recipe is unchanged at 12 minutes.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Cook exactly as the classic in a wide skillet over medium-high, tossing to cook the chicken through in about 8-10 minutes, then finish with the bap. No table-top show — just a fast, honest weekday bowl with the same red lacquer and the same final crispy rice."}]}},{"name":"Tofu Dakgalbi (Veg)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The same bubbling red pan with pan-crisped tofu standing in for the chicken — the sauce and the bap are unchanged and the table still disappears in 15 minutes.","trivia":"The gochujang glaze is naturally vegan and the bap finish asks nothing of the protein — pan-crisped tofu takes the red exactly as well as chicken, and the pan becomes plant-based without losing a single signature.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"250g chicken thigh, in bite-size pieces"}],"addIng":[{"item":"250g extra-firm tofu, pressed and cubed"},{"item":"extra cabbage and mushrooms for bulk"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Press and cube the tofu, then sear the cubes hard in the pan in a little sesame oil until deeply golden before laying the cabbage bed under everything. Stir-fry as the classic for 8-10 minutes until the tofu is glazed and the vegetables tender, then the bap finish unchanged. The red lacquer and the crispy-rice ending are the dish — the tofu simply wears them well."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-sundae","name":"Sundae","nameAlt":"Korean blood sausage — the street-food tube of barley and noodles","aliases":["sundae","soondae","korean blood sausage","blood sausage korea"],"course":"main","type":["main","sausage","blood-sausage","street-food","offal"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["street-food","drinking-food","everyday","restorative"],"mood":"Earthy & hearty","time":"Prep 30 min · Poach 40 min","health":"Iron-rich and honest — the blood and barley make it as nourishing as sausages get; it is rich, so it is a sometimes-food, eaten as street snack and drinking food.","allergen":"Blood sausage (pork). Served with salt and a sesame sauce — soy in the sauce.","budget":"★★★ — blood sausage is among the cheapest proteins in the Korean market; this feeds generously for almost nothing","audience":"The street-food icon — bought by the coil from vendors and eaten with skewers beside the tteokbokki stall; at home it is a restorative cold-day pot.","season":"Cold days and drinking nights — the warming, salty, iron-rich coil is comfort in street-food form","ingredients":"200g fresh pork blood (from the butcher \u2014 about 1\u00bd cups) \u00b7 50g dangmyeon (sweet potato glass noodles), soaked and cut \u00b7 40g pearled barley, cooked \u00b7 30g glutinous rice (optional, for extra chew) \u00b7 1 small onion, very finely chopped \u00b7 4 cloves garlic, minced \u00b7 5g ginger, grated \u00b7 10ml soy sauce \u00b7 5ml sesame oil \u00b7 1 tsp gochugaru (optional heat) \u00b7 black pepper \u00b7 salt \u00b7 1m sausage casing (natural pork casing from the butcher or Asian grocer \u2014 or see the Quick version) \u00b7 FOR THE TABLE: flaky salt, sesame seeds, and a dipping sauce of soy + vinegar + a pinch of chilli","method":"⚖️ SUNDAE IS KOREA'S BLOOD SAUSAGE — a poetic name for a humble, deeply comforting tube: fresh pork blood mixed with chewy glass noodles, barley, garlic and aromatics, stuffed into a casing and poached until set. It is sold by the coil on every Korean market and street-food row, sliced warm and handed over on a Styrofoam tray with salt and a dipping sauce. The honest global reader should hear this plainly: it is a blood sausage, exactly the family the French call boudin noir — and the Korean twist is the glass noodles and barley that give it its chewy, gentle-soft texture.\n\n⚖️ FIRST, THE FILLING — THE BLOOD AND THE STARCH ARE THE TEXTURE DUO. Mix the fresh blood with the soaked and cut glass noodles, the cooked barley, the garlic, ginger, onion, soy, sesame oil, gochugaru and a generous seasoning of salt and pepper. The noodles and barley are not garnish — they are what makes sundae chewy rather than dense, and they absorb the blood into a soft, savoury crumb once cooked.\n\nTHE CASING — WORTH BUYING RIGHT, OR SKIP IT. Natural pork casing (available from the butcher or an Asian grocer) is the traditional tube. Rinse it thoroughly and leave it in cold water while the filling comes together. If casing feels out of reach, the honest hack is to cook the mixture as a blood pudding in a greased loaf tin, covered, baked gently — the texture of a sliced sundae with none of the casing fuss (see the Quick version).\n\n⚖️ THE STUFF AND THE POACH — HALF FULL, AND NEVER A BOIL. Tie one end of the casing. Using a funnel or a piping bag, fill it no more than half full (it expands as it sets) and tie it off in a coil or into individual sausages. Then THE ONE RULE: poach in barely trembling water at 85-90\u00b0C for 35-40 minutes — never a rolling boil, which bursts the casing and wrecks the texture. A skewer through a sausage should come out clean.\n\nSERVE WARM, SLICED INTO ROUNDS, WITH THE EXACT TRADITIONAL TRIO: a small dish of flaky salt, a bowl of sesame seeds, and the classic dipping sauce of soy, vinegar and a pinch of chilli. In Korea the street ritual is to skewer each warm slice, dip it in the salt, and eat it with the rice cakes from the neighbouring tteokbokki — the combination of the soft, earthy sausage and the fiery red sauce is the classic street pairing.\n\n⚖️ THE TEOKBOKKI TRADITION: sundae and tteokbokki are the inseparable two-piece street snack — the spicy rice cakes and the milky, iron-rich sausage balancing each other like the yin and yang of the sidewalk.","cookTime":"Prep 30 min · Poach 40 min","kcal":"~480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 26g · Carbs 30g · Fat 28g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Sundae is best the day it is made. Keeps 3 days refrigerated, sliced and reheated gently in a pan with a little oil — or even better, pan-fried until just crisp on the cut faces, which is how vendors revive it. Freezes for a month, poached then cooled whole.","chefNotes":"Two rules only: half-full filling in the casing (it expands), and a poach that NEVER boils (85-90\u00b0C, 35-40 minutes). The noodles and barley are the texture, the salt at the table is the seasoning, and the soy-vinegar-chilli dip is non-negotiable. And if casing is out of reach — the baked tin version is honestly delicious.","pairsWith":"Tteokbokki (the inseparable street pairing) · Flaky salt + sesame seeds · Soy-vinegar-chilli dip · Cold soju","goesWith":["Tteokbokki","Kimchi Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Sundae is Korea's answer to the worldwide family of blood sausages — a direct cousin of boudin noir, blutwurst and morcilla — but with a distinctly Korean soul: the filling leans on sweet potato glass noodles and barley rather than just fat and bread, which is why sliced sundae has that chewy, almost delicate crumb. It is the quintessential Korean street food, sold by the coil at market stalls and eaten with the spicy tteokbokki from the stall next door — the two together being one of the most famous flavour marriages on the Korean street.","howThisFeels":"The warm coils sliced on the tray, each soft-chewy disc of blood, barley and noodles dipped in flaky salt — earthy, iron-rich and oddly comforting, the street-food taste of a Korean market in winter.","leftovers":["The vendor's revival: pan-fry leftover slices in a little oil until the cut faces go crisp — the best leftover move there is.","Chop leftover sundae into a pot of kimchi jjigae for a rich, earthy depth.","Slice it into a bowl of warm broth with vegetables for a restorative sundae soup."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Cased Sundae","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"The genuine coil — blood, noodles, barley and garlic in a natural casing, poached gently and served with the salt trio.","trivia":"The classic is the full casing method — the way the market vendors make it, by the coil. The half-full stuffing and the never-boil poach are the two rules that keep the casing intact and the texture soft.","delta":{},"ingredients":"200g fresh pork blood · 50g glass noodles · 40g cooked barley · 30g glutinous rice (optional) · 1 onion, minced · 4 garlic cloves · 5g ginger · 10ml soy · 5ml sesame oil · 1 tsp gochugaru · pepper · salt · 1m pork casing · salt, sesame, dip for the table","method":["Mix the blood with the soaked noodles, barley, rice, onion, garlic, ginger, soy, sesame and seasoning.","Rinse the casing; tie one end.","Funnel the filling in HALF full; tie into a coil or individual sausages.","Poach at 85-90\u00b0C (barely trembling, never boiling) for 35-40 minutes until a skewer comes out clean.","Slice warm; serve with flaky salt, sesame seeds and the soy-vinegar-chilli dip."]},{"name":"Baked Sundae (No Casing) — Budget & Approachable","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":18,"howThisFeels":"All the savoury blood-and-barley crumb, baked in a greased tin and sliced — the honest home version for cooks who cannot find casing.","trivia":"The blood pudding baked in a tin is the classic European boudin method, and it works perfectly for the same Korean filling — you lose the tube, but the texture and the salt-and-dip ritual survive completely.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the casing entirely. Grease a small loaf tin, pour in the same filling, cover with foil, and bake at 170\u00b0C for 40-45 minutes until set and a skewer comes out clean. Cool slightly, slice thick, and serve with the same salt, sesame and dip. It slices like a pudding and eats like sundae."}]}},{"name":"Tofu & Barley Sundae-Style Roll (Veg)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"An honest plant-based take — the barley, noodles, garlic and soy are the innards; a savoury tofu base stands in for the blood, keeping the chew and the seasoning table.","trivia":"This is an adaptation, respectfully noted — the traditional coil is defined by its blood. But the chewy noodle-and-barley crumb and the salty, garlicky seasoning are almost entirely the eating experience, and a firm tofu base with dark soy for colour and deep mushroom stock for savour makes a surprisingly convincing, kind-hearted version.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"200g fresh pork blood (from the butcher — about 1½ cups)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"250g firm tofu, crumbled finely"},{"item":"1 tbsp dark soy sauce (for colour)"},{"item":"100ml strong mushroom stock (for savour and moisture)"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Replace the blood with finely crumbled firm tofu bound with the dark soy, the mushroom stock and a little more sesame oil, mixed with the same noodles, barley, garlic and aromatics. Continue with the baked loaf method (or the casing if you enjoy the craft — the stuffing has the right crumb). The result is a savoury, chewy, salt-and-dip-loving tube that honours the texture and the ritual without the blood."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-makgeolli","name":"Makgeolli","nameAlt":"Korean cloudy rice wine — the milky, fizzy, beloved drink","aliases":["makgeolli","rice wine korea","korean rice wine","cloudy rice wine","makoli"],"course":"drink","type":["drink","beverage","alcohol","fermented","rice-wine"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","drinking-food","social","anju"],"mood":"Playful & convivial","time":"Soak 2 h · Steam 30 min · Ferment 4-7 days (mostly hands-off) · Total ~1 week","health":"A genuinely living drink — cloudy with live yeasts and unfiltered rice, lighter in alcohol than most wines (~7%), with B-vitamins from the fermentation. It is alcohol, enjoyed honestly and responsibly.","allergen":"Alcohol · gluten (the nuruk starter may carry some; brewers' rice is gluten-free) · rice","budget":"★★★ — rice, water and a starter; a culture's whole drink for the price of a bag of rice","audience":"The social drink — Korea's beloved milky pour, served chilled and cloudy, whirled in its bottle before serving, best shared with friends and a plate of pajeon.","season":"A year-round companion, but its peak is the summer — served ice-cold from clay jars and earthen bowls.","ingredients":"300g glutinous rice (sweet rice) \u00b7 50g nuruk (Korean fermentation starter \u2014 the traditional mould-yeast block; available at Asian grocers or online) \u00b7 750ml water (filtered is best) \u00b7 30g sugar (optional, for a friendlier first brew \u2014 traditional brews often skip it) \u00b7 a clean 2L glass jar \u00b7 a clean cloth cover or a lid with a loose fit","method":"⚖️ MAK GEOLLI IS KOREA'S CLOUDY RICE WINE — a living, milky, lightly fizzy brew made from glutinous rice, water and nuruk, the traditional Korean fermentation starter. It is the oldest drink on the Korean table, the ancestor of the clear soju, and its cloudiness is not a flaw — it is the point. The sediment is the soul: live yeasts and unfiltered rice, which is why every bottle says SHAKE BEFORE POURING. Brewing it is a week of watching rice and water become something alive — the easiest drink fermentation there is, and the most rewarding. THE WHOLE METHOD IS: STEAM RICE, COOL IT, MIX WITH NURUK AND WATER, AND WAIT.\n\n⚖️ NURUK — THE INGREDIENT THAT MAKES IT KOREAN. Nuruk is the traditional brick of wheat (or barley) crusted with wild moulds and yeasts — a cousin of the Japanese koji that makes sake and miso. It is the culture that turns rice into makgeolli, and it is the one ingredient worth seeking at an Asian grocer (or online). Bread yeast is NOT the same — it ferments sugar, while nuruk's moulds actually break the rice's starch down first. The honest shortcut for a first brew is a makgeolli brew kit that includes a starter; the honest tradition is the nuruk brick.\n\n⚖️ STEP ONE — THE RICE. Soak the glutinous rice 2 hours, then steam it (or cook in a rice cooker with less water than usual) until tender but dry — the rice should be soft, sticky and not wet. Spread it on a clean tray and let it cool completely, to about body temperature. Hot rice would kill the nuruk's cultures — this is the one temperature moment that matters.\n\n⚖️ STEP TWO — THE MIX. Crumble the nuruk into the warm-cooled rice, add the water (and the optional sugar — many first brews use it for a friendlier sweetness), and mix everything thoroughly with CLEAN hands or a cleaned spoon. Transfer to the jar, cover with a clean cloth, and leave in a warm corner (20-25\u00b0C).\n\n⚖️ STEP THREE — THE WAIT, AND THE WATCH. Days 1-2: the mash begins to bubble and froth, softly alive. Days 3-4: stir the mash once a day with a clean spoon — the sign of a healthy ferment is gentle, steady bubbling. By day 4-7, the bubbling slows and the liquid has settled cloudy with the mash sinking. The taste test is the only judge: when it is gently sweet, softly fizzy and faintly sour, the brew is ready.\n\n⚖️ STEP FOUR — THE POUR. To drink the milky traditional way, simply DIG A LADLE into the whole mash — the cloudy brew IS makgeolli, sediment and all — and serve it chilled. To make the cleaner bottled version, strain the liquid through a fine cloth, bottle it, and let it carbonate 1 day at room temperature for the classic gentle fizz. Either way: SHAKE (or stir) BEFORE POURING — you are drinking a living thing on purpose.\n\nSERVE CHILLED in the traditional shallow earthenware bowls (patbae-ju), with somyun or pajeon — makgeolli and the spring-onion pancake being one of Korea's most beloved pairings.","cookTime":"Soak 2 h · Steam 30 min · Ferment 4-7 days (mostly hands-off)","kcal":"~130 kcal per 250ml glass","nutrition":"Alcohol ~7% · B-vitamins and live cultures from the unfiltered fermentation","storage":"The brewed makgeolli keeps 3-5 days in the fridge, and honestly TASTES best days 2-3 as it settles and mellows. The bottled version keeps carbonating gently — open loose. It is alive; keep it cold, shake it before pouring, and drink it while it is young.","chefNotes":"The three rules: COOL the rice to body temperature before the nuruk goes in (hot rice kills the culture), STIR the mash once a day through the ferment (give the living thing air), and SHAKE BEFORE POURING always (the sediment is the soul). Taste is the only true timer — sweet, fizzy, faintly sour — nothing on a calendar.","pairsWith":"Haemul pajeon (the classic pairing) · Smoked cheeses and nuts (the modern delight) · Fruit · Cold beer on hot days","goesWith":["Haemul Pajeon","Korean Fried Chicken"],"trivia":"Makgeolli means literally 'roughly filtered' — and the name is the recipe: it is the cloudy, unfiltered ancestor of Korean clear rice spirits, drunk for centuries by farmers and scholars alike. It was once the everyday drink of the countryside, poured from clay jars — and after a long near-twilight of cheap industrial versions, it has had a spectacular revival since the early 2000s as artisanal brews, with Seoul's makgeolli bars treating the milky cloud like a fine wine's sediment. The fermentation brings genuine nutrition — B vitamins and live cultures — and the tradition of serving it in earthenware bowls with a shared ladle makes it the most social pour on the Korean table.","howThisFeels":"The bottle whirled milky-cloudy, the first cool sip of something sweet, gentle-fizzy and faintly wild on the tongue — and the instant urge to order another plate of pajeon to share between friends.","leftovers":["The fizzy bottled brew keeps 3-5 days cold — the classic leftover is strawberries or peaches soaked in the leftover makgeolli overnight, a modern sweet at the end of the party.","Use the settled leftover as a gentle marinade base for pork or a splash in a soy glaze.","The spent rice mash makes a wonderful garden compost — the whole brew gives twice."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Nuruk Brew","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":14,"howThisFeels":"The real culture — glutinous rice, a nuruk brick and a week of watching the mash bubble itself alive.","trivia":"The classic is nuruk and glutinous rice — the traditional culture that turns starch into wine over a week of quiet, living fermentation.","delta":{},"ingredients":"300g glutinous rice · 50g nuruk · 750ml water · 30g sugar (optional) · clean jar","method":["Soak the rice 2 hours; steam until tender but dry; cool COMPLETELY to body temperature.","Crumble the nuruk into the rice with the water (and sugar); mix thoroughly.","Cover the jar with a cloth; leave at 20-25\u00b0C.","Stir once daily days 3-4; watch for gentle bubbling.","Taste from day 4 — ready when gently sweet, softly fizzy, faintly sour (4-7 days).","Strain for a cleaner bottle, or ladle the milky mash straight; SHAKE before pouring; serve chilled."]},{"name":"Quick — Bread-Yeast First Brew (5 Days)","icon":"⚡","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":12,"howThisFeels":"The honest beginner's path when nuruk is out of reach — ordinary brewers' yeast plus a spoon of flour as the culture stand-in, and a sweeter, friendlier first brew.","trivia":"Traditional nuruk is the real culture, but a small pinch of dry brewers' yeast with a spoon of flour to feed it produces a genuinely decent, sweet-and-fizzy first makgeolli — the honest modern workaround. The result is more wine-like and less wild, but the milky pour and the shake-before-serve ritual are all yours.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Replace the nuruk with a pinch (about 2g) of dry brewers' yeast and one spoon of plain flour to feed the culture. The rest is identical: steamed rice, cooled to body temperature, mixed with the yeast-flour and water, and fermented 4-5 days at room temperature with a daily stir. The brew will be sweeter and simpler — a friendly, honest first makgeolli that earns the ritual. (Traditionalists, look away.)"}]}},{"name":"Makgeolli with Strawberries (Modern Sweet)","icon":"🍓","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":16,"howThisFeels":"The summery modern favourite — ripe strawberries or peaches macerating in the cloud, turning the milky pour pink and fragrant.","trivia":"Fruit-infused makgeolli is the modern Seoul bar trend — the gentle, slightly wild wine takes fruit beautifully, and strawberries are the classic crown.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"To the finished brew (either version), add a handful of washed, hulled and lightly crushed strawberries (or sliced peaches) to the jug or bottle, and let them steep 6-12 hours in the fridge. Serve with a berry bobbing in the milky cloud. The fruit softens the fizz and perfumes the whole pour — the modern sweet that turns a rustic drink into a party."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-patbingsu","name":"Patbingsu","nameAlt":"Shaved ice with sweet red beans — Korea's summer dessert icon","aliases":["patbingsu","bingsu","korean shaved ice","red bean shaved ice","빙수"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","ice","summer","shaved-ice","sweet"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["summer","party","dessert","cafe"],"mood":"Joyful & refreshing","time":"Prep 20 min · Freeze 4 h (or overnight) · Assembly 5 min","health":"A sugar-forward special treat — the sweet red beans and condensed milk make it a reward, not a health bowl. Red beans themselves are protein-and-fibre good; the dessert is the occasion.","allergen":"Dairy (condensed milk — skip or use coconut cream for vegan) · red beans (legume) · rice (the tteok) · fruit (assorted)","budget":"★★★ — shaved ice is water and the toppings are pantry; this is the cheapest big-hearted dessert on the summer table","audience":"The summer-rescue dessert — the cafe date, the family treat, the after-the-meal celebration on the hottest day of the year.","season":"SUMMER, without apology — the bingsu cafe is the summer gathering place, and Korea queues for it when the mercury climbs","ingredients":"ICE BASE: 500ml milk or water, frozen solid in a shallow tray (or 400g already-prepared shaved ice) \u00b7 THE RED BEANS: 150g dried adzuki beans, soaked \u00b7 50g sugar (for the bean paste) \u00b7 a pinch of salt \u00b7 THE ROOF: 30g condensed milk (or coconut cream for vegan) \u00b7 40g tteok (rice cake) pieces, or small chewy mochi \u00b7 30g assorted fruit (strawberries, mango or kiwi) \u00b7 2 scoops of vanilla ice cream (optional but classic) \u00b7 a scatter of roasted soy powder (or crushed peanuts)","method":"⚖️ PATBINGSU IS KOREA'S SUMMER DESSERT ICON — a towering bowl of snow-fine shaved ice, poured with condensed milk, crowned with sweet red beans, chewy rice cakes, fruit and a scoop of ice cream. The name means 'red bean shaved ice' (pat = red bean, bingsu = shaved ice) and it is the dish that empties the cafes in the heat. The two essentials are the FINENESS of the ice and the SWEETNESS of the beans — everything else is the joyful topping lattice.\n\n⚖️ FIRST, THE RED BEANS — THE EARTHLY HEART OF THE BOWL. Soak the adzuki beans overnight (or 6 hours). Drain, add fresh water to cover plus a good two fingers above, and simmer gently 1 hour to 1 hour 15 minutes until the beans are completely soft — mashable between finger and thumb. Drain and reserve a little of the water. Return the beans to the pot with the sugar and a pinch of salt, and cook gently, mashing a little, until the mixture turns into a soft, glossy paste — sweet, earthy, and the soul of the bowl. Cool it completely. (The honest shortcut: good tinned sweetened red bean paste, the kind sold at Asian grocers, is a legitimate stand-in — this is the one place the shortcut is completely accepted.)\n\n⚖️ THE ICE — SNOW, NOT CUBES. The shaved ice of a proper bingsu is shaved impossibly fine, like falling snow — not crushed cubes, which crunch instead of melting softly. The home truth: freeze the milk (or water) in a SHALLOW tray, then shave the block with a food processor's grating blade, or scrape it with a strong fork into fine snow. Freezing a thin layer and scraping it snow creates the right featherlight texture. Bring the ice together only at the LAST MINUTE — it melts fast, and the window is the joy.\n\n⚖️ THE BUILD — ICE, BEAUTY, AND THE TOPPINGS MUST TOWER. Mound the fine ice high in a big bowl like a snow mountain. Pour the condensed milk (or coconut cream) over so it soaks into the flanks. Spoon the sweet red beans generously over the top — not scattered, a proper crown. Tuck in the chewy rice cakes, the fruit, and the scoop of vanilla ice cream on top like a little snow-capped hat, and finish with the roasted soy powder dusting the whole mountain.\n\n⚖️ EAT IT FAST, BY THE SNOWFUL — THE KOREAN WAY. The ritual is to spoon from the peak down, each spoon getting a bit of everything: snow, condensed milk, a crumb of red bean, a piece of chewy tteok, a berry. It is a race against the melt, and a proper patbingsu is one of the great summer communions — one bowl, two spoons, shared on the hottest afternoon of the year.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Freeze 4 h (or overnight)","kcal":"~420 kcal","nutrition":"Carbs 80g · Fat 8g · Protein 10g · Sugar ~60g (a treat, honestly stated)","storage":"Patbingsu is made-to-melt — assemble and eat immediately. The cooked red bean paste keeps 5 days refrigerated (the most useful leftover here) and freezes 3 months. The ice is only good the moment it is shaved.","chefNotes":"Four rules: the ice must be SNOW-fine (freeze shallow, shave fine — never cubes), the red beans must be properly sweet-savoury (the pinch of salt is the secret), the condensed milk soaks the flanks (not just a drizzle on top), and EAT IT FAST — the melt is part of the occasion.","pairsWith":"A hot green tea or coffee to contrast · Friends and two spoons – a party of two · The hottest afternoon available","goesWith":["Korean Fried Chicken","Korean Fried Chicken"],"trivia":"Patbingsu began in the early 20th century as a simple bowl of shaved ice and sweet red beans, and grew into the undisputed summer dessert of Korea — today you find bingsu cafes on every corner, serving towering mountains crowned with everything from fruit to cheesecake. The ice itself became an art: proper bingsu ice is frozen MILK shaved fine, so it is creamy rather than watery, and the texture debate — snow vs ice, hard-frozen vs soft-serve — is a genuine field of Korean culinary argument. It is a summer symbol to the point that the arrival of bingsu season opening is treated like a celebration.","howThisFeels":"The snowy mountain arriving on the hottest day of the year, the spoon plunging from the peak through condensed-milk snow to a chewy tteok and a sweet red-bean crumb — the collective ahh of two people sharing one bowl and winning the race against the melt.","leftovers":["The red bean paste is the treasure: it keeps 5 days and becomes the filling for toast, pancakes or a little sweet juk.","Freeze the leftover milk-ice block for a second impromptu bowl whenever the craving strikes.","The tteok pieces freeze well — the next bowl needs only fresh ice and a sunny afternoon."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Red-Bean Patbingsu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"The snow mountain with condensed milk, sweet red bean crown, chewy tteok, fruit and the vanilla scoop on top.","trivia":"The classic is ice, red beans, condensed milk and tteok — the original bingsu, and still the standard by which all modern mountains are measured.","delta":{},"ingredients":"500ml milk, frozen in a tray · 150g dried adzuki beans · 50g sugar · pinch of salt · 30g condensed milk · 40g tteok pieces · 30g fruit · 2 scoops vanilla ice cream · roasted soy powder","method":["Soak the beans overnight; simmer 1 h 15 min until mashable; drain, reserving a little water.","Cook the beans with the sugar and salt into a glossy soft paste; cool completely.","Freeze the milk shallow; shave into fine snow at the last minute.","Mound the snow high; pour condensed milk down the flanks.","Crown with red beans, tteok, fruit and the vanilla scoop; dust with soy powder.","Eat fast, spoonfuls from the peak — one bowl, two spoons."]},{"name":"Mango Patbingsu (Modern Favourite)","icon":"🥭","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The beloved modern mountain — ripe mango chunks and mango pulp drenching the milk-snow, with the red beans tucked in as the traditional anchor.","trivia":"Mango bingsu is the most popular of the modern fruit variations — sweet tropical fruit against the milky snow is a new-school classic, and the red beans still hide underneath like a wink at the original.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Crown the snow with generous chunks of ripe mango and a ladle of mango pulp (blended ripe mango with a little of the syrup) in place of part of the mixed fruit, keeping the red beans, condensed milk and tteok as the traditional base. The mango-snow-cream trifecta is the modern reason the cafes line up."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 5-Minute No-Machine Bingsu","icon":"⚡","diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"costPP":16,"howThisFeels":"The honest home hack — scrape frozen milk-cream into snow with a fork, finish with tinned red beans, and the bowl is on the table in five minutes.","trivia":"A tray of frozen sweetened milk or cream, scraped with a strong fork, produces surprisingly fine snow in a pinch — and tinned sweet red bean paste plus tinned fruit makes the whole mountain a 5-minute assembly.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Freeze a thin layer of sweetened milk or coconut cream in a shallow tray overnight, then scrape it with a strong fork into fine snow at assembly time. Use good tinned sweetened red beans and tinned fruit for the tower, with condensed milk or coconut cream poured over. Five minutes, zero machines, a genuine bowl — the melts-and-genuineness debate is best had over the empty bowl."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-jjajangmyeon","name":"Jjajangmyeon","nameAlt":"Black bean noodles — the beloved Chinese-Korean classic","aliases":["jjajangmyeon","jajangmyeon","black bean noodles","korean black bean noodles","jjajang"],"course":"main","type":["main","noodles","sauce","chinese-korean","comfort","one-bowl"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","comfort","celebration","quick"],"mood":"Comforting & indulgent","time":"Prep 15 min · Cook 25 min","health":"A rich, moreish bowl — the black bean paste and caramelised onion make it indulgence food, best balanced with the cucumber finish and a light side.","allergen":"Gluten (wheat noodles, soy sauce) · soy (soy sauce, chunjang) · sesame (sesame oil)","budget":"★★★ — the black bean paste (chunjang) is cheap and the vegetables are pantry; a bowl for pennies","audience":"The everyday comfort noodle — and the special-day noodle too: on moving day, Koreans order jjajangmyeon from the delivery service as the new-home's first meal.","season":"Year-round comfort — the delivery classic for any night that asks for a glossy, savoury bowl","ingredients":"120g fresh Chinese-style wheat noodles (or udon) · 25g chunjang (Korean black bean paste — at Asian grocers) · 60g pork belly, small diced (or 100g firm tofu, for vegan) · 30g onion, diced · 30g potato or zucchini, small diced · 30g cabbage, chopped · 10ml soy sauce · 5g sugar · 100ml water or stock · 1 tsp cornflour mixed with 2 tbsp water (the thickener) · 10ml sesame oil · 1 cucumber, in fine matchsticks · a splash of oil for the pan","method":"JAJANGMYEON IS THE BELOVED CHINESE-KOREAN CLASSIC — a crown of glossy, savoury black bean sauce over chewy wheat noodles, finished with crisp cucumber matchsticks. It arrived with Chinese migrants in the early 1900s, took on a fully Korean life, and became the nation's most-loved delivery noodle. The sauce gets its glossy black colour and deep savour from chunjang — roasted black soybean paste — and its sweetness from patiently caramelised onion. THE TWO RULES ARE THE FRY AND THE THICKEN.\n\nTHE BLACK BEAN PASTE MUST BE FRIED — THIS IS THE WHOLE COLOUR AND FLAVOUR. In a hot pan with oil, fry the chunjang paste for a good 2-3 minutes, stirring constantly, until it darkens and smells deeply roasted — this stir-frying of the paste (bokkeum) is the step that takes it from raw paste to glossy sauce. The difference between a flat brown sauce and a silky rich one is almost entirely these three minutes. Keep the heat confident so the paste bubbles without burning.\n\nTHE ONIONS ARE CARAMELISED SLOWLY — THE SECOND SECRET. In a separate pan (or after removing the paste), sweat the diced onion over medium-low in a little oil for 5-6 minutes until it turns golden and sweet — this caramelised sweetness is what balances the paste's deep savour. Add the pork (or tofu) and brown it, then the potato, zucchini and cabbage, and cook everything together for a few minutes.\n\nTHE TWO PANS MEET, THE SAUCE DEEPENS. Return the fried chunjang to the vegetables, add the soy, sugar and the water (or stock), and simmer everything 8-10 minutes until the vegetables are tender and the sauce is rich. Then the last step: stir in the cornflour slurry and let it bubble 1 minute until everything turns GLOSSY and coats the back of a spoon — the shine is the point, and the sauce should be thick enough to cling to the noodles, not swim around them.\n\nTHE NOODLES AND THE CROWN. Cook the fresh noodles per the packet (they should be springy-chewy, not soft), drain, and swirl them into a bowl. Spoon the glossy black sauce generously over the top and crown with the fine cucumber matchsticks — the cool, fresh crunch against the deep hot sauce is a big part of why this bowl works.\n\nTHE RITUAL: mix it thoroughly at the table — the black sauce coats every strand — and eat with a pickled radish or kimchi on the side, the sharp counter to the rich black sauce.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~560 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 22g · Carbs 80g · Fat 18g · Sodium ~1300mg","storage":"The sauce keeps 3 days refrigerated, and it genuinely improves as the flavours marry — reheat gently and cook a fresh batch of noodles. The black paste stains everything it touches, so store it in a sealed container and know it wears its heart on its sleeve.","chefNotes":"Two rules make the bowl: FRY the chunjang paste hard for 2-3 minutes (the colour and savour live in that step), and CARAMELISE the onions slowly (the sweetness is half the sauce). The cornflour slurry at the end is what turns it from soup to GLOSSY sauce — that shine is the identity of jjajangmyeon.","pairsWith":"Pickled radish (danmuji) or kimchi — the sharp counter to the rich sauce · A light soup alongside · Rice on the side (the black sauce over rice is the classic follow-up)","goesWith":["Jjamppong","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Jjajangmyeon is the Korean adaptation of Chinese zhajiangmian — noodles with fried sauce — brought to Korea in the early 1900s by Chinese immigrants in Incheon's Chinatown, and completely remade in Korean kitchens. It became the definitive Korean delivery noodle, and its cultural weight is enormous: jjajangmyeon day is celebrated on 14 April by people without partners (the joke being that black sauce is for the single), and on moving day it is the traditional first meal ordered in the new home — because it arrives fast and needs no plates. The black bean paste, chunjang, is roasted and salted so the sauce needs almost no additional salt — the sweetness of the caramelised onions and pork does the balancing.","howThisFeels":"The unmistakable glossy black crown arriving on the white noodles, the sharp cool crunch of cucumber cutting the rich hot sauce, and the deeply satisfying mix-at-the-table ritual that turns the whole bowl one perfect colour.","leftovers":["The classic: spoon the leftover black sauce over a bowl of hot rice with a little cucumber — jjajang-bap, the beloved second act.","The sauce reheats beautifully for a fresh noodle bowl tomorrow — the flavours only deepen overnight.","Stir the leftover sauce into fried rice with a pinch more soy for an instant glossy jjajang fried rice."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Pork Jjajangmyeon","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"The full classic — fried chunjang, caramelised onion, pork, glossy thick sauce over chewy noodles with cucumber.","trivia":"The classic is the delivery-standard: pork belly, onion, potato and zucchini in the glossy black sauce — the version the whole nation has delivered on moving day.","delta":{},"ingredients":"120g fresh wheat noodles · 25g chunjang · 60g pork belly, diced · 30g onion · 30g potato · 30g cabbage · 10ml soy · 5g sugar · 100ml stock · cornflour slurry · 10ml sesame oil · cucumber · oil","method":["Fry the chunjang paste in hot oil 2-3 minutes until deeply roasted; set aside.","Caramelise the diced onion slowly in oil 5-6 minutes; add the pork and brown, then the potato, zucchini and cabbage.","Return the paste, add soy, sugar and stock; simmer 8-10 minutes until tender and rich.","Stir in the cornflour slurry; bubble 1 minute until glossy and thick.","Serve over springy noodles, crowned with cucumber matchsticks; mix at the table."]},{"name":"Vegan Jjajangmyeon (Tofu and Shiitake)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The same glossy black crown with tofu and shiitake doing the savoury work — a bowl that loses nothing to the meat version.","trivia":"The chunjang paste, cornflour, soy and sesame are all naturally vegan — this adaptation simply trades the pork for pan-crisped tofu and shiitake, and the sauce is every bit as deep.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"60g pork belly, small diced (or 100g firm tofu, for vegan)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"100g firm tofu, cubed and pan-crisped"},{"item":"30g shiitake mushrooms, sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Crisp the tofu cubes and brown the shiitake in the pan with the caramelised onions before the other vegetables go in. Everything else — the fried chunjang, the slow onions, the glossy slurry finish — is identical and already completely plant-based. The shiitake brings the deep savour the pork would have lent."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-jjamppong","name":"Jjamppong","nameAlt":"Spicy seafood noodle soup — the fiery red bowl of the Chinese-Korean nook","aliases":["jjamppong","jjampong","spicy seafood noodle soup","korean spicy noodles seafood"],"course":"main","type":["main","noodles","soup","seafood","spicy","chinese-korean"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","comfort","restaurant-style","quick"],"mood":"Fiery and warming","time":"Prep 20 min · Cook 20 min","health":"A fiery, seafood-rich broth with plenty of vegetables — genuinely filling and far lighter than its red look suggests; the spice is warmth as much as heat.","allergen":"Seafood (prawns, squid, mussels) · gluten (wheat noodles, soy sauce) · soy (soy sauce, gochugaru paste) · sesame","budget":"★★☆ — a little seafood stretches far in a big noodle soup; the broth is pantry-built","audience":"The restaurant-bowl standard — the fiery twin of jjajangmyeon on every Chinese-Korean menu, ordered when the jjajangmyeon was too gentle a plan","season":"Cold nights and damp weather — the red broth is the warming counter to the day","ingredients":"120g fresh wheat noodles (or udon) · 100g mixed seafood (prawns, squid rings, mussels) · 40g pork belly, sliced (optional but classic) · 100g mixed vegetables (onion, cabbage, courgette, carrot) in thick slices · 15g gochugaru (Korean chilli flakes) · 5ml gochujang (optional, for deeper red) · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 4 cloves garlic, minced · 2 slices ginger · 400ml seafood or chicken stock · 1 spring onion, in lengths · black pepper","method":"JJAMPPONG IS THE FIERY TWIN OF JJAJANGMYEON — the spicy red seafood noodle soup that shares the Chinese-Korean menu with its glossy black sibling, and could not be more different in temperament. Where jjajangmyeon is sweet and comforting, jjamppong is a red, fiery, seafood-laden broth built to warm you from the inside out. THE METHOD IS THE BLOOM AND THE SOUP.\n\nFIRST, THE FIRE — BLOOM THE GOCHUGARU IN OIL. Heat the sesame oil in a deep pot, add the garlic, ginger and the gochugaru, and stir over medium heat for 30-45 seconds until the flakes bloom into a deep red, fragrant oil — this step is the difference between a red-tinted broth and the rich, flaming colour of a proper jjamppong. It is the same bloom technique as yukgaejang and the heart of the red Korean school.\n\nTHE PORK GOES IN NEXT — THE RICHNESS. Add the sliced pork belly (if using) and brown it in the red oil for a minute or two — its fat absorbs the chilli bloom and becomes the backbone of the broth. Then the sliced vegetables, stir-fried until just beginning to soften — the cabbage, onion, courgette and carrot should still hold their shape in the finished bowl.\n\nTHE STOCK AND THE RED — IN WITH THE LIQUID. Pour in the stock (or water deepened with a strip of kelp simmered 5 minutes), add the soy and a spoon of gochujang for extra depth and a deeper red, and bring the pot to a rolling bubble. Let it simmer 5 minutes so the vegetables soften and the broth turns deeply red and glossy.\n\nTHE SEAFOOD GOES IN LAST — AND ONLY JUST. Drop in the prawns, squid and mussels for the final 3-4 minutes of cooking — seafood needs minutes, not an eternity. When the prawns are pink and the squid just curled and tender, the soup is done.\n\nTHE NOODLES — COOKED, SWIRLED, AND SMOTHERED IN RED. Cook the noodles per the packet, drain, and swirl them into a deep bowl. Ladle the fiery broth and seafood generously over, add the spring onion lengths, and serve immediately.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 20 min","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 30g · Carbs 70g · Fat 14g · Sodium ~1400mg","storage":"The broth keeps 2 days refrigerated and is every bit as fiery on day two; reheat gently and cook a fresh batch of noodles and a fresh handful of seafood to revive it. The seafood itself is best freshly added.","chefNotes":"Two laws: BLOOM the gochugaru hard in the oil before any liquid goes in (that deep red is the dish), and ADD THE SEAFOOD LAST for only 3-4 minutes (rubbery squid is the one tragedy of jjamppong). The noodles are cooked then swirled into the broth — never boiled in it — so they stay springy.","pairsWith":"Jjajangmyeon on the same table (the black and red twins — order both when the mood is indecision) · Cold beer · Pickled radish between the heat","goesWith":["Jjajangmyeon","Sundubu Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Jjamppong is the other half of the legendary Chinese-Korean delivery pair — the fiery seafood noodle soup that sits beside jjajangmyeon on every menu. It originated in the Chinese-Korean community in Incheon in the early 1900s, and its red, briny, vegetable-rich broth is the Korean adaptation of the Chinese chow mein style — remade with gochugaru and gochujang until it became wholly Korean. The pairing is so iconic that Korean delivery apps list them side by side, and the eternal diner's question — jjajang or jjamppong? — is answered by many the cowardly way: both.","howThisFeels":"The red broth steaming up into your face, the springy noodles swimming through, a perfect pink prawn and a curl of just-tender squid — warmth, fire and briny comfort in one deep bowl.","leftovers":["The broth keeps 2 days cold — reheat and add fresh noodles and a handful of seafood for a complete second bowl.","Spoon the leftover red broth over a bowl of rice with an egg for a jjamppong rice-bowl — the broth soaks in gloriously.","Freeze the broth base (before the seafood); a fresh pot is 15 minutes away whenever the craving calls."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Seafood Jjamppong","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42,"howThisFeels":"The full red classic — bloomed gochugaru, pork, vegetables and a mixed seafood haul in a deep fiery broth over springy noodles.","trivia":"The classic is the delivery standard: pork, a mixed seafood medley, and the red-bloomed broth that made jjamppong half of Korean delivery culture.","delta":{},"ingredients":"120g fresh noodles · 100g mixed seafood · 40g pork belly · 100g mixed vegetables · 15g gochugaru · 5ml gochujang · 10ml soy · 5ml sesame oil · 4 cloves garlic · 2 slices ginger · 400ml stock · 1 spring onion · pepper","method":["Bloom the garlic, ginger and gochugaru in sesame oil 30-45 seconds until deep red.","Brown the pork in the red oil; add the vegetables and stir-fry until just softening.","Add the stock, soy and gochujang; simmer 5 minutes until deep red and glossy.","Add the seafood for the final 3-4 minutes until the prawns are pink and the squid just curled.","Serve immediately over springy noodles with spring onion."]},{"name":"Budget — Seafood-Light Jjamppong","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"Mostly squid and prawns with extra vegetables stretching the broth — the fiery character is all in the red, which costs nothing to make.","trivia":"The chilli bloom and the pork are where the broth's character lives — halving the seafood and doubling the vegetables keeps the bowl 90 percent as bold at two-thirds the cost.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use 60g of seafood (mostly squid, a few prawns), double the cabbage and add a sliced mushroom or two. The red bloom carries the whole bowl — the seafood becomes a garnish against the fire rather than the centrepiece, and the budget stretches to feed another bowl."}]}},{"name":"Veggie Jjamppong (Tofu and Mushroom)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The same red fiery broth, deep with mushrooms and tofu instead of seafood — and honestly the red bloom makes the vegetables the star.","trivia":"A mushroom- and tofu-loaded jjamppong with the full chilli bloom is a genuinely satisfying bowl — the fiery broth and the springy noodles are most of the experience, and everything in it is naturally plant-based.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"100g mixed seafood (prawns, squid rings, mussels)"},{"item":"40g pork belly, sliced (optional but classic)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"100g mushrooms (shiitake, oyster), thick-sliced"},{"item":"100g firm tofu, cubed"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Brown the mushrooms hard in the red bloom with the vegetables, and add the cubed tofu with the stock so it warms through. Use a deep kelp-and-mushroom stock in place of chicken, and skip the pork entirely. The red broth, the springy noodles and the fiery comfort are all intact."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-tteokguk","name":"Tteokguk","nameAlt":"Rice cake soup — the New Year's bowl that ages everyone up","aliases":["tteokguk","rice cake soup","korean new year soup","tteok soup"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","rice-cake","new-year","celebration","comfort"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["new-year","celebration","family","winter","breakfast"],"mood":"Warm, auspicious and comforting","time":"Prep 15 min · Cook 30 min","health":"A light, gentle, warming bowl — chewy rice cakes in a clean broth with a shredded omelette on top; the New Year's breakfast that is easy on the stomach and meaningful for the heart.","allergen":"Egg (the classic garnish) · gluten (the soy sauce in the broth) · sesame · rice","budget":"★★★ — rice cakes, stock and an egg; one of the most affordable meaningful bowls in Korean food","audience":"Everyone — the New Year's morning bowl is eaten by the whole family, and the ritual of eating it to grow a year older is shared by every table in Korea.","season":"NEW YEAR (Seollal) is its home, but this gentle warming broth is a winter breakfast all season","ingredients":"150g sliced rice cakes (tteok, the oval discs — fresh or frozen) · 400ml light stock (beef or chicken, or kelp-anchovy for vegan) · 4 slices beef brisket (optional but classic) · 2 eggs · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 1 sheet nori (seaweed), toasted and crumbled · 1 spring onion, finely sliced · sea salt · black pepper","method":"TTEOKGUK IS THE NEW YEAR'S BOWL OF KOREA — a clear, gentle broth of sliced rice cakes, eaten on the first morning of the lunar year, when its ritual is the warmest piece of Korean ceremony you can eat. EATING TTEOKGUK MEANS GAINING A YEAR: the white discs of rice cake stand for purity and a fresh start, and the question children are asked on Seollal morning — have you eaten your tteokguk? — is the way Koreans ask how old you are now.\n\nTHE BROTH IS THE GENTLE FOUNDATION — MADE CLEAN, NEVER MUDDY. The traditional gives the broth its depth from a light beef brisket simmer (or a kelp-anchovy stock for the vegan version — the same gentle base that carries so much of Korean soup). Simmer 15 minutes, strain, and season with the soy and a little salt until it is clean and savoury — a New Year's broth should taste of clear depth, not heavy richness.\n\nTHE RICE CAKES GO IN, AND THE TIMING IS THE SKILL. Add the sliced rice cakes to the simmering broth and cook just 3-5 minutes, until they float and turn soft and CHEWY — the moment they float is the moment they are done. Overcooked rice cakes go gluey and collapse.\n\nTHE GARNISH IS THE BEAUTY — THE SHREDDED OMELETTE AND THE REST. Beat the eggs separately, cook the whites into a thin sheet and the yolks into another, and slice both into fine shreds of white and yellow ribbon. Crown each bowl with the two-colour egg, the crumbled toasted nori, the sliced spring onion and a thread of sesame oil.\n\nTHE RITUAL, SAID PLAINLY: eat the bowl, and you have grown a year older. The children race to the first spoonful; the family shares the gentlest possible breakfast, and the table begins the year in the most Korean of ways.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 30 min","kcal":"~380 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 16g · Carbs 64g · Fat 9g · Sodium ~900mg","storage":"Best the morning it is made — the rice cakes soften as they sit. Keeps a day in the fridge; the broth sets gently and the rice cakes firm — reheat gently, add a splash of stock and a little water, and refresh the egg garnish fresh. Not a freezer dish (the rice cakes collapse).","chefNotes":"The two rules: the rice cakes go in only when the broth is ready and cook just until they FLOAT (3-5 minutes — gluey is the failure), and the broth must be CLEAN and clear, gently seasoned with soy — a heavy broth drowns the New Year's gentleness. The shredded egg ribbon garnish is the beauty, and it is half the ritual.","pairsWith":"A side of the New Year's table (jeon, pancakes, or the classic sides) · Kimchi · A cup of tea after","goesWith":["Bibimbap","Kimchi Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Tteokguk's meaning is inseparable from its ritual: eating it on Seollal, the lunar New Year, is the traditional way Koreans mark growing a year older — children are asked if they have eaten their tteokguk as a way of teasing their new age, and the white rice discs symbolise a clean start and long life. It is one of the few dishes that is simultaneously a whole family's breakfast, a birthday marker and a national symbol of the new year.","howThisFeels":"The warm clear broth, the soft-chewy white rice discs floating through it, the yellow-and-white egg ribbons and dark nori on top — the gentlest, most meaningful first meal of the year, eaten in the warmth of the family table.","leftovers":["The broth alone keeps a day and is the base for a second quick bowl with fresh rice cakes.","Stir leftover tteokguk broth through a bowl of rice with an egg for a gentle rice soup.","The shredded-egg garnish keeps a day — sprinkle it over rice or a salad for the colour."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Beef Tteokguk","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The traditional New Year's bowl — beef-brisket broth, floating chewy rice discs, and the two-colour egg ribbon crown.","trivia":"The classic is beef brisket simmered into a clean broth, the rice cakes floated to chewiness, and the shredded white-and-yellow egg ribbons — the exact bowl of Korean New Year's morning.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g sliced rice cakes · 400ml beef or chicken stock · 4 slices beef brisket · 2 eggs · 10ml soy · 5ml sesame oil · 1 sheet nori · 1 spring onion · salt · pepper","method":["Simmer the brisket in the stock 15 minutes; strain for a clean broth; season with soy and salt.","Cook the rice cakes in the simmering broth 3-5 minutes until they float and turn soft-chewy.","Beat the egg whites and yolks separately; cook both into thin sheets; slice into ribbons.","Bowl: rice cakes and broth, crowned with the egg ribbons, crumbled nori, spring onion and a thread of sesame oil.","Eat — and you have grown a year older, the ritual says."]},{"name":"Vegan Tteokguk (Kelp and Shiitake)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"The same gentle New Year's bowl with a deep kelp-and-shiitake broth in place of beef — the white discs and the ritual unchanged.","trivia":"A kelp-and-shiitake broth is the classic temple-vegetable base, and it carries the gentle New Year's savour beautifully — the soup becomes fully vegan while keeping every symbol.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"4 slices beef brisket (optional but classic)"},{"item":"2 eggs"}],"addIng":[{"item":"1 strip kelp plus 2 dried shiitake for the stock"},{"item":"extra tofu strips for the garnish (optional)"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Simmer a strip of kelp and two dried shiitake in the water for 10 minutes for the vegan broth, then remove them. Skip the egg ribbons and use toasted nori shreds plus a few extra strips of seasoned tofu for the garnish — the white discs, the clean broth and the ritual stand complete."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-hobak-juk","name":"Hobak-juk","nameAlt":"Pumpkin porridge — the golden bowl of Korean comfort","aliases":["hobak juk","pumpkin porridge","korean pumpkin porridge","danhobak juk"],"course":"main","type":["main","porridge","pumpkin","comfort","breakfast","gentle"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["breakfast","comfort","sick-day","winter"],"mood":"Gentle and nourishing","time":"Prep 10 min · Cook 30 min","health":"A genuinely gentle, nourishing bowl — pumpkin's natural sweetness, a little rice and a touch of sesame; the classic sick-day and recovery food of the Korean kitchen.","allergen":"Sesame (sesame oil, seeds) · rice","budget":"★★★ — pumpkin and rice; the cheapest, kindest comfort bowl there is","audience":"The gentle bowl — for breakfast, for the sick day, for the cold evening; Korea's answer to chicken soup in the congee family.","season":"Autumn and winter, when the pumpkin is golden and the cold asks for warmth — but a kind bowl has no strict season","ingredients":"300g pumpkin (any orange variety — butternut or kabocha) peeled and cubed · 40g short-grain rice, soaked 30 minutes · 400ml water or light stock · 10g glutinous rice flour or cooked sweet rice (for the silky finish) · 5ml sesame oil · a pinch of salt · toasted sesame seeds · a little honey or sugar (optional, to taste)","method":"HOBAK-JUK IS KOREA'S GOLDEN PORRIDGE — a gently sweet, silky bowl of pumpkin and rice, the comfort food of the Korean kitchen for breakfast, for the sick day, and for the cold night. It is the sweet side of the Korean congee (juk) family, and its whole character is one of warmth and comfort — the kind of bowl that arrives when someone needs feeding gently.\n\nTHE LOGIC: PUMPKIN FOR SWEETNESS AND COLOUR, RICE FOR BODY. Soak the short-grain rice 30 minutes while the pumpkin prepares. The pumpkin is the star — its natural sweetness and golden colour ARE the dish; the rice cooks into it so the bowl is satin-smooth rather than watery.\n\nTHE COOK — ONE POT, A SIMMER, AND GENTLE STIRRING. In a heavy pot, warm the sesame oil and give the cubed pumpkin a minute over medium heat, then add the drained rice and the water (or light stock). Bring to a gentle simmer and cook 20-25 minutes, stirring every few minutes, until the pumpkin is completely soft and the rice has cooked into the liquid.\n\nTHE PUREE — THIS IS THE WHOLE TEXTURE. Either mash the mixture with a potato masher for a rustic, gently lumpy bowl, or blitz it with an immersion blender for a completely silky one — the classic hobak-juk is silky. For the proper-satin finish, stir in the glutinous rice flour (or a little cooked sweet rice) and simmer 3-4 minutes more — the starch gives it that softly glossy, spoon-coating body that is the bowl's signature. Season with a pinch of salt (which brightens the pumpkin's sweetness) and adjust with honey or sugar only if the pumpkin needs it.\n\nTHE FINISH — THE SEED OIL AND THE TOASTED SEEDS. Ladle the golden porridge into a bowl and finish with a thread of sesame oil and a scatter of toasted sesame seeds — the nutty restraint against the sweet gold. A bowl of hobak-juk is the gentlest yellow on the Korean table, and it tastes exactly the way a warm blanket feels.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 30 min","kcal":"~260 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 5g · Carbs 50g · Fat 6g · Sodium ~120mg","storage":"Hobak-juk keeps 3 days in the fridge and reheats beautifully with a splash of water — it is a genuine make-ahead comfort. It freezes well for a month in portions. The sesame finish is always fresh on top.","chefNotes":"Three gentle rules: SIMMER low and stir often (the rice wants patience, not a hard boil), PUREE until silky and thicken with a little glutinous rice flour for the satin body, and FINISH with a thread of sesame oil and toasted seeds — the nutty lightness is what keeps the sweet bowl subtle.","pairsWith":"A side of kimchi (the savoury counter) · A cup of green tea · More toasted sesame seeds, always","goesWith":["Bibimbap","Kimchi Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Hobak-juk is the sweet member of Korea's juk family — the rice porridges that are the country's ultimate comfort food, from the savoury vegetable juk to this golden pumpkin sweet. It is the classic recovery bowl, fed to children, the sick and the tired, and its gentle sweetness is the reason it is equally loved as a breakfast and an autumn treat. The glutinous rice flour finish is the professional's touch, turning a good porridge into the satin-smooth, spoon-coating bowl that defines the classic.","howThisFeels":"The golden silky bowl steaming gently, the first spoonful warm and softly sweet against the nutty sesame — the taste of being taken care of, the yellow hour of the morning made edible.","leftovers":["Reheat gently with a splash of water for the same golden bowl tomorrow.","Use the cold porridge as the base of a smooth pumpkin smoothie with a little milk and honey.","Freeze it in portions — a kind bowl is always 5 minutes from the pot."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Silky Hobak-juk","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":14,"howThisFeels":"The golden satin bowl — pumpkin, rice, silky from the immersion blender, finished with sesame oil and toasted seeds.","trivia":"The classic is the fully silky version, thickened with glutinous rice flour — the professional finish that makes the bowl spoon-coating smooth.","delta":{},"ingredients":"300g pumpkin, cubed · 40g short-grain rice, soaked · 400ml water · 10g glutinous rice flour · 5ml sesame oil · salt · sesame seeds · honey (optional)","method":["Soak the rice 30 minutes.","Warm the sesame oil; give the pumpkin a minute, then add the rice and water.","Simmer 20-25 minutes, stirring often, until the pumpkin is soft and the rice cooked.","Blitz silky with an immersion blender; stir in the glutinous rice flour; simmer 3-4 minutes for satin body.","Season with salt; finish with sesame oil and toasted sesame seeds."]},{"name":"Budget — Chunky Hobak-juk","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":12,"howThisFeels":"The same golden warmth, mashed rather than blended — a rustic, gently chunky bowl that is even simpler and no less comforting.","trivia":"The rustic mash version is the home-kitchen original — no blender, no rice flour; just pumpkin, rice and a good potato masher. It is the honest everyday version of a bowl that never needs to be fancy to work.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the blender and the glutinous rice flour entirely. Mash the cooked pumpkin-rice mixture with a potato masher for a rustic, gently chunky texture — the rice will have thickened it naturally. Season, finish with the sesame, and you have the honest home-kitchen bowl in fewer steps."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-songpyeon","name":"Songpyeon","nameAlt":"Half-moon rice cakes — the Chuseok gift of pine and bean","aliases":["songpyeon","songpyun","korean rice cake moon","chuseok rice cake","half moon rice cake"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","rice-cake","chuseok","celebration","festival"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["chuseok","family","celebration","festival","make-your-own"],"mood":"Warm, nostalgic and ceremonial","time":"Prep 30 min · Steam 20 min","health":"A gentle, mostly-natural sweet — rice flour, sweet bean paste and a little honey or sugar; the celebration cake of the Korean harvest festival, made for sharing.","allergen":"Sesame (sesame oil coating) · soy (the bean paste — for those with legume sensitivity) · rice","budget":"★★★ — rice flour and beans; the cheapest ceremonial sweet on the Korean table","audience":"The family-making-day cake — traditionally made together on the eve of Chuseok, when the whole household sits around the table shaping half-moons.","season":"CHUSEOK (the autumn harvest festival) is its home — but these are made, and loved, all year at festive tables","ingredients":"150g rice flour (short-grain rice flour — the fine milling kind) · 75ml hot water · a pinch of salt · 100g sweetened red bean paste (or a mix with a little crushed sesame and honey for the filling — the classic savoury-sweet variation) · 5ml sesame oil (to brush) · a handful of pine needles (real ones if you can find them — the authentic steamer layer; or food-safe parchment) · optional: a pinch of green tea powder or pumpkin powder for tinted dough (the festive colours)","method":"SONGPYEON IS THE CHUSEOK RICE CAKE — small half-moons of chewy rice dough, filled with sweetened bean paste (or sesame and honey), and steamed over pine needles until they take on that singular, gentle pine perfume. They are Korea's harvest-festival cake, made BY HAND at the family table on the eve of Chuseok — the autumn thanksgiving — and the making is the ritual: families sit together shaping the little moons, and it is said that a daughter who makes beautiful songpyeon will have a beautiful child.\n\nTHE DOUGH — RICE FLOUR, HOT WATER, AND THE RIGHT STICK. Mix the rice flour and salt, and pour in the hot water, bringing it together with a spatula (it is too hot for fingers at first), then kneading until it comes into a smooth, pliable, playdough-soft ball — soft enough to shape, firm enough to hold. The dough should not crack when it bends; if it does, add a touch more hot water. Rest it 10 minutes under a damp cloth.\n\nTHE FILLING AND THE SHAPE. Portion the dough into small balls (about 20g each). Flatten each into a small disc, place a teaspoon of the sweetened bean paste (or the sesame-honey mix) in the centre, and fold the dough up over it into the classic HALF-MOON, pinching the seam shut. The half-moon is the shape of the festival's meaning — a full moon is for the ancestors; the half-moon is for the living, a symbol of a better future to come.\n\nTHE PINE — THE SIGNATURE. Line the steamer with a layer of pine needles (rinsed), place the songpyeon on top, and steam over boiling water for about 20 minutes until glossy and cooked through. The pine perfumes the cakes — that gentle, resinous, unmistakably Korean aroma is the dish's true signature, and nothing else quite replaces it. (Food-safe parchment or cabbage leaves work when real pine is out of reach, honestly.)\n\nTHE FINISH — RINSE AND GLAZE. The traditional finish: rinse the steamed cakes briefly in cold water (this sets their shine and keeps them from sticking), drain, and brush each with a little sesame oil so they gleam. Serve warm — the chewy shell, the sweet-savoury centre, and that quiet pine note are the taste of the Korean autumn festival.","cookTime":"Prep 30 min · Steam 20 min","kcal":"~150 kcal each","nutrition":"Carbs 30g · Protein 3g · Fat 1g each — a gentle festival sweet","storage":"Best the day they are made — fresh-steamed, warm and chewy. Keeps 2-3 days refrigerated; re-steam gently 5 minutes to revive the chew (the fridge firms them). Wrap and freeze for a month — re-steam to serve.","chefNotes":"The four-part ritual: a SMOOTH pliable dough (not cracking — add a touch of hot water if it does), the generous half-moon SHAPE pinched shut (the seam is the craft), STEAMING over real pine needles for the signature perfume, and the RINSE-AND-SESAME finish for the gleam. The half-moon is for the living.","pairsWith":"A cup of Korean tea (or barley tea) · Other festival foods at Chuseok · A quiet moment making them with family","goesWith":["Patbingsu","Korean Fried Chicken"],"trivia":"Songpyeon derives its name from the pine tree — song means pine, and the cakes are steamed over pine needles, which is where they get their perfume. They are the definitive Chuseok food, made on the eve of the harvest festival when the family gathers to shape the half-moons by hand, and the folk belief that a beautifully made songpyeon brings a beautiful future is half the charm of the making. The half-moon shape itself is the symbol: full moons were set out in honour of ancestors, while the living are given the half-moon as a promise of the better year to come.","howThisFeels":"The warm half-moons gleaming with sesame, the first bite through the chewy shell into the sweet bean centre, the pine perfume rising — the taste of the Korean autumn harvest, made with laughter around the family table.","leftovers":["Re-steam the chilled cakes 5 minutes to bring back the chew — the proper revival.","Pan-fry leftover songpyeon in a little oil until golden for a crisp-edged festival sweet.","Freeze in a bag for a month; the pine perfume returns with a fresh steam."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Bean-Paste Songpyeon","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":16,"howThisFeels":"The festival original — chewy half-moons of rice dough filled with sweet bean paste, steamed over pine and finished with sesame.","trivia":"The classic is the sweet red-bean half-moon steamed over real pine needles — the shape, the perfume and the sesame gleam are the whole identity.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g rice flour · 75ml hot water · pinch of salt · 100g sweetened red bean paste · 5ml sesame oil · pine needles","method":["Mix the rice flour, salt and hot water into a smooth, pliable dough; rest 10 minutes.","Divide into 20g balls; flatten to discs; spoon a teaspoon of bean paste in each; fold into half-moons, pinching the seams.","Steam over rinsed pine needles for 20 minutes until glossy and cooked.","Rinse briefly in cold water, drain, and brush each with sesame oil until gleaming.","Serve warm with tea — the half-moon is for the living."]},{"name":"Sesame-Honey Songpyeon (The Savoury-Sweet Variation)","icon":"🍯","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":16,"howThisFeels":"The classic alternative filling — crushed toasted sesame mixed with honey and a pinch of salt, the savoury-sweet counter to the bean version.","trivia":"The sesame filling is the traditional northern variation — coarsely crushed sesame seeds sweetened with honey, its nutty saltiness against the plain rice dough being a beloved change from the bean.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"For the filling, coarsely crush 50g toasted sesame seeds and mix with 30g honey (or sugar syrup) and a pinch of salt. Use it exactly where the bean paste would sit. The nutty, gently savoury centre against the chewy white shell is the classic northern songpyeon — and it is every bit as beloved at the festival table."}]}}]},{"id":"korea-dubu-jjigae","name":"Dubu Jjigae","nameAlt":"Spicy tofu stew — the gentle pot with a fiery heart","aliases":["dubu jjigae","tofu stew","korean tofu stew","spicy tofu stew"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","tofu","spicy","vegetarian","one-pot","comfort"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian","omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","comfort","winter","vegetarian","lunch"],"mood":"Warming & gentle","time":"Prep 10 min · Cook 20 min","health":"A genuinely healthful weekday pot — soft tofu in a spicy, vegetable-bright broth, lower in fat than the meat stews and high in protein.","allergen":"Soy (tofu, gochujang, soy sauce) · sesame (sesame oil)","budget":"★★★ — tofu and pantry staples; one of the cheapest meals the Korean table offers","audience":"The everyday one-pot for everyone — and the go-to weeknight stew for the vegetarian eater, since the classic is already meat-free at its core.","season":"Winter and rainy days are its home — a bubbling pot that warms the hands and the mood","ingredients":"1 block soft or medium tofu (about 300g), in big slices · 400ml light stock (kelp or vegetable — the classic backing) · 15g gochugaru (Korean chilli flakes) · 10ml gochujang · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 30g onion, sliced · 50g zucchini, in half-moons · 3 fresh shiitake mushrooms, sliced · 1 spring onion, sliced · black pepper","method":"DUBU JJIGAE IS THE GENTLE MEMBER OF THE JJIGAE FAMILY — a bubbling pot of soft tofu in a spicy, savoury red broth, built on vegetables and the chilli bloom rather than meat. Like its cousin sundubu-jjigae, it arrives alive, the tofu trembling in the stone pot, and the eating is the warmth. THE METHOD IS THE BLOOM AND THE TOFU.\n\nFIRST, THE SPICE BLOOM — THE FIERY HEART. Heat the sesame oil in the pot, add the garlic and onion, and fry gently for a minute. Add the gochugaru and stir it into the oil for that crucial 30 seconds — the bloom that turns bare chilli flakes into a deep red, fragrant base. Stir in the gochujang and soy until the paste melts into the oil, glossy and red.\n\nTHE STOCK, THEN THE VEGETABLES. Pour in the stock and bring to a bubble. Add the zucchini and shiitake, and simmer 5 minutes until just tender — the vegetables stay bright, never cooked to grey. Taste and season; the gochujang is salty, so the pot needs almost nothing else.\n\nTHE TOFU — SLICED BIG, ADDED LAST, NEVER STIRRED. Slide the thick slices of soft tofu in, pushing the broth over rather than stirring the pot — the tofu stays intact, soaking the red broth from around it. Gently bubble 5 minutes more. The tofu is the point: it should be impossibly soft, still holding its slices, carrying the broth like a sponge.\n\nTHE FINISH — SPRING ONION OFF THE HEAT. Scatter the spring onion in and take the pot straight to the table, still bubbling if you have a stone pot. Serve with a bowl of steamed rice, and alternate a spoonful of the tofu-rich broth with a bite of plain rice — the Korean rhythm that lets the fire keep singing.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 20 min","kcal":"~320 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 22g · Carbs 16g · Fat 20g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Best the day it is made — the soft tofu is at its silkiest. Keeps 2 days in the fridge; reheat very gently with a splash of stock so the tofu does not break down. The tofu firms and weeps as it cools — add a fresh cube when reheating a batch.","chefNotes":"Three gentle laws: BLOOM the gochugaru in the oil for the deep red (the heart of every jjigae), ADD THE TOFU LAST and never stir it (soft tofu is the point), and SEASON THROUGH THE PASTES — the gochujang and soy are already salty, so taste before adding a grain of salt.","pairsWith":"A bowl of steamed rice (non-negotiable) · Fresh kimchi · A crisp cucumber side · Cold barley tea","goesWith":["Sundubu Jjigae","Doenjang Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Dubu-jjigae is the everyday tofu stew of the Korean home — the gentler sibling of sundubu-jjigae, differing mainly in the tofu: where sundubu uses the wobbling silken kind, dubu-jjigae traditionally uses slices of medium-soft tofu that hold their shape and soak the broth. It is the stew that proves the Korean jjigae family needs no meat to be beloved, and in Korea it is as common on the Tuesday-night table as on the vegetarian's weekday.","howThisFeels":"The bubbling pot of red broth with the soft white tofu slices floating like calm islands, the warm chilli scent, and that first spoonful of broth-soaked tofu against cool rice — a pot that feels like the kitchen's gentle answer to a cold day.","leftovers":["The leftover broth is the treasure — reheat and pour it over rice with a fresh spoon of sesame for an instant tofu rice bowl.","Add the leftover stew to a pan of fried rice with the extra gochugaru for a spicy tofu fried rice.","The broth alone freezes well for a month — a fresh block of tofu and 10 minutes rebuilds the whole pot."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Dubu Jjigae (Vegetarian)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"The gentle classic — soft tofu in a bloomed-chilli red broth with zucchini and shiitake, naturally meat-free and fully vegan.","trivia":"The classic is already plant-based at its core — tofu, vegetables and the chilli bloom, no meat required. In Korean homes it is simply the weekday stew.","delta":{},"ingredients":"300g soft tofu, sliced big · 400ml kelp or vegetable stock · 15g gochugaru · 10ml gochujang · 10ml soy · 5ml sesame oil · 2 cloves garlic · 30g onion · 50g zucchini · 3 shiitake · 1 spring onion · pepper","method":["Bloom the garlic, onion and gochugaru in sesame oil 30 seconds; stir in gochujang and soy.","Add the stock; bring to a bubble; add the zucchini and shiitake; simmer 5 minutes.","Slide in the big tofu slices; gently bubble 5 minutes without stirring.","Season to taste through the pastes; finish with spring onion off the heat.","Serve with steamed rice, alternating broth and plain rice."]},{"name":"Dubu Jjigae with Pork (The Home Rich Version)","icon":"🥩","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The same gentle pot with a few slices of pork belly singed into the bloom — the home version that adds a rich backbone to the red broth.","trivia":"Adding a few thin pork belly slices to dubu-jjigae is the common home enrichment — the pork's fat absorbs the chilli bloom exactly as it does in kimchi-jjigae, and the tofu carries the richer broth beautifully.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"40g pork belly, thinly sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add the thin pork slices to the bloom (after the garlic and onion, before the gochugaru) and let them brown for a minute — their fat soaks the chilli and becomes the broth's backbone. Everything else identical."}]}},{"name":"Quick — 15-Minute Dubu Jjigae","icon":"⚡","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":18,"howThisFeels":"Bottled stock and pre-cubed tofu shrink the gentle pot to a true quarter-hour — the weekday version that keeps the bloom and the never-stir tofu law.","trivia":"A good bottled vegetable stock and tofu straight from the packet make the classic nearly instant — the only laws that survive any shortcut are the chilli bloom and the tofu-added-last.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use a quality bottled kelp or vegetable stock, cube the tofu rather than slicing, and skip the aromatics' long sweat. Bloom the chilli, pour, simmer, slide in the tofu, off the heat — fifteen minutes, complete, and the red warmth intact."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-godeungeo-jorim","name":"Godeungeo Jorim","nameAlt":"Braised mackerel — the soy-glazed dinner stew of the Korean home","aliases":["godeungeo jorim","braised mackerel","korean braised fish","mackerel jorim"],"course":"main","type":["main","fish","braise","banchan-style","everyday","one-pot"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","family","weeknight","lunchbox"],"mood":"Savoury & homely","time":"Prep 15 min · Braise 25 min","health":"A genuinely good-for-you weekday dinner — omega-rich mackerel in a soy-garlic glaze with plenty of vegetables; the classic Korean home table main.","allergen":"Fish (mackerel) · soy (soy sauce) · sesame (sesame oil, seeds)","budget":"★★★ — fresh mackerel is among the most affordable fish; the glaze is pantry staples","audience":"The everyday home main — the braised-fish pot that anchors a Korean weeknight table, served with rice and several banchan.","season":"Year-round weeknight staple; at its best in autumn when the mackerel is fat and lovely","ingredients":"2 fresh mackerel fillets (about 250g total) · 30g Korean radish or daikon, in thick half-moons (the classic bed) · 40g onion, sliced · 30g carrot, in thick rounds · 3 cloves garlic, minced · 1 tsp ginger, grated · 15ml soy sauce · 10ml gochugaru (or a little gochujang) · 10ml mirin or cooking wine · 5ml sesame oil · 1 tsp sugar · 150ml water · 1 spring onion, in lengths · toasted sesame seeds","method":"GODEUNGEO JORIM IS THE BRAISED-FISH STEW OF THE KOREAN HOME — a shallow pot of mackerel simmered in a soy-garlic-chilli glaze until the sauce reduces to a glossy plate-licking coat and the fish is just-cooked-tender. It is the fish version of the home's beloved jorim (braised) family, served as the weeknight main beside rice and banchan. THE METHOD IS THE VEGETABLE BED AND THE REDUCTION.\n\nTHE BED — THE RADISH IS THE QUIET HERO. Lay thick half-moons of radish (or daikon) across the bottom of a shallow pot. The radish is not garnish — it absorbs the braising sauce and turns startlingly savoury, and it protects the fish from the direct pan. It is half the reason Koreans make this dish at all.\n\nTHE SAUCE — ONE BOWL, POURED OVER. Whisk the garlic, ginger, soy, gochugaru, mirin, sesame oil, sugar and water into the braising liquid. Pour it over the radish bed, add the onion and carrot, and bring to a gentle bubble.\n\nTHE FISH GOES IN ON TOP — AND THE LID DOES THE WORK. Lay the mackerel fillets on top (not stirred into the sauce), cover, and braise gently 15 minutes. Then uncover, and spoon the reducing sauce over the fish a few times for the last 5-10 minutes, until the liquid has reduced to a glossy, savoury glaze that clings to both fish and radish.\n\nTHE FINISH — SPRING ONION AND SESAME, STRAIGHT FROM THE POT. Scatter the spring onion lengths and sesame seeds over, and serve the pot at the table with steamed rice — the glaze is made for spooning over grain. Eat the radish like the prize it is: it has soaked up everything.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Braise 25 min","kcal":"~380 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 32g · Carbs 18g · Fat 20g · Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"The braise keeps 3 days in the fridge and genuinely deepens — the sauce soaks further into the radish and the fish stays moist. Reheat very gently; serve with fresh rice. The radish is famously better on day two.","chefNotes":"Three rules: the RADISH BED is the quiet hero (it absorbs the glaze and protects the fish), the fish sits ON TOP and is never turned in the sauce, and the UNCOVERED REDUCTION for the last minutes is what turns broth into glossy glaze. The radish at the end, soaked and savoury, is the best bite.","pairsWith":"Steamed rice (non-negotiable) · A fresh salad or cucumber side · Kimchi · The other banchan of the table","goesWith":["Bibimbap","Kimchi Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Godeungeo jorim is a pillar of the Korean home dinner table — the braised mackerel that appears as regularly as rice, cooked in the soy-garlic-chilli glaze (jorim) that the Korean kitchen uses for so many fish and vegetables. The radish or daikon bed is the traditional touch: in the autumn, when the radish is at its sweetest, Koreans say the braised radish from the mackerel pot is the dish's best-kept secret. It is one of those honest, homely, deeply beloved dishes that travellers rarely see on restaurant menus — because it belongs to the family table.","howThisFeels":"The glossy braised mackerel glistening in its reduced sauce, the radish soaked dark and savoury, and the smell of soy, garlic and sesame filling the kitchen — the homely pot that makes a weeknight feel like a proper meal.","leftovers":["The day-two radish is the legend — it has soaked the whole glaze and is the most sought-after spoonful in the pot.","Shred leftover mackerel (careful of small bones) over rice with a spoon of the glaze and a fried egg.","The reduced sauce alone is a gift — spoon it over vegetables or rice for an instant Korean bowl."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Mackerel Jorim","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The home classic — mackerel braised over a radish bed in the soy-garlic-chilli glaze, reduced to gloss, finished with spring onion and sesame.","trivia":"The classic is the family-table version with the radish bed — the autumn radish that soaks up the glaze is half the reason the dish exists.","delta":{},"ingredients":"2 mackerel fillets · 30g radish, thick half-moons · 40g onion · 30g carrot · 3 cloves garlic · 1 tsp ginger · 15ml soy · 10ml gochugaru · 10ml mirin · 5ml sesame oil · 1 tsp sugar · 150ml water · 1 spring onion · sesame seeds","method":["Lay the radish half-moons across a shallow pot.","Whisk garlic, ginger, soy, gochugaru, mirin, sesame, sugar and water; pour over; add onion and carrot; bubble gently.","Lay the mackerel fillets on top; cover and braise 15 minutes.","Uncover; spoon the reducing sauce over 5-10 minutes until glossy and clinging.","Finish with spring onion and sesame; serve from the pot with rice."]},{"name":"Budget — Mackerel Fillet from Frozen","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The same glossy braise with thawed frozen mackerel — an honest, affordable weeknight that loses nothing to the fresh version.","trivia":"Frozen mackerel fillets are excellent — mackerel freezes well, and the braise's strong glaze does the rest. Thaw gently and pat dry before it meets the pot.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Thaw frozen mackerel fillets overnight in the fridge (or an hour in cold water), pat dry, and proceed exactly as the classic. Frozen fillets are often already boned, which makes the eating even easier — the braise and the radish bed are unchanged."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-nakji-bokkeum","name":"Nakji-bokkeum","nameAlt":"Spicy stir-fried octopus — the searing pan favourite of Korea","aliases":["nakji bokkeum","spicy octopus stir fry","korean octopus","nakji"],"course":"main","type":["main","seafood","octopus","stir-fry","spicy","quick"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","weeknight","quick","drinking-food"],"mood":"Fiery & fast","time":"Prep 15 min · Cook 8 min","health":"Lean, iron-rich seafood in the spicy-sweet glaze — a light and quick main, best balanced with the rice bowl and a fresh vegetable side.","allergen":"Seafood (octopus) · soy (soy sauce, gochujang) · sesame (sesame oil, seeds)","budget":"★★☆ — octopus is mid-price; the sauce is pantry and the vegetables stretch it","audience":"The fast weeknight pan for confident quick-sear cooks — and a beloved anju (drinking snack) ordered with beer and soju.","season":"Year-round and fast — the quick hungry night, whenever it strikes","ingredients":"200g baby octopus or small octopus, cleaned · 20g gochujang · 10g gochugaru · 10ml soy sauce · 10ml honey or sugar · 5ml sesame oil · 1 tbsp minced garlic · 1 tsp grated ginger · 30g onion, sliced · 30g carrot, in matchsticks · 1 green chilli, sliced (optional) · 2 spring onions, in 4cm lengths · sesame seeds · oil for the pan","method":"NAKJI-BOKKEUM IS THE OCTOPUS COUSIN OF OJINGEO-BOKKEUM — the same scorching fast pan, the same red glaze, the fiercer creature. It is one of the most loved quick seafood dishes of the Korean table, ordered both as a weeknight main and as anju with soju. THE LAW IS THE SAME: OCTOPUS COOKS IN MINUTES, OR IT COOKS FOREVER.\n\nTENDERISE FIRST — THE KOREAN SECRET. The single most important step: tenderise the cleaned octopus before it ever hits the pan. The traditional method is to rub it with sliced radish (or coarse salt) for a couple of minutes — the enzymes and the abrasion begin breaking down the tough fibres — then rinse. Even a quick 2-minute rub with coarse salt makes the difference between chewy-tender and rubber. Cut the body into bite pieces and keep the tentacles whole or halved.\n\nTHE SAUCE READY, THE PAN SEARING. Whisk the gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, sesame oil, garlic and ginger into a red paste, ready beside the stove. Heat the pan hard with oil, toss the onion and carrot 1 minute, then add the octopus. Let it sear untouched for 60-90 seconds until it begins to curl, then toss — the brief, violent heat is all it needs.\n\nTHE GLAZE LAST, OFF THE HEAT. Spoon the sauce over, toss once to coat everything in the red glaze, add the spring onion and chilli, and take the pan straight off the heat. The residual heat finishes the glaze without a second wasted. First bite of a properly done nakji-bokkeum: tender, red, sweet-spicy, with a bounce that is the whole point.\n\nTHE RICE AND THE GLASS. Serve immediately with rice — and for the classic finish, some Koreans break cold rice into the leftover red pan, toss it in the glaze, and eat it as the scarlet final act. That pan-rice move is the anju table's secret last course.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 8 min","kcal":"~360 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 31g · Carbs 22g · Fat 10g · Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"Best the second it leaves the pan — octopus waits for no one. Leftovers keep 1 day in the fridge, reheated in a flash over high heat, but they are a shadow of the fresh bowl; the honest day-two move is the pan-rice rescue below.","chefNotes":"The three rules: TENDERISE the octopus first (the radish or coarse-salt rub is the whole texture), sear it hard for only 60-90 seconds (rubber is the one failure), and GLAZE LAST then off the heat. The pan-rice finish in the leftover red sauce is the anju secret worth stealing.","pairsWith":"Steamed rice (non-negotiable) · Cold beer or soju (the anju pairing) · Kimchi · A cool cucumber side between the fire","goesWith":["Ojingeo Bokkeum","Kimchi Fried Rice","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Nakji-bokkeum is the fiercer sibling of the squid stir-fry — the same pan, the same red glaze, the octopus's bolder chew. It is one of Korea's most-loved quick seafood dishes, ordered as much for the soju table as for dinner, and the pan-rice finish — cold rice tossed in the leftover scarlet glaze — is a beloved anju-table move that turns the end of the dish into a secret final course. The tenderising radish rub is the traditional craft: a couple of minutes of working the octopus with sliced radish is what makes it silky rather than rubber.","howThisFeels":"The octopus curling and reddening in the searing pan, the whole kitchen full of chilli and garlic, and the first chewy-sweet-spicy bite mopped through rice — then the pan-rice in the leftover scarlet, the anju table's finest secret.","leftovers":["The classic rescue: toss cold rice and the extra red glaze into the leftover pan for scarlet pan-rice — the anju table's beloved final act.","Chop leftover octopus small and stir it through fried rice with the glaze.","A spoon of the leftover glaze alone glazes any quick vegetable stir-fry the next night."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Nakji-bokkeum","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42,"howThisFeels":"The genuine article — tenderised octopus, 90-second sear, one-coat red glaze, the pan-rice secret at the end.","trivia":"The classic is tenderised baby octopus in the gochujang glaze, seared fast and finished with the pan-rice move — the anju classic in full.","delta":{},"ingredients":"200g baby octopus, cleaned · 20g gochujang · 10g gochugaru · 10ml soy · 10ml honey · 5ml sesame oil · 1 tbsp garlic · 1 tsp ginger · 30g onion · 30g carrot · 1 green chilli · 2 spring onions · sesame seeds · oil","method":["Tenderise the cleaned octopus 2 minutes with coarse salt or sliced radish; rinse; cut up.","Whisk the gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, sesame, garlic and ginger into a paste.","Stir-fry onion and carrot 1 minute in a searing pan; add the octopus; sear 60-90 seconds until it curls.","Glaze, toss once, add spring onion and chilli, OFF the heat.","Finish with sesame; serve with rice; toss the leftover red pan with cold rice for the final payback."]},{"name":"Squid & Prawn Nakji-Style (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The same searing pan and red glaze, with squid rings and prawns in place of the pricier octopus — all the fire, gentler on the purse.","trivia":"Squid and prawns share the octopus's cooking law and catch the same glaze — a legitimate budget family fork that is just as beloved at home.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use 100g squid rings and 100g peeled prawns in place of the octopus (squid is often more affordable). Skip the tenderising rub — squid and prawns are naturally tender; sear them together 60-90 seconds exactly as the classic, glaze last, off the heat. The pan-rice finish still works its magic in the leftover red."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-beoseot-jeon","name":"Beoseot Jeon","nameAlt":"Mushroom pancakes — the savoury jewels of the jeon family","aliases":["beoseot jeon","mushroom pancakes","korean mushroom pancake","vegetable jeon"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","pancake","mushroom","jeon","vegetarian","side"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["party","everyday","banchan","side","festive"],"mood":"Crisp & savoury","time":"Prep 15 min · Cook 10 min","health":"A light, mostly-vegetable savoury pancake — mushrooms and a whisper of batter, pan-fried crisp; the healthiest member of the jeon family.","allergen":"Gluten (wheat flour in the batter) · soy (in the dipping sauce) · mushroom (an allergen for a few)","budget":"★★★ — mushrooms and flour; the cheapest, prettiest starter on the Korean table","audience":"The party starter and banquet side — the jeon platter is made for sharing, and mushroom jeon is its elegant, vegetarian-friendly jewel.","season":"Party season and every festive table; jeon are the classic accompaniment to festivals and family gatherings","ingredients":"150g assorted mushrooms (shiitake, oyster, button) · 50g cake flour (for dredging) · 60g pancake batter: 50g cake flour + 1 egg + 80ml cold water + a pinch of salt (use 60ml cold water + no egg for vegan) · 5ml sesame oil · oil for frying · dipping sauce: 15ml soy + 10ml vinegar + a pinch of chilli flakes + a drop of sesame oil","method":"BEOSEOT JEON ARE THE SAVOURY JEWELS OF THE JEON FAMILY — the Korean savoury-pancake platter that graces every festive table. Thin, beautifully scored mushrooms in a whisper of egg-flour batter, pan-fried until golden-crisp at the edges, served with the sharp soy-vinegar dip. The jeon platter is one of the great sharing spreads of Korean cooking, and these mushroom pancakes are its light, vegetarian-friendly heart. THE METHOD IS THE SHAPE AND THE CRISP.\n\nTHE MUSHROOMS ARE SCORED — THE TRICK THAT MAKES THEM LIE FLAT. Wipe the mushrooms clean (never water them) and slice the caps thick. Score the cut faces in a light crosshatch — the marks let the batter grip and stop the mushroom curling up as it fries, so the pancake stays flat and even. This scoring is the professional jeon move.\n\nTHE DREDGE AND THE DIP — THE TWO-STEP BITE. Dust each mushroom slice in the flour (dredging) — this dry coat is what makes the batter cling. Then dip the floured slice into the thin batter, coat completely, and lay it straight into a hot, barely-oiled pan.\n\nTHE PAN — MEDIUM HEAT, GOLDEN EDGES, NO CROWDING. Fry the battered mushrooms over medium heat, a single layer at a time, 2-3 minutes per side, until the edges are golden and lacy-crisp. Do not crowd the pan — the jeon needs room to crisp, not steam. Drain briefly on a rack (not paper, which steams them) and keep warm.\n\nTHE DIP — THE SHARP COUNTER. Whisk the soy, vinegar, chilli flakes and a drop of sesame oil into the sharp, bright dip. Serve the mushrooms piled on a sharing plate with the dip, eaten hot — the crisp, savoury mushroom against the sharp glossy dip is the whole joy of the jeon platter.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 10 min","kcal":"~220 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 8g · Carbs 26g · Fat 10g · Sodium ~500mg","storage":"Best eaten the moment they leave the pan — jeon crispness is the entire point. Leftovers keep a day in the fridge and re-crisp in a hot dry pan (never the microwave); they are also a beloved addition to a make-ahead plate.","chefNotes":"Three rules: SCORE the mushroom caps (the marks make the batter grip and stop the curling), DREDGE in flour before the batter whisper (the dry coat makes the batter cling), and fry MEDIUM in a single layer for lacy golden edges. The sharp dip is non-negotiable — it cuts the savoury richness like a key.","pairsWith":"The soy-vinegar-chilli dip · Rest of the jeon platter (kimchi or squash jeon) · Cold beer · A bowl of rice on the side","goesWith":["Kimchi Jeon","Kimchi Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Jeon are the savoury-pancake family that owns the Korean festive table — hundreds of varieties, from squash to seafood, pan-fried in a whisper of batter and piled on sharing platters. Beoseot jeon, the mushroom version, is the vegetarian jewel of the spread, often included in the platter precisely so the vegetable-eaters have their own golden pile. The light egg-flour batter and the crisp edge are the family fingerprint, and the sharp soy-vinegar dip is the constant companion that cuts the richness.","howThisFeels":"The golden, lacy-edged mushroom coins piled warm on the sharing plate, the first crisp bite giving way to the savoury mushroom, the sharp dip pulling it all together — the elegant jewel of a table made for passing around.","leftovers":["Re-crisp leftover jeon in a hot dry pan and tuck them into a gimbap — the jeon-gimbap is a beloved way to use the pancake platter.","Chop cold jeon over a bowl of rice with a fried egg for a fast jeon-bap lunch.","They join the make-ahead plate proudly — cold jeon with the dip is a classic Korean picnic and banquet offering."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Beoseot Jeon","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":18,"howThisFeels":"The elegant classic — scored mushrooms in the thin egg batter, pan-fried gold, served with the sharp dip.","trivia":"The classic uses a whisper of egg-and-flour batter and the scored-flat mushroom trick — the jeon-platter jewel.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g assorted mushrooms · 50g cake flour (dredging) · batter: 50g flour + 1 egg + 80ml water + salt · 5ml sesame oil · frying oil · dip: soy + vinegar + chilli + sesame","method":["Wipe and thick-cut the mushrooms; score the cut faces in a light crosshatch.","Dredge each slice in flour, then dip in the thin batter.","Fry over medium heat in a single layer, 2-3 minutes per side, until golden-lacy.","Drain on a rack and keep warm; make the sharp soy-vinegar-chilli dip.","Serve hot with the dip — crisp, savoury, eaten while the edges sing."]},{"name":"Vegan Beoseot Jeon (No-Egg Batter)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":14,"howThisFeels":"The same golden mushroom jewels with a simple flour-and-water batter — no egg, all crisp, and the mushroom carries it beautifully.","trivia":"A plain flour-cold-water batter (a whisper of cornflour helps the crisp) fries just as golden — the mushroom's own savour does the work, and the dip stays fully plant-based.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use 50g flour + 60ml cold water (plus a teaspoon of cornflour for extra crisp) for the batter, skipping the egg entirely. Everything else — the scoring, the dredge, the medium pan — is identical, and the dip (soy, vinegar, chilli, sesame) is naturally vegan. The crisp, savoury result is every bit the platter jewel."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-kimchi-jeon","name":"Kimchi Jeon","nameAlt":"Kimchi pancake — the batter brick of Friday-night comfort","aliases":["kimchi jeon","kimchi pancake","kimchijeon","kimchi buchimgae"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","pancake","kimchi","quick","comfort","snack"],"diet":["vegetarian","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","quick","snack","weeknight","rainy-day"],"mood":"Quick & tangy","time":"Prep 10 min · Cook 10 min","health":"A comfort snack, honestly stated — the batter and oil make it rich, but the kimchi's ferment and fibre keep it more interesting than a plain pancake.","allergen":"Gluten (wheat flour) · soy (in the kimchi brine and the dip) · sesame (sesame oil) · shellfish (fish sauce in some kimchi — check)","budget":"★★★ — old kimchi and flour; among the cheapest comforts in the Korean kitchen","audience":"The Friday-night quick — the pancake that rescues the night the fridge offers only old kimchi and a craving. It is Korea's answer to the stir-fry-what-you-have instinct.","season":"Rainy days and every quick hungry night — the sizzle is the sound of comfort","ingredients":"100g OLD kimchi, chopped (the riper, the better) · 70g cake flour · 20g cornflour · 100ml cold water (or the kimchi brine topped up with water for extra tang) · 1 spring onion, sliced · 5ml sesame oil · a pinch of sugar · oil for frying · dipping sauce: 15ml soy + 10ml vinegar + a pinch of chilli + a drop of sesame","method":"KIMCHI JEON IS THE ONLY PANCAKE THAT LOVES ITS INGREDIENTS FOR BEING OLD — the beloved batter pancake built on ripe, sour kimchi, pan-fried crisp-edged and eaten hot from the pan. Where the pajeon is the spring-onion raft, kimchi jeon is the batter brick: chopped kimchi folded THROUGH the batter, every bite tangy and red. THE METHOD IS THE BRINE AND THE CRISP.\n\nTHE KIMCHI — CHOPPED, SQUEEZED, AND THE JUICE SAVED. Chop the old kimchi small and squeeze it gently — the ferment is rich in flavour and bright red juice. SAVE the squeezed juice: it goes into the batter for the colour and the extra tang. The older and riper the kimchi, the better the pancake — fresh kimchi is too bright and crunchy; old kimchi is tangy, mellow and perfect.\n\nTHE BATTER — THIN, AND THE TANG INSIDE. Whisk the flour, cornflour, half the kimchi juice topped up with cold water (about 100ml total), the sugar and a pinch of salt into a loose batter — thinner than you think, about double-cream consistency. Fold in the chopped kimchi and the spring onion. The batter should be thin enough that the pancake fries crisp rather than heavy.\n\nTHE PAN — A HOT SLICK, AND PATIENCE FOR THE EDGE. Heat a generous slick of oil in a pan over medium-high. Ladle in the batter in one big round (or two small ones), spread it even, and let it cook UNTOUCHED until the edges turn golden and lacy and the whole pancake releases easily — the crisp edge is the reward, and flipping too early tears it. Flip once and cook until the second side is golden.\n\nTHE DIP AND THE HOT EAT. Slice the pancake into wedges and serve immediately with the sharp soy-vinegar dip — hot, crisp-edged, tangy-red from the old kimchi. This is Korea's Friday-night comfort: the pan, the sizzle, and the impossible urge to eat the first wedge straight off the cutting board.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 10 min","kcal":"~380 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 8g · Carbs 56g · Fat 14g · Sodium ~1000mg","storage":"Best straight from the pan, hot and crisp. Leftovers keep a day in the fridge and re-crisp in a hot dry pan (never the microwave). They are also a beloved leftover to slice into a gimbap the next day.","chefNotes":"Three rules: USE OLD KIMCHI and save its squeezed juice for the batter (the ripeness is the tang), keep the BATTER THIN so it fries crisp not heavy, and PATIENCE at the pan — wait for the golden lacy edge before the single flip. The sharp dip is the constant companion.","pairsWith":"The soy-vinegar dip · Cold beer · A bowl of rice to round it into a meal · More old kimchi, even on top","goesWith":["Beoseot Jeon","Kimchi Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Kimchi jeon is the only pancake that genuinely wants its ingredients to be past their best — the old, ripe kimchi that is too sour for the banchan table is exactly what makes the pancake sing. It is Korea's purest quick comfort: flour, old kimchi, a hot pan and five minutes, the tangy-red sibling of the pajeon and the answer to every rainy day and Friday-night craving.","howThisFeels":"The tangy-red pancake sizzling crisp in the pan, the first wedge torn off hot with the sharp dip — the instant comfort of old kimchi turned golden, the taste of the quickest happy hour in the Korean kitchen.","leftovers":["Slice leftover kimchi jeon and roll it into a gimbap with rice and sesame — the jeon-gimbap, a beloved next-day move.","Chop cold jeon over a bowl of hot rice with a fried egg for a fast kimchi-jeon-bap.","Re-crisp in a hot dry pan and serve with the dip again — it is still the same happy wedge."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Kimchi Jeon","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":12,"howThisFeels":"The classic tangy-red pancake — old kimchi folded through a thin batter, fried crisp-edged, the sharp dip beside.","trivia":"The classic is the purest form: old kimchi plus its saved brine in a thin batter, crisp-edged from the hot pan. It is the cheapest comfort in the Korean kitchen.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g old kimchi, chopped · 70g flour · 20g cornflour · 100ml cold water + kimchi juice · 1 spring onion · 5ml sesame oil · pinch of sugar · frying oil · soy-vinegar-chilli dip","method":["Chop the old kimchi small; squeeze and save the juice.","Whisk flour, cornflour, the juice-and-water (100ml total), sugar and salt into a thin batter; fold in the kimchi and spring onion.","Fry in a hot oiled pan over medium-high, untouched until the edges turn golden-lacy; flip once.","Slice into wedges; serve hot with the sharp soy-vinegar dip."]},{"name":"Cheese Kimchi Jeon (The Modern Favourite)","icon":"🧀","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":16,"howThisFeels":"The same tangy pancake with a molten cheese heart — the modern Korea favouriting that takes the comfort up a notch.","trivia":"A scatter of mozzarella (or a square of cheese) pressed into the batter before the flip is the modern Seoul addition — molten cheese against the tangy kimchi is the same pairing that made cheese tteokbokki a hit.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"25g mozzarella, grated"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Ladle the batter, and once the first side has set (about 1 minute), scatter the grated mozzarella over the top before the single flip. Press the flip gently so the cheese melts between the two layers — the result is a molten-cheese kimchi pancake, the modern favourite. Use a vegan cheese alternative or skip it for the fully vegan version."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-andong-jjimdak","name":"Andong Jjimdak","nameAlt":"Braised chicken with glass noodles — the one-pot feast born in Andong's market","aliases":["andong jjimdak","jjimdak","braised chicken korea","korean soy braised chicken"],"course":"main","type":["main","chicken","braise","one-pot","spicy","sweet-savoury"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","family","sharing","comfort"],"mood":"Bold & communal","time":"Prep 20 min · Cook 45 min","health":"A rich, moreish one-pot — chicken braised in a soy-sweet-spicy glaze with noodles and vegetables; the noodle part makes it a complete meal in one dish.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce) · gluten (soy sauce, wheat noodles in some versions) · sesame (sesame oil, seeds) · chilli","budget":"★★★ — chicken pieces and vegetables; one of the great crowd-feeding one-pots of the Korean table","audience":"The sharing feast — a single deep dish at the centre of the table that everyone reaches into, with the glossy noodles as the prize at the bottom.","season":"Year-round comfort — at its best on cold, hungry nights when one pot is the whole answer","ingredients":"400g chicken pieces (thighs and drumsticks — bone-in keeps it rich) · 40g dangmyeon (Korean sweet potato glass noodles), soaked · 30ml soy sauce · 15ml mirin or cooking wine · 10g gochugaru (or a little gochujang for deeper heat) · 1 tbsp brown sugar or honey · 4 cloves garlic, minced · 1 tsp ginger, grated · 1 potato, in thick wedges · 1 carrot, in thick rounds · 1 onion, in thick petals · 400ml water or stock · 2 spring onions, in lengths · 5ml sesame oil · toasted sesame seeds","method":"ANDONG JJIMDAK IS THE ONE-POT FEAST BORN FROM A MARKET MYTH — a deep dish of chicken braised low in a glossy soy-sweet-spicy glaze, with vegetables and a raft of glass noodles soaking at the bottom. It is named for the city of Andong, and its origin story is part of the dish: in the 1980s a restaurant there, legend says, braised an entire chicken in a rich soy stock to feed throngs of market visitors, and the crowded pot became a word-of-mouth sensation that swept the nation. THE METHOD IS THE GLAZE AND THE NOODLES.\n\n⚖️ FIRST, THE CHICKEN — BLANCHED FOR THE CLEAN RICH. Put the chicken pieces in a pot of cold water, bring to a boil, and blanch 3-4 minutes; drain and rinse. This removes the scum and leaves the braise CLEAN and glossy rather than murky — the same discipline as the galbi-jjim soak. Rinse the pot clean.\n\n⚖️ THE GLAZE — ONE POT, EVERYTHING IN GOOD ORDER. Return the chicken to the clean pot with the water (or stock), soy, mirin, gochugaru, sugar, garlic and ginger. Bring to a bubble, then lower to a steady simmer for 15 minutes — the chicken begins to drink the glaze.\n\n⚖️ THE VEGETABLES, STAGED. Add the potato, carrot and onion, and simmer 10 minutes more, until everything is tender but not collapsing. The staging matters: the chicken needs the head start, the vegetables need their own 10 minutes, and everyone arrives at the finish together.\n\n⚖️ THE NOODLES — THE PRIZE AT THE BOTTOM. Add the soaked glass noodles and simmer 5-7 minutes until they are translucent, glossy and have drunk the glaze. This is the moment the dish becomes Andong jjimdak — the noodles at the bottom, gleaming red-brown and impossible to resist. Taste and adjust the balance of sweet, salty and heat.\n\n⚖️ THE FINISH — REDUCE TO GLOSSY, OFF THE HEAT. Take the lid off and let the glaze reduce a few minutes until it coats the chicken and noodles like syrup. Scatter the spring onion, sesame oil and toasted sesame seeds over, and carry the whole pot to the table. Everyone reaches in — chicken, vegetables, and the great struggle for the last glossy noodles.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 45 min","kcal":"~560 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g · Carbs 48g · Fat 22g · Sodium ~1300mg","storage":"Famously better on day two — the chicken drinks the glaze and the noodles soak deeper. Keeps 3 days in the fridge, reheated gently with a splash of water. The noodles absorb as they sit, so add a fresh soaked handful when reheating for the proper texture.","chefNotes":"Three rules: BLANCH the chicken first (the clean glossy glaze is the whole look), STAGE the vegetables (chicken 15 minutes, then the roots), and ADD THE NOODLES LAST so they drink the glaze without going mushy. The balance — sweet, salty, a thread of heat — is the Andong signature; taste and adjust before the reduction.","pairsWith":"Steamed rice (to catch every last drop) · A sharp pickled radish or kimchi · Cold beer","goesWith":["Gamjatang","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Andong jjimdak's origin is one of the great food legends of Korea: in the 1980s, a restaurant in Andong — the city famous for its markets — began braising whole chickens in a rich soy broth to feed the crowds of visitors, and the one-pot with glass noodles at the bottom became so beloved it spread across the country as its own dish, named for the city that birthed it. It remains a signature of Andong and one of Korea's great sharing-table one-pots.","howThisFeels":"The deep pot arriving glossy and steaming, everyone leaning in at once — the first bite of chicken that has drunk the sweet-savoury glaze, then the hunt for the glass noodles at the bottom, which everyone knew were the real prize all along.","leftovers":["The day-two pot is the classic — reheat gently with a splash of water, add a fresh handful of soaked noodles, and the dish is reborn.","Shred leftover chicken off the bone over rice with a spoon of the glaze and a fried egg — the jjimdak rice bowl.","The leftover glaze alone is gold — spoon it over any quick stir-fry of vegetables the next night."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Andong Jjimdak","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":38,"howThisFeels":"The full one-pot — braised chicken, staged vegetables and the glossy glass noodles at the bottom, reduced to a sweet-savoury lacquer.","trivia":"The classic is the market-born original — whole chicken pieces braised in the soy glaze with potato, carrot and glass noodles, the one-pot of Andong legend.","delta":{},"ingredients":"400g chicken pieces · 40g glass noodles, soaked · 30ml soy · 15ml mirin · 10g gochugaru · 1 tbsp sugar · 4 cloves garlic · 1 tsp ginger · 1 potato · 1 carrot · 1 onion · 400ml water · 2 spring onions · sesame oil · sesame seeds","method":["Blanch the chicken in cold water 3-4 minutes; drain and rinse; clean the pot.","Return the chicken with water, soy, mirin, gochugaru, sugar, garlic and ginger; simmer 15 minutes.","Add the potato, carrot and onion; simmer 10 minutes until tender.","Add the soaked noodles; simmer 5-7 minutes until glossy and glazed.","Reduce uncovered to a lacquer; finish with spring onion and sesame; serve the whole pot at the table."]},{"name":"Budget — More Vegetables, Glossier Noodles","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The same glossy one-pot with extra vegetables and double the noodles — the broth's biggest fan becomes the whole party staying hungry.","trivia":"Doubling the vegetables and the noodles is the classic home economy — the glaze stretches to feed a bigger table and the noodles remain the prize.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Add an extra potato, a zucchini in thick rounds and double the glass noodles (80g). Stage them with the other roots, and the extra noodles will drink up the abundant glaze. The pot feeds another two hungry guests for pennies."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-gamjatang","name":"Gamjatang","nameAlt":"Pork bone & potato stew — the spicy late-night feast","aliases":["gamjatang","pork bone stew","korean pork bone soup","gamja tang"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","pork","bones","spicy","late-night","one-pot"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["late-night","comfort","winter","drinking-food","sharing"],"mood":"Robust & restorative","time":"Prep 20 min · Simmer 1 h 30 min–2 h (pressure cooker 40 min)","health":"A genuinely restorative bone stew — the long simmer draws collagen from the pork bones, and the potato and greens round it into a heavy, honest meal for a cold night.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce, gochujang) · sesame (sesame oil, seeds) · perilla (the signature garnish — see note) · chilli","budget":"★★★ — pork neck bones are the cheapest beef-adjacent buy at the butcher; the rest is pantry, making this the great budget-feast of the Korean soup table","audience":"The late-night institution — the stew that anchors the drinking-table and the cold-night table alike, eaten with the hands holding a bone and a bowl of rice within reach.","season":"Winter and late nights are its home — it is the pot requested when the weather and the hour both demand something fierce and warming","ingredients":"400g pork neck bones (or pork spine — the classic cut; ask your butcher) · 2 medium potatoes, in thick wedges · 100g cabbage, in squares · ½ onion, sliced · 20g gochugaru · 10ml gochujang · 15ml soy sauce · 2 tbsp minced garlic · 1 tbsp Korean perilla seed powder (deulggae — the signature; substitute toasted sesame seed powder honestly) · 1 tsp ginger, grated · 4 shiitake or a little kimchi (optional depth) · 2 spring onions, in lengths · 5ml sesame oil · black pepper","method":"GAMJATANG IS THE LATE-NIGHT PORK BONE STEW OF KOREA — a fierce red pot of pork neck bones simmered until the meat falls off, with potatoes, cabbage and the signature swirl of perilla seed powder. Its name means potato soup (gamja + tang), a friendly disguise for a pot that is really about the bones. It is the stew that anchors the drinking table and the coldest nights, eaten with chopsticks in one hand and a bone in the other.\n\n⚖️ THE BONES — THE SOAK AND THE BLANCH ARE THE WHOLE CLEAN START. Soak the pork neck bones in cold water 30 minutes to draw out the blood (the same discipline as galbi-jjim), then drain. Put them in a pot of cold water, bring to a boil, and blanch 5 minutes, skimming the scum — drain and rinse the bones clean. A clean bone start is what lets the broth turn rich and golden rather than murky.\n\n⚖️ THE LONG SIMMER — THE BROTH IS THE BONE. Return the bones to a clean pot with fresh water to cover, add the garlic, ginger and a splash of soy, and bring to a gentle simmer. Cook LOW for 1 hour 15 minutes to 1 hour 30 minutes, skimming occasionally, until the meat is beginning to fall from the bones and the broth has gone pale and rich. (A pressure cooker does the same in 35 minutes — see the Quick version.)\n\n⚖️ THE RED LAYER — BLOOM, THEN THE STEW COMES TOGETHER. In a small pan, bloom the gochugaru and gochujang in a little oil for 30 seconds until fragrant and deep red — this gives the dust-red stew its colour and body. Add the bloom to the pot along with the potatoes, cabbage, onion and the perilla seed powder. Simmer 20-25 minutes until the potatoes are completely soft and the broth has turned red and glossy.\n\n⚖️ THE PERILLA — THE SIGNATURE THAT MAKES IT GAMJATANG. The swirl of Korean perilla seed powder (deulggae) stirred into the broth — or scattered on top — is what gives gamjatang its distinctive nutty, earthy depth. It is the quiet marker of the dish. Toasted sesame seed powder is the honest global stand-in; the nuttiness is the point.\n\n⚖️ THE EATING — HANDS, RICE, AND THE FINISH. Serve the pot at the table with the spring onions and sesame oil scattered over. The ritual: hold a bone in the chopsticks or a hand, pull off the falling meat, dip it in the seasoning, then the potato, the cabbage, the broth. When the pot is nearly empty, toss in a bowl of rice and let it soak up the red — the gamjatang finish that wastes nothing.\n\nNUTRITION NOTE ON THE SIGNATURE: perilla is the green herb of the East Asian garden (the leaf is kkaennip, the seed is deulggae) — it is the nutty, slightly minty seed powder that defines this stew's finish, and it is worth seeking at an Asian grocer.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Simmer 1 h 30 min–2 h (pressure cooker 40 min)","kcal":"~620 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g · Carbs 40g · Fat 32g · Sodium ~1400mg","storage":"Gamjatang is famously at its best on day two — the bones have simmered their depth in and the broth thickens richly overnight. Keeps 3 days in the fridge (lift the yellowed fat off the top before reheating) and freezes well for a month, potatoes and all.","chefNotes":"Four rules: SOAK and BLANCH the bones (the clean golden broth is the whole look), simmer LOW and long until the meat falls from the bone, BLOOM the gochugaru for the deep red, and NEVER skip the perilla seed powder — it is the signature that makes the pot gamjatang rather than just pork bone soup.","pairsWith":"A bowl of steamed rice · Cold beer or soju (the late-night companion) · A fresh kimchi or radish side between the fire","goesWith":["Andong Jjimdak","Dwaeji Gukbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Gamjatang's friendly name hides its honest character — it means potato soup, named for the potatoes that soften into the red broth, though the pot's true heart is the pork bones themselves. It belongs to the tradition of Korean pork bone soups, simmered long and eaten late, and in Korea it is one of the great late-night and drinking-table institutions — a pot that asks for time, returns warmth, and finishes with the classic move of rice soaked into the last red spoonfuls.","howThisFeels":"The fierce red pot arriving steaming, the first pull of a bone shedding its tender meat, the nutty swirl of perilla in the warm broth — the heavy, restorative comfort of a late-night Korean table.","leftovers":["The day-two pot is the legend — the broth thickens richly, and it is the finest leftover in the Korean soup family.","Toss cold rice into the leftover red broth and simmer 2 minutes for a gamjatang congee finish.","Pull the remaining meat from the bones, shred it, and freeze with a cup of the broth for a future rice bowl."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Pork Bone Gamjatang","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":36,"howThisFeels":"The late-night classic — long-simmered pork bones, soft potatoes, red bloom and the perilla swirl.","trivia":"The classic is the long-simmered original — soaked and blanched bones simmered until the meat falls off, finished with the nutty perilla seed powder.","delta":{},"ingredients":"400g pork neck bones · 2 potatoes · 100g cabbage · ½ onion · 20g gochugaru · 10ml gochujang · 15ml soy · 2 tbsp garlic · 1 tbsp perilla seed powder · 1 tsp ginger · 2 spring onions · sesame oil · pepper","method":["Soak the bones 30 minutes; drain. Blanch 5 minutes in fresh water, skimming; rinse clean.","Simmer the clean bones in fresh water with garlic, ginger and soy, LOW, 1 h 15–1 h 30, until the meat falls from the bone.","Bloom gochugaru and gochujang in oil 30 seconds; add to the pot with the potatoes, cabbage, onion and perilla powder.","Simmer 20-25 minutes until the potatoes are soft and the broth is red and glossy.","Finish with spring onion and sesame; serve the pot with rice — and soak the last red spoonfuls into a rice bowl."]},{"name":"Quick — Pressure Cooker Gamjatang","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34,"howThisFeels":"The same fierce red pot in 40 minutes — the pressure cooker does the bone-simmer in a third of the time, and the perilla still makes it gamjatang.","trivia":"A pressure cooker genuinely works for the bones: 35 minutes at high pressure breaks down the collagen with the same rich result as the two-hour open simmer.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Soak and blanch as the classic, then pressure-cook the clean bones with the water, garlic, ginger and soy on high for 35 minutes, natural release. Bring to an open boil, add the bloom of gochugaru-gochujang, the potatoes, cabbage, onion and perilla powder, and simmer 15 minutes until the potatoes soften. The red, nutty, bone-rich pot is on the table in about 40 minutes."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-dwaeji-gukbap","name":"Dwaeji Gukbap","nameAlt":"Pork & rice soup — the Busan morning-and-late-night institution","aliases":["dwaeji gukbap","pork soup korea","busan pork soup","gukbap"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","pork","rice","comfort","regional"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["breakfast","late-night","comfort","everyday","regional"],"mood":"Soulful & hearty","time":"Prep 15 min · Cook 40 min (using a rich broth — see note)","health":"A genuinely nourishing bowl — clear pork broth, sliced pork and rice, gently seasoned at the table; the classic Korean hangover-and-comfort cure of the south.","allergen":"Soy (the ssamjang-style paste at the table) · pork · sesame (sesame oil) · shellfish (saewujeot, salted shrimp, at the table — optional)","budget":"★★★ — pork and rice in a rich broth; the honest soul-food of the Korean south, priced for daily eating","audience":"The regional soul-food bowl — Busan's defining dish, eaten at breakfast by workers, at late night by the city, and by visitors who learn the local rhythm of spoon, rice and broth.","season":"A year-round institution — the morning bowl and the late-night bowl both belong to it","ingredients":"250g pork (belly or collar), sliced thin · 400ml rich pork or chicken stock (the better the stock, the better the bowl — see method) · 80g cooked short-grain rice, hot · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 1 slice ginger · 1 spring onion, very finely sliced · 5ml sesame oil · FOR THE TABLE: coarse salt, black pepper, and a small bowl each of ssamjang-style paste and saewujeot (salted shrimp — optional but classic) · black pepper","method":"DWAEGI GUKBAP IS THE SOUL FOOD OF BUSAN — a bowl of hot rice covered with sliced pork and a rich, milky pork broth, seasoned at the table by the eater with salt, pepper and the sharp pastes. It is Korea's southern institution, served from morning markets to midnight spots, and the entire craft is the BROTH — a rich, clear, slightly milky pork stock that tastes of patience. THE METHOD IS THE BROTH AND THE TABLE SEASONING.\n\n⚖️ FIRST, THE BROTH — THIS IS THE ENTIRE DISH. A proper dwaeji gukbap broth is made by simmering pork bones low and long until it turns pale and milky-rich — the same long discipline as the bone soups of the Korean table. For the home bowl, the honest path is a good quality pork or chicken stock deepened with the garlic and ginger, simmered 20 minutes. If you have the bones and the time, the real bowl is the reward: 1 hour of gentle simmering of pork bones with the aromatics makes the milky-pale stock that defines Busan's soup. Everything else is the table.\n\n⚖️ THE BOWL — RICE FIRST, PORK OVER, BROTH OVER ALL. Place the hot cooked rice in a deep bowl. Lay the thin slices of pork over the rice, then pour the hot broth over everything. Scatter the finely sliced spring onion and a thread of sesame oil. The bowl should arrive at the table steaming, the rice hidden under the broth and the pork.\n\n⚖️ THE TABLE — THE EATER SEASONS, THE EATER FINISHES. This is the soul of gukbap: the table holds coarse salt, black pepper, a small bowl of ssamjang-style paste (gochujang + a little sesame) and, in the classic south, saewujeot — salted small shrimp, the sharp, briny spoonful that is the local signature. Each eater seasons their own bowl: a pinch of salt, a grind of pepper, a stir of the paste, and — for the brave — a spoon of the saewujeot. The broth is meant to be finished to the last drop, and a second helping of rice is often ordered to soak up what remains.\n\n⚖️ THE RHYTHM — SPOON UP THE RICE, SIPPED WITH THE BROTH. The bowl is eaten with the spoon working the rice up through the broth — each spoon a little rice, a little pork, a little seasoned broth. It is the breakfast of Busan's markets, the late-night comfort of the whole city, and one of the great soul dishes of Korea.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 40 min (with a good stock; 1 h 30 if simmering bones)","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 30g · Carbs 55g · Fat 18g · Sodium ~900mg (before table seasoning)","storage":"The broth keeps 3 days refrigerated (lift the fat off the top) and freezes well for a month — the bowl itself is 10 minutes from a rich stock. The pork slices keep 2 days; reheat gently in the broth.","chefNotes":"The whole craft is the BROTH — rich, milky and patient (or a good stock deepened with garlic and ginger). The table is the ritual: coarse salt, pepper, the paste and the saewujeot, with the eater in charge of the finish. The rice is hidden under the broth and the pork, and it is meant to be spooned up through it to the last drop.","pairsWith":"The table's saewujeot (salted shrimp) and ssamjang-style paste · Kimchi on the side · A cold barley tea to close","goesWith":["Gamjatang","Doenjang Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Dwaeji gukbap is the defining soul food of Busan, Korea's southern port city — a bowl of rice, sliced pork and rich broth that anchors both the working breakfast and the late-night table. Gukbap, literally soup-rice, is a whole Korean family of such bowls, and Busan's pork version is its most famous. The saewujeot — salted shrimp — on the table is the local signature, a briny sharpness that the city's eaters stir into the broth with the practised confidence of a centuries-old habit.","howThisFeels":"The steaming bowl with the pork hidden under the milky broth, the first spoon that brings rice, pork and seasoned broth together — the deep, clean satisfaction of being fed properly, the taste of Busan's streets in a bowl.","leftovers":["The broth is the treasure — it keeps 3 days and a fresh bowl (new rice, fresh pork slices) is 10 minutes away.","Spoon the remaining broth over a bowl of rice with kimchi for a second, sharper soup-rice.","Freeze the broth in portions — the soul of the bowl is always one pot of rice away."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Busan Dwaeji Gukbap","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"The full Busan bowl — rich broth, sliced pork, hidden rice, and the table's salt, pepper, paste and saewujeot in the eater's hands.","trivia":"The classic is the Busan original — rice under the broth, sliced pork over it, seasoned at the table with the salted shrimp that is the city's signature.","delta":{},"ingredients":"250g pork, sliced thin · 400ml rich pork or chicken stock · 80g cooked rice, hot · 2 cloves garlic · 1 slice ginger · 1 spring onion · 5ml sesame oil · table: salt, pepper, ssamjang paste, saewujeot","method":["Simmer the stock with the garlic and ginger 20 minutes (or simmer pork bones 1 hour for the milky classic).","Place the hot rice in a deep bowl; lay the sliced pork over; pour the hot broth over all.","Scatter the spring onion and a thread of sesame oil.","Set the table with coarse salt, pepper, the ssamjang paste and the saewujeot.","Each eater seasons and finishes their own bowl — the broth to the last spoon, rice spooned up through it."]},{"name":"Quick — Pressed-On-Time Gukbap","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The same warming bowl in 20 minutes from a quality stock — the table's seasoning and the bowl's ritual are unchanged, only the broth's patience is borrowed.","trivia":"A quality pot of good stock (or the leftover broth from a previous cook) is the legitimate shortcut — the bowl itself is then 10 minutes of assembly, and the table does the rest.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Use a good bottled pork or chicken stock (or a frozen batch of your own), simmer it with the garlic and ginger for 15 minutes, and build the bowl exactly as the classic — rice, pork slices, hot broth, spring onion, sesame. The table's salt, pepper, paste and saewujeot are the soul regardless of how the stock was earned."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-hotteok","name":"Hotteok","nameAlt":"Sweet-filled griddle pancakes — the winter street sweet","aliases":["hotteok","korean sweet pancake","hotteok pancake","syrupy pancake korea"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","pancake","street-food","sweet","winter"],"diet":["vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["street-food","winter","snack","festive","weekend"],"mood":"Warm & indulgent","time":"Prep 30 min (dough rise 1 h) · Cook 15 min","health":"A winter street indulgence, honestly stated — the fried dough and molten brown-sugar heart make it a treat, not a daily bowl. The classic winter comfort of the Korean street.","allergen":"Gluten (wheat flour) · nuts (the classic peanut or walnut filling) · sesame (sesame oil for the griddle)","budget":"★★★ — flour, brown sugar, cinnamon and a handful of nuts; among the cheapest joys on the winter street","audience":"The winter street-stall favourite — bought fresh and eaten hot in the cold, and made at home for the fun of the fill-and-press fold.","season":"WINTER, absolutely — the smell of hotteok frying is the smell of the Korean winter market and the holiday street","ingredients":"150g plain flour · 5g instant yeast · 20g sugar · a pinch of salt · 120ml warm water · 10ml oil (for the dough) · THE FILLING: 50g brown sugar · 5g cinnamon · 40g chopped peanuts or walnuts · oil for the griddle · sesame oil (a thread for the pan)","method":"HOTEOK IS THE WINTER STREET SWEET OF KOREA — a yeasted pancake, crisp-golden outside, with a molten heart of brown sugar, cinnamon and chopped nuts that spills when you bite it. It is sold from the open-air griddles of the winter markets, where the smell of frying dough and melted sugar stops every passer-by, and it is the taste of the Korean cold season. THE METHOD IS THE FILL AND THE PRESS.\n\n⚖️ THE DOUGH — SOFT, SLIGHTLY SWEET, RISEN. Mix the flour, yeast, sugar and salt, then the warm water and a spoon of oil, and knead into a soft, slightly sticky dough. Cover and let it rise in a warm spot for an hour — it should double, soft and pillowy. (A baking-powder quick version exists for the impatient — see the Quick version.)\n\n⚖️ THE FILLING — THE BROWN SUGAR HEART. Mix the brown sugar, cinnamon and chopped nuts together. The classic filling is brown sugar and cinnamon with peanuts or walnuts — the nuts are not garnish, they are the textural anchor that turns melted sugar into a soft, chewy syrup instead of a scorching puddle.\n\n⚖️ THE FILL AND THE SEAL — THE LITTLE POCKET. Take a piece of risen dough (about walnut-sized), flatten it into a disc in your palm, and place a generous spoon of the filling in the centre. Fold the edges up and pinch them together into a little sealed parcel — the seal must be complete, or the molten sugar escapes on the griddle. Gently flatten the parcel into a thick pancake.\n\n⚖️ THE GRIDDLE — OILED, MEDIUM, AND THE PRESS. Heat a griddle or heavy pan with a slick of oil over medium heat. Lay the hotteok in, and press it GENTLY with a spatula as it cooks — this pressing is the famous move that spreads the pancake thin and helps the sugar melt into syrup against the hot centre. Cook 2-3 minutes per side until deeply golden and crisp-edged.\n\n⚖️ THE EAT — HOT, WITH THE WARNING. Eat the hotteok while it is scorching — the molten heart is the danger and the delight. The first bite cracks the crisp golden shell and the brown-sugar syrup pours out, warm and nutty. This is winter in Korea: cold hands, hot pancake, and the immediate reach for another while the griddle sizzles.","cookTime":"Prep 30 min · Rise 1 h · Cook 15 min","kcal":"~340 kcal each","nutrition":"Carbs 52g · Fat 14g · Protein 5g · Sugar ~22g — a winter treat, served with the honesty it deserves","storage":"Best straight from the griddle while the shell is crisp and the heart molten. Leftovers keep a day in the fridge and re-crisp briefly in a dry pan — the molten heart will not return, but the warm sweet shell does. The risen dough keeps a day in the fridge for morning hotteok.","chefNotes":"The three moves: a CLEAN SEAL on the parcel (molten sugar on the griddle is the one failure), the GENTLE PRESS as it cooks (the classic spreading move that melts the heart), and MEDIUM heat for the golden crisp — too hot burns the shell before the inside cooks. Eat scorching, with the warning.","pairsWith":"Cold hands on a winter street · A cup of hot tea or coffee · Milk for the children at the stall","goesWith":["Patbingsu","Korean Fried Chicken","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Hotteok is the winter street food of Korea — the yeasted sweet pancake fried on open griddles at every cold-season market, where the smell alone is the marketing. It is said to have arrived through the Chinese-Korean kitchens, adapted into Korea's sweetest street classic, and its winter hold on the national palate is absolute: hotteok and the cold season are synonymous, and the syrup that spills from the molten heart is one of the great simple joys of Korean eating.","howThisFeels":"The golden pancake steaming in its paper sleeve, the first careful bite cracking the crisp shell — and the warm brown-sugar syrup pouring out, nutty and molten, the perfect warmth for cold hands on a winter street.","leftovers":["Re-crisp leftover hotteok briefly in a dry pan and serve with tea — the shell returns, the heart stays sweet.","Slice cold hotteok and toast it for a surprisingly good breakfast sweet.","The risen dough keeps overnight in the fridge — fresh hotteok is 10 minutes away tomorrow morning."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Brown-Sugar Hotteok","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":10,"howThisFeels":"The winter classic — yeasted dough, brown-sugar-cinnamon-nut heart, pressed golden on the griddle, molten when it cracks.","trivia":"The classic is the street-standard: brown sugar, cinnamon and chopped peanuts, sealed in yeast dough and pressed on the hot griddle.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g flour · 5g yeast · 20g sugar · pinch of salt · 120ml warm water · 10ml oil · filling: 50g brown sugar + 5g cinnamon + 40g chopped nuts · griddle oil","method":["Knead the flour, yeast, sugar, salt, warm water and oil into a soft dough; rise 1 hour until doubled.","Mix the brown sugar, cinnamon and nuts into the filling.","Flatten a walnut-sized piece of dough, spoon in the filling, seal into a parcel, flatten gently.","Fry over medium heat in an oiled pan, pressing gently, 2-3 minutes per side until golden-crisp.","Eat scorching hot — the molten heart is the warning and the delight."]},{"name":"Quick — Baking-Powder Hotteok (No Yeast)","icon":"⚡","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":10,"howThisFeels":"The same golden sweet in 20 minutes — a baking-powder dough skips the hour-long rise and lands the molten heart on the table fast.","trivia":"A baking-powder dough (flour + baking powder + a little sugar + water) rises instantly on the griddle — a legitimate home shortcut that keeps the filling and the press.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Swap the yeast for 1 tsp baking powder in the dry mix (add a teaspoon of oil and use a little less water for a drop-dough consistency), skip the rise entirely, and proceed with the same fill, seal and press. The pancakes come out thicker and slightly more cake-like, but the molten sugar heart and the golden crisp are all yours in 20 minutes."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-mandu","name":"Mandu","nameAlt":"Korean dumplings — the pleated bundles of celebration","aliases":["mandu","mandoo","korean dumplings","gunmandu","mandu-guk"],"course":"main","type":["main","dumplings","celebration","new-year","sharing","make-your-own"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["new-year","celebration","family","party","everyday"],"mood":"Warm & festive","time":"Prep 40 min · Cook 15 min","health":"A balanced bite in a wrapper — the filling carries protein, vegetables and a little chew; steamed or pan-fried, they are one of the more honest celebration foods.","allergen":"Gluten (the wrappers) · soy (soy sauce in the filling and dip) · sesame (sesame oil) · egg (some wrapper recipes) · pork (in the classic filling)","budget":"★★★ — a little pork stretches far in a big filling; the cheapest way to feed a hungry, happy table","audience":"The celebration dumpling-dish — made in big batches with family or friends for New Year and holidays, and equally at home as a hungry-night snack or a mandu-guk soup bowl.","season":"New Year and the holidays are its home, but a batch is welcome any cold night; the New Year table's dumpling soup is its greatest moment","ingredients":"12 round dumpling wrappers (fresh from an Asian grocer — or see the notes for making your own) · THE FILLING: 100g pork mince · 60g cabbage, very finely chopped, lightly salted and squeezed dry · 30g chives or spring onion, finely chopped · 20g glass noodles, soaked and finely chopped · 50g firm tofu, crumbled and squeezed dry · 1 clove garlic, minced · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 1 tsp grated ginger · black pepper · DIP: 15ml soy + 10ml vinegar + a pinch of chilli flakes + a drop of sesame oil","method":"MANDU ARE THE KOREAN DUMPLINGS — pleated bundles of savoury filling, steamed, pan-fried or dropped into soup, and the great make-together dish of the Korean celebration table. They appear at New Year in mandu-guk (dumpling soup), at the family table fried crisp as gunmandu, and at every gathering where a table of people and a pile of wrappers is the afternoon's plan. THE METHOD IS THE FILLING AND THE FOLD.\n\n⚖️ THE FILLING — DRY IS THE RULE. Finely chop the cabbage, salt it lightly, and squeeze it DRY; crumble and squeeze the tofu dry; soak and chop the glass noodles fine. Combine all with the pork mince, chives, garlic, soy, sesame oil, ginger and pepper, and mix thoroughly. The squeezing is the craft — a wet filling makes soggy, bursting dumplings, and a dry, well-mixed filling makes the perfect plump bite.\n\n⚖️ THE FOLD — THE SIMPLE HALF-MOON. Place a wrapper in your palm, spoon a small amount of filling in the centre, and moisten the edge with water. Fold the wrapper over into a half-moon and press the edge firmly, pleating one side as you go for the classic crimped look — or simply pinch it shut for the honest home dumpling. The seal is the whole skill: a tight seal keeps the juice inside.\n\n⚖️ THE THREE WAYS — STEAM, PAN, OR SOUP. Steam the mandu (5-7 minutes, until the wrappers are translucent and the filling cooked) for the tender version; pan-fry them in a little oil until golden-crisp on the bottom, then add a splash of water and cover to steam — the classic gunmandu with an almost glassy, crisp base; or drop them into a hot broth for mandu-guk. The filling is cooked and the wrappers thin, so they cook fast.\n\n⚖️ THE DIP AND THE TABLE — A POT OF SHARING. Serve the mandu piled on a plate with the sharp soy-vinegar-chilli dip, or set the table with wrappers, filling and dip and let everyone fold their own — the mandu-making table is one of the great Korean social gatherings. Eat one immediately for quality control. Then another.","cookTime":"Prep 40 min · Cook 15 min","kcal":"~420 kcal (for 6 dumplings)","nutrition":"Protein 20g · Carbs 45g · Fat 16g · Sodium ~1100mg (with dip)","storage":"Mandu freeze brilliantly — lay the folded dumplings on a floured tray, freeze until firm, then bag them for up to 2 months. Cook frozen mandu directly (add 2-3 minutes). The filling keeps a day in the fridge; the cooked dumplings keep 2 days and re-steam or re-crisp well.","chefNotes":"Two rules: the filling must be DRY (squeeze the cabbage and tofu — wet filling is the one failure), and the SEAL must be tight (a burst dumpling loses its juice). The three ways — steam, crisp-pan, or soup — all start from the same fold. And the dip is non-negotiable.","pairsWith":"The sharp soy-vinegar-chilli dip · Mandu-guk (dumpling soup) for the New Year table · Kimchi on the side · Cold beer","goesWith":["Gimbap","Tteokguk","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Mandu are Korea's dumplings, and they are the great make-together food of the celebration table — New Year's mandu-guk is the holiday soup of the winter season, and the dumpling-making afternoon, with a table of wrappers and filling and everyone folding their own, is one of the warmest social rituals of the Korean kitchen. Pan-fried crisp as gunmandu they are the beloved snack; dropped in soup they are the holiday; and a batch in the freezer is the Korean household's answer to the emergency hungry hour.","howThisFeels":"The steaming or crisp-crisp pile of pleated bundles, the first bite releasing the savoury filling, the sharp dip cutting through — and the quiet pride of having folded each pleat by hand, the taste of a celebration shared.","leftovers":["Frozen mandu are the great treasure — boil or pan-fry them straight from the freezer for an instant hungry-bowl.","Drop leftover cooked mandu into a hot broth with vegetables for a fast mandu-guk soup.","Re-crisp cold mandu in a dry pan and serve with the dip again — they are just as good the second time."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Pork Mandu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The classic half-moons — pork, cabbage, chives and glass noodles folded tight, steamed or pan-crisped with the sharp dip.","trivia":"The classic is the pork-and-vegetable filling — the balanced, savoury bundle that anchors the Korean dumpling table.","delta":{},"ingredients":"12 dumpling wrappers · 100g pork mince · 60g cabbage, squeezed dry · 30g chives · 20g glass noodles, chopped · 50g tofu, squeezed dry · 1 clove garlic · 10ml soy · 5ml sesame oil · 1 tsp ginger · pepper · dip: soy + vinegar + chilli + sesame","method":["Squeeze the cabbage and tofu dry; mix all the filling ingredients thoroughly.","Spoon a small filling onto each wrapper; moisten the edge; fold and pleat into a tight half-moon.","Steam 5-7 minutes, or pan-fry crisp-then-steam for gunmandu, or simmer in a hot broth for mandu-guk.","Serve piled with the sharp soy-vinegar-chilli dip."]},{"name":"Vegan Mandu (Mushroom & Tofu)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"The same pleated bundles with mushrooms and tofu doing the savoury work — a filling that loses nothing, and the vegan guest finally gets a dumpling.","trivia":"Finely chopped shiitake and squeezed tofu, seasoned with soy, garlic and ginger, make a filling impossibly close to the classic — the mushroom brings the savour and the tofu brings the body.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"100g pork mince"}],"addIng":[{"item":"80g shiitake or oyster mushrooms, very finely chopped"},{"item":"extra 50g firm tofu, crumbled and squeezed dry"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Brown the finely chopped mushrooms in a dry pan until they release their water and turn golden, then season with a drop of soy. Mix with the tofu and the rest of the filling and fold exactly as the classic. The wrappers are naturally vegan (check the packet), and the dip is fully plant-based — the vegan dumpling table is complete."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-sikhye","name":"Sikhye","nameAlt":"Sweet rice drink — the dessert that ends the Korean meal","aliases":["sikhye","sweet rice drink","korean rice punch","sikhye drink"],"course":"drink","type":["drink","beverage","dessert-drink","fermented","sweet"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","dessert","party","festive","digestif"],"mood":"Sweet & refreshing","time":"Prep 15 min · Malt steep 2 h · Simmer 30 min · Ferment 6-8 h","health":"A modestly sweet, fermented grain drink — lighter in sugar than most desserts, and the traditional end-of-meal palate cleanser of the Korean table.","allergen":"Gluten (barley malt — the fermenting agent) · rice","budget":"★★★ — rice, malt and sugar; among the cheapest sweet finishes in Korean food","audience":"The traditional end-of-meal drink — served after festive meals and everyday dinners alike, with the floating rice grains and the chewy pine nuts on top.","season":"A year-round digestif — served after every festive meal, and as a cold, syrupy refresher in summer","ingredients":"60g cooked short-grain rice · 25g barley malt powder (yeotgireum — the enzyme-rich fermenting agent at Asian grocers) · 500ml water · 20g sugar (to taste) · a few pine nuts (the classic garnish)","method":"SIKHYE IS THE SWEET RICE DRINK THAT ENDS THE KOREAN MEAL — a clear, golden, gently sweet brew with grains of rice floating in it, served cold with a scatter of pine nuts. It is the traditional digestif of the Korean table, the palate-cleansing finish to festive meals, and its story is a quiet act of kitchen alchemy: the barley malt breaks the rice's starch down INTO sugar, with no extra sweetness doing the talking until the very end.\n\n⚖️ FIRST, THE MALT TEA — THE ENZYME BATH. Mix the barley malt powder with 300ml of warm water (not hot — around 50°C, the temperature that wakes the enzymes without killing them), and let it steep 1-2 hours. Strain through a fine sieve or cloth, pressing the malt well — the strained liquid is your enzyme-rich malt tea, the engine of the whole drink.\n\n⚖️ THE RICE AND THE REACTION — WHERE THE SWEETNESS COMES FROM. Add the cooked rice to the strained malt tea and keep it at a warm 50-60°C for about 4-6 hours (an oven with its light on, or a warm spot, works). During this rest, the malt's amylase enzymes break the rice starch down into maltose — the sugar created by chemistry rather than by the bag. The liquid will taste noticeably sweet without a spoon of sugar having touched it.\n\n⚖️ THE SIMMER — SETTING THE SWEETNESS. Strain the liquid into a pot, pressing the rice grains well. Bring to a gentle boil — this stops the enzyme action and sets the sweetness — then add the sugar to taste (traditional sikhye is sweet but not cloying; the malt creates a lot of the sweetness alone). Separate a spoon of the cooked rice to float in the finished drink.\n\n⚖️ THE CHILL AND THE SERVE. Chill the sikhye thoroughly. Serve cold in a bowl with a spoonful of the reserved rice grains floating in it and a scatter of pine nuts. The floating rice is the signature: a sip of sweet golden liquid, then a chewy grain and a pine-nut to finish — the perfect closing note to the Korean meal.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Malt steep 2 h · Simmer 30 min · Ferment 6-8 h (mostly hands-off)","kcal":"~140 kcal per 250ml","nutrition":"Carbs 32g · Sugar ~20g (largely from the malt's own enzymes) · no fat · a traditional light finish","storage":"Sikhye keeps 3-4 days refrigerated and is best chilled. The bottled version keeps carbonating gently — open loose. It is served cold always; the floating rice and pine nuts are added fresh to each bowl.","chefNotes":"The three rules: the MALT TEA must be warm-not-hot (50°C wakes the enzymes, hotter kills them), the RICE AND MALT REST must be patient (4-6 hours for the enzymes to do their sweet work), and the BOIL at the end SETS the sweetness — it stops the enzymes and stabilises the drink. The floating rice is not garnish; it is the signature.","pairsWith":"The end of the meal, always · Fresh pine nuts · A quiet moment after a festive table","goesWith":["Patbingsu","Yaksik"],"trivia":"Sikhye is the traditional end-of-meal drink of Korea, and its magic is real chemistry: the barley malt (yeotgireum) contains amylase enzymes that break the rice starch down into maltose — so the sweetness is largely born from the grain itself, with only a final touch of sugar. It is served after festive meals as the palate cleanser and digestive, and the floating rice grains with pine nuts are its signature. In Korea, sikhye is also the classic home-made sweet sipped cold through the year, a gentle, golden, quietly astonishing brew from nothing but grain, water and time.","howThisFeels":"The cold golden sip of gentle sweetness, then a chewy float of rice and a pine-nut finish — the quiet, palate-cleansing close to a big Korean meal, the taste of the table settling content.","leftovers":["The drink keeps 3-4 days cold — serve it chilled with fresh rice and pine nuts each bowl.","Use leftover sikhye (strained of rice) as a gentle sweet poaching liquid for pears.","Freeze the strained sikhye in ice-cube trays for a sweet, mellow ice to drop into tea or rice punch later."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Sikhye","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":6,"howThisFeels":"The genuine brew — barley malt and rice making their own sugar over hours, served cold with the floating grains and pine nuts.","trivia":"The classic is the full enzyme method — malt tea, patient rest, and only a final touch of sugar, the traditional sweetness born of chemistry.","delta":{},"ingredients":"60g cooked rice · 25g barley malt powder · 500ml water · 20g sugar · pine nuts","method":["Steep the malt in warm water (50°C) 1-2 hours; strain, pressing well.","Add the cooked rice; rest at 50-60°C for 4-6 hours until the liquid turns sweet from the enzymes.","Strain, pressing the rice; bring to a gentle boil to set the sweetness; add sugar to taste.","Reserve a spoon of rice floats; chill thoroughly.","Serve cold with the floating rice grains and a scatter of pine nuts."]}]},
{"id":"korea-yaksik","name":"Yaksik","nameAlt":"Sweet rice with dates & nuts — the sticky celebratory sweet","aliases":["yaksik","yakbap","sweet rice dates nuts","korean sweet rice cake"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","rice","sweet","celebration","festive","make-ahead"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["celebration","new-year","festive","dessert","make-ahead"],"mood":"Warm & festive","time":"Prep 20 min (+soak 2 h) · Steam 40-50 min","health":"A celebration sweet, honestly stated — glutinous rice, dates, nuts and honey make it an occasion, not a daily bowl; the nuts and dates bring some honest nutrition to the treat.","allergen":"Nuts (the classic chestnuts, walnuts) · soy (the soy sauce in the syrup — a surprising but classic touch) · rice · honey","budget":"★★☆ — dates, chestnuts and honey are the cost; the rice is the cheap base","audience":"The festive sweet — served at New Year and celebrations, made ahead in a batch and cut into sticky, glossy squares for sharing.","season":"New Year and the holidays; the celebration table's sweet is its home","ingredients":"150g glutinous rice (sweet rice), soaked 2 hours · 40g dried dates (jujube), stoned and slivered · 30g chestnuts (cooked, or walnuts), chopped · 20g raisins or dried cranberries · 20ml honey (or brown sugar syrup) · 5ml soy sauce (the unexpected classic savour) · 1 tsp sesame oil · a pinch of cinnamon · a pinch of salt · optional: 1 tbsp pine nuts","method":"YAKSIK IS THE STICKY CELEBRATORY SWEET OF KOREA — glutinous rice steamed with dates, chestnuts and honey into a glossy, magnificent sliceable sweet, served cut into squares at New Year and on festive tables. Its name means medicine food (yak), the old Korean belief that food and medicine are one — and this sweet, sticky, nut-studded treat was made for holidays and the sharing of good wishes. THE METHOD IS THE STEAM AND THE GLOSS.\n\n⚖️ THE RICE — SOAKED, THEN STEAMED TO STICKY. Soak the glutinous rice 2 hours, drain, and steam it until tender and sticky (about 20 minutes) — not mushy, but glossy and holding together. Glutinous rice is the right grain: it is the sticky variety, and its chew is the whole texture of yaksik.\n\n⚖️ THE GLOSS — THE HONEY-SOY SYRUP IS THE UNEXPECTED CLASSIC. Mix the honey (or sugar syrup), the soy sauce, cinnamon and salt into a syrup. The soy sauce is the quiet surprise of the dish: a teaspoon of savoury saltiness against the honey is what makes the sweetness deep and adult rather than cloying — the same balance the Korean kitchen uses across its sweets.\n\n⚖️ THE MEETING — EVERYTHING TOGETHER. Mix the steamed rice with the slivered dates, chopped chestnuts, raisins and the syrup, and combine thoroughly — every grain should be glossed. The spices and the fruit stud the sticky rice like jewels.\n\n⚖️ THE SECOND STEAM — THE GLOSSY SET. Press the mixture into a shallow dish, scatter the pine nuts over, and steam again for 20-25 minutes until the whole thing is glossy, sticky and set — the second steam is what makes it sliceable rather than a loose grain pile.\n\n⚖️ THE CUT AND THE SERVE. Let the yaksik cool slightly, then cut it into neat squares and serve — sticky, glossy, sweet-savoury, studded with dates and nuts. The traditional serving is a small square or two with tea, the festive table's most elegant sweet.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min (+soak 2 h) · Steam 40-50 min","kcal":"~260 kcal per square","nutrition":"Carbs 52g · Fat 4g · Protein 3g · Sugar ~18g — a celebration sweet, honestly stated","storage":"Yaksik keeps beautifully — refrigerated 5-7 days in a sealed container, and it freezes well for a month (slice then freeze, defrost at room temperature). It is genuinely better on day two as the syrup soaks through.","chefNotes":"Two elements make it: the SECOND STEAM (the glossy, sliceable set after mixing) and the TEASPOON OF SOY in the honey syrup (the savoury-salt that makes the sweet deep rather than cloying). Glutinous rice is non-negotiable — its chew is the whole texture; the fruit and nuts stud it like jewels.","pairsWith":"A cup of Korean or barley tea · The festive table's other sweets · A moment to share good wishes","goesWith":["Sikhye","Songpyeon"],"trivia":"Yaksik's name means medicine food — the old Korean conviction that food and medicine are one — and this sticky, glossy sweet was the holiday gift of the Korean table, made for New Year when the household wished each other well. The teaspoon of soy sauce in the honey syrup is the dish's quiet magic: a savoury-salt edge against the sweetness that makes it deep rather than cloying, a balance the Korean kitchen practises across its sweets.","howThisFeels":"The glossy, sticky square studded with dates and nuts, the first bite releasing the honey-sweet rice with that quiet savoury depth — the festive table's most elegant sweet, eaten with tea and good wishes.","leftovers":["Yaksik keeps a week cold and is genuinely better on day two as the syrup soaks through.","Slice and freeze for a month — defrost at room temperature for an instant festive sweet.","Warm a square briefly and serve with custard or cream for a modern dessert finish."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Yaksik","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"The festive original — glutinous rice, dates, chestnuts and raisins glossed in the honey-soy syrup, steamed to a sliceable sticky square.","trivia":"The classic is the New Year's square — the honey-soy gloss and the studded fruit, steamed twice for the sliceable set.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g glutinous rice, soaked · 40g dates, slivered · 30g chestnuts or walnuts · 20g raisins · 20ml honey · 5ml soy · 1 tsp sesame oil · pinch of cinnamon · pinch of salt · pine nuts","method":["Soak the rice 2 hours; steam until sticky, about 20 minutes.","Mix honey, soy, cinnamon and salt into a syrup.","Fold the dates, chestnuts, raisins and syrup through the steamed rice until every grain is glossed.","Press into a dish, scatter pine nuts, and steam again 20-25 minutes until set and glossy.","Cool slightly, cut into squares, and serve with tea."]}]},
{"id":"korea-bindaetteok","name":"Bindaetteok","nameAlt":"Mung bean pancakes — the crisp golden classic of the market stalls","aliases":["bindaetteok","mung bean pancake","nokdu jeon","mung bean jeon"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","pancake","mung-bean","street-food","crisp","jeon"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["street-food","market","party","festive","rainy-day"],"mood":"Crisp & savoury","time":"Prep 20 min (+soak 3-4 h) · Cook 15 min","health":"A genuinely wholesome savoury pancake — whole hulled mung beans ground with vegetables and pan-fried crisp; the protein-rich jewel of the Korean street stalls.","allergen":"Gluten (the dipping sauce) · soy (the dipping sauce) · sesame · bean (mung bean)","budget":"★★★ — hulled mung beans are cheap and the vegetables are pantry; the market-stall favourite priced for everyone","audience":"The market-stall classic — the crisp golden pancakes made to order over the street stalls' griddles, and a beloved chimaek-adjacent and rain-day companion at home.","season":"Market days, festival days and rainy days — the smell of bindaetteok frying is the smell of the Korean market","ingredients":"100g hulled mung beans (nokdu — the yellow split mung beans at any Asian grocer), soaked 3-4 hours · 40g cabbage, finely shredded · 30g bean sprouts, chopped · 20g spring onion, finely sliced · 50g pork mince or beef (optional; skip for vegan) · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 5ml sesame oil · salt · black pepper · oil for frying · DIP: soy + vinegar + a pinch of chilli + a drop of sesame oil","method":"BINDAETEOK IS THE MUNG BEAN PANCAKE OF THE KOREAN MARKET — a crisp, golden, protein-rich pancake ground from whole hulled mung beans, studded with cabbage and bean sprouts, fried on the street stalls' griddles and eaten hot in the hand. It is the jeon family's savoury heavyweight, and its crisp-edged golden crust over a soft, bean-sweet interior is one of the great market tastes of Korea. THE METHOD IS THE GRIND AND THE CRISP.\n\n⚖️ THE BEANS — SOAKED, THEN GROUND — THE BEAN IS THE BATTER. Soak the hulled mung beans 3-4 hours (they absorb water and soften), drain well, and grind them in a food processor with a little water into a thick, grainy paste — the beans THEMSELVES are the batter, no flour needed. The paste should be thick enough to hold its shape on the spoon, with a little grain left for texture. This is the whole craft: a bindaetteok is bean, not batter.\n\n⚖️ THE STUDDING — VEGETABLES FOLDED IN. Season the paste with the garlic, salt, pepper and sesame oil, and fold in the finely shredded cabbage, chopped bean sprouts and spring onion. If using the meat (the classic market version), brown the mince first and fold it through — the meat is the luxury, the beans and vegetables the heart.\n\n⚖️ THE GRIDDLE — HOT OIL, AND THE CRISP EDGE IS THE PRIZE. Heat a generous layer of oil in a pan over medium-high. Ladle in the bean batter in thick rounds (the classic is a generous hand-sized pancake), spread them flat, and cook until the edges turn deeply golden and lacy-crisp — the crisp, frilly golden edge is the bindaetteok signature. Flip once and cook the second side to the same gold.\n\n⚖️ THE EAT — HOT FROM THE GRIDDLE. Drain the pancakes briefly on a rack (not paper, which steams them) and serve immediately, hot and crisp, with the sharp soy-vinegar dip. The crust should crackle, the inside should be soft and bean-sweet — and the whole thing should vanish from the plate with suspicious speed.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min (+soak 3-4 h) · Cook 15 min","kcal":"~280 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 14g · Carbs 38g · Fat 10g · Sodium ~500mg (before dip)","storage":"Best straight from the griddle while the edges sing. Leftovers keep a day in the fridge and re-crisp in a hot dry pan; they are also a beloved leftover to slice into a gimbap. The bean paste keeps a day in the fridge ready to fry.","chefNotes":"Two rules: the BEAN IS THE BATTER (soaked hulled mung beans ground thick — no flour — the grainy paste is the whole character), and the GRIDDLE must be hot and well-oiled for the frilly golden CRISP EDGE (the signature). The sharp dip is the constant. And the classic meat is optional — the bean-and-vegetable heart is the dish.","pairsWith":"The sharp soy-vinegar dip · Cold beer (the classic market pairing) · Kimchi · Rain on the window","goesWith":["Kimchi Jeon","Haemul Pajeon","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Bindaetteok is the mung bean pancake of the Korean market — whole hulled mung beans ground into the batter, studded with vegetables, fried crisp on the street stalls' griddles. It takes its name and heritage from the bean itself (bindaetteok is named for the mung bean, nokdu), and it is the savoury heavyweight of the jeon family: where other jeon wear a whisper of flour batter, this one IS the bean. It is made to order over the market stalls and eaten hot in the hand, the crisp gold edge and the soft, bean-sweet interior defining one of the great street tastes of Korea.","howThisFeels":"The frilly golden pancake hot from the griddle, the first bite cracking the crisp edge into the soft bean-sweet interior, the sharp dip cutting through — the loud, crisp, satisfying taste of the Korean market, eaten in the hand.","leftovers":["Re-crisp leftover bindaetteok in a hot dry pan and slice into a gimbap — the jeon-gimbap again proves its worth.","Chop over a bowl of hot rice with the dip for a fast bean-cake rice bowl.","The prepared bean paste keeps a day in the fridge — fresh crispy pancakes are 10 minutes away."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Bindaetteok","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":16,"howThisFeels":"The market classic — bean-ground batter, cabbage and sprouts studded through, fried crisp-edged and eaten hot with the sharp dip.","trivia":"The classic is the bean-is-the-batter original — hulled mung beans ground thick, the vegetable studding, and the frilly golden edge that is the market's signature.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g hulled mung beans, soaked · 40g cabbage, shredded · 30g bean sprouts · 20g spring onion · 2 cloves garlic · 5ml sesame oil · salt · pepper · frying oil · soy-vinegar-chilli dip","method":["Soak the hulled mung beans 3-4 hours; drain; grind thick with a little water — the bean IS the batter.","Season and fold in the cabbage, sprouts, spring onion, garlic and sesame.","Fry thick rounds in hot oil over medium-high until the edges turn deeply golden and lacy-crisp; flip once.","Drain on a rack; serve immediately, hot and crisp, with the sharp dip."]},{"name":"With Pork (The Classic Market Version)","icon":"🥩","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"The same crisp golden pancakes with browned pork folded through — the market's classic luxury, and the version that anchors the drinking table.","trivia":"The market bindaetteok often folds a little browned pork or beef through the bean batter — the meat is the luxury layer over the bean-and-vegetable heart.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"50g pork mince or beef mince"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Brown the mince in a little oil until crisp, season with a pinch of salt and pepper, and fold it through the bean batter with the vegetables before frying exactly as the classic. The meat's sizzle and savour inside the crisp bean pancake is the market's classic richness."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-seolleongtang","name":"Seolleongtang","nameAlt":"Milky ox bone soup — the patient, pale broth of the Korean table","aliases":["seolleongtang","ox bone soup","korean bone soup","milky beef bone soup"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","beef","bones","long-simmer","comfort","white-broth"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["comfort","everyday","winter","restorative","lunch"],"mood":"Deep & restorative","time":"Prep 20 min · Simmer 3-5 h (pressure cooker 45 min)","health":"A deeply nourishing bone broth — the long simmer draws collagen and minerals from the bones into a milky, unctuous broth that is the traditional restorative of the Korean table.","allergen":"Beef (bones) · soy (the table seasoning) · gluten (the noodles / soy sauce at the table)","budget":"★★☆ — bones are the cheapest beef buy at the butcher; the pot feeds many for little","audience":"The restorative soup — the pale, milky broth that anchors the Korean cold-day table, simmered for hours and finished at the table with salt and spring onion.","season":"Winter and cold days are its home — and the recovery table, whenever the body asks for bone broth's patient wealth","ingredients":"400g beef leg or shin bones (the gelatinous, marrow-rich kind — ask your butcher) · 100g beef brisket (optional, for meaty chunks in the broth) · 1 onion, halved · 1 leek or 2 spring onions, in lengths · garlic (4 cloves, smashed) · 1 slice ginger · 1.5L water · FOR THE TABLE: fine salt, black pepper, very finely sliced spring onion, and thin wheat noodles or rice to serve in the broth · sesame seeds","method":"SEOLLEONGTANG IS THE MILKY OX BONE SOUP OF KOREA — a pale, milky, deeply restorative broth made by simmering beef bones long and patiently until the liquid turns the colour of white tea and the flavour is pure, clean, bone-rich depth. It is the patient soup of the Korean table, served simply with salt, pepper and spring onion at the table, the eater seasoning their own bowl. THE METHOD IS THE SOAK, THE BLANCH AND THE LONG WHITE SIMMER.\n\n⚖️ THE SOAK AND THE BLANCH — THE CLEAN WHITE START. Soak the bones in cold water 1 hour to draw out the blood — this is the step that lets the broth turn pale and clean rather than grey and murky. Drain, put them in a pot of cold water, and bring to a boil; blanch 5 minutes, skimming the scum, then drain and rinse the bones thoroughly.\n\n⚖️ THE LONG SIMMER — THE MILK IS THE BONE. Return the clean bones to a large pot with the fresh water, the onion, leek, garlic and ginger, and bring to a gentle boil. Lower to a steady simmer and cook for 3-5 HOURS, topping up with boiling water as it reduces, until the broth has turned pale and milky-white. This milky transformation is the collagen emulsifying into the water — the same science as samgyetang's white broth, on a grander scale. Do not stir vigorously; the gentle lapping of the simmer is what keeps it clear-pale rather than cloudy-grey.\n\n⚖️ THE MEAT — ADDED FOR THE POOL. If using the brisket, add it in the last 90 minutes so it cooks tender without grey: lift it out, slice or shred it, and serve it in the bowls.\n\n⚖️ THE TABLE — THE EATER FINISHES THE BOWL. Strain the milky broth, season it only gently at the pot, and serve with fine salt, black pepper, finely sliced spring onion, sesame seeds and noodles or rice on the side. The ritual: each eater seasons their own bowl — a pinch of salt, a grind of pepper, the bright spring onion — and the clean, milky, bone-deep broth is the whole point. The noodles or rice soak up its wealth, and the bowl ends with the pale broth to the last drop.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Simmer 3-5 h (pressure cooker 45 min)","kcal":"~280 kcal (with noodles)","nutrition":"Protein 22g · Fat 12g · Collagen-rich broth · Sodium from the table seasoning","storage":"The broth is the treasure — it keeps 5 days refrigerated (lift the fat cap off the top) and freezes perfectly for 2 months in portions. It is the best leftover stock in Korean cooking, the base for a dozen soups.","chefNotes":"Three rules: SOAK and BLANCH the bones (the clean white broth is the whole look), simmer LOW and LONG until the broth turns MILKY (the collagen is the point — 3 hours minimum, and a pressure cooker is the honest shortcut), and SEASON AT THE TABLE (the pale broth is the canvas; the salt, pepper and spring onion are the eater's brushes).","pairsWith":"Fine salt and pepper at the table · Thin wheat noodles or rice in the broth · Kimchi on the side · A warming winter window","goesWith":["Galbi Jjim","Dwaeji Gukbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Seolleongtang is the milky ox bone soup that has anchored the Korean table for centuries — a pale, patient broth simmered from beef bones until the collagen turns it milky-white, served simply with the eater's own seasoning. It is one of the great long-simmer soups of Korea, the restorative offered on cold days and the deep, clean flavour that defines the humble power of the bone. Its milk-white colour and unctuous depth are entirely the product of hours — the same gift the Korean table asks of its bone soups: time, patience, and the bones' own quiet wealth.","howThisFeels":"The milky pale broth steaming in the bowl, the first spoon clean and bone-deep, the salt and spring onion brightening each mouthful — the deep, restorative warmth of a soup that asked only for time, and returned everything.","leftovers":["The broth keeps 5 days and freezes perfectly — it is the best leftover stock in Korean cooking, the base for a dozen soups and rice bowls.","Ladle the chilled broth over rice with a fresh noodle handful for a quick second bowl.","Use the leftover broth as the base for a kalguksu (knife-cut noodle soup) or a quick kimchi soup."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Seolleongtang","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The patient classic — soaked and blanched bones simmered to a milky white broth, finished at the table with salt, pepper, spring onion and noodles.","trivia":"The classic is the long-simmered original — the clean white bone broth and the eater's own seasoning, the pale soup of the Korean cold-day table.","delta":{},"ingredients":"400g beef leg bones · 100g brisket (optional) · 1 onion · 1 leek · 4 cloves garlic · 1 slice ginger · 1.5L water · table: salt, pepper, spring onion, noodles, sesame","method":["Soak the bones 1 hour; drain; blanch 5 minutes, skimming; rinse thoroughly.","Simmer the clean bones with the aromatics and water, LOW, 3-5 hours, topping up with boiling water, until milky-white.","Add the brisket for the last 90 minutes; lift, slice or shred.","Strain the milky broth; season gently.","Serve with the meat, noodles, and the table's salt, pepper, spring onion and sesame — each eater finishes their own bowl."]},{"name":"Quick — Pressure Cooker Seolleongtang","icon":"⚡","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"The same milky bone broth in 45 minutes — the pressure cooker draws the collagen far faster, and the pale, clean bowl is honestly indistinguishable.","trivia":"A pressure cooker genuinely performs the miracle: 40-45 minutes at high pressure emulsifies the collagen into the same milky white as the five-hour simmer. It is the modern Korean home shortcut for the patient classic.","delta":{"addStep":[{"text":"Soak and blanch as the classic, then pressure-cook the clean bones with the aromatics and water on high for 40-45 minutes, natural release. The broth comes out pale and milky; strain, add the sliced brisket, season gently, and serve exactly as the classic with the table's salt, pepper, spring onion and noodles. Forty-five minutes, the same white wealth."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-haejangguk","name":"Haejangguk","nameAlt":"Hangover soup — the fiercely restorative broth of the morning after","aliases":["haejangguk","hangover soup","kongnamul guk","bean sprout hangover soup"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","spicy","restorative","bean-sprout","morning"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["morning","restorative","comfort","everyday","winter"],"mood":"Fiery & restorative","time":"Prep 15 min · Cook 25 min","health":"A genuinely restorative morning bowl — a fiery, briny broth heavy with bean sprouts and vegetables, the traditional Korean answer to the morning after and the cold morning before anything else.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce, gochujang) · sesame (sesame oil) · gluten (soy sauce) · shellfish (optional in some versions)","budget":"★★★ — bean sprouts and a few vegetables; the cheapest fierce bowl in the Korean morning","audience":"The morning-after institution — the steaming, fiery bowl that the whole country knows as the cure, ordered from the early morning soup houses before the day begins.","season":"Morning and the morning after, whenever the body asks — the spicy, restorative bowl that starts a difficult day","ingredients":"150g bean sprouts (kongnamul — a generous handful) · 1 egg (the classic topping) · 100g napa cabbage, in squares · 30g onion, sliced · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 15g gochugaru (or 1 tsp gochujang for depth) · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 1 tsp Korean soup soy sauce or fish sauce (optional depth — skip for vegan) · 400ml stock or water · 1 spring onion, sliced · black pepper","method":"HAEJANGGUK IS THE HANGOVER SOUP — the fiercely spicy, bean-sprout-heavy broth that Korea has sworn by since time out of mind as the morning-after cure, and which is beloved far beyond its reputation as the restorative of the hungover. Its name says exactly what it does — haejang means to settle or dispel, and the bowl is the summoned fire that puts the world right again. THE METHOD IS THE FIERY BROTH AND THE SPROUTS.\n\n⚖️ FIRST, THE FIRE — BLOOM AND BROTH. Heat the sesame oil in a pot, add the garlic and the gochugaru, and stir 30 seconds until the chilli blooms into a deep red, fragrant base — the same bloom that drives so much of the Korean red school. Add the soy (and the soup soy or fish sauce, if using), then the stock or water, and bring to a rolling bubble.\n\n⚖️ THE SPROUTS AND CABBAGE — THE BODY OF THE BOWL. Add the bean sprouts and the cabbage and simmer 10-12 minutes until the sprouts are tender but still have bite — the sprouts are the body of the soup, a generous heap in the fiery broth, and they must keep a little crunch rather than melting away.\n\n⚖️ THE EGG — THE CLASSIC CLOUD. Just before serving, crack the egg into the bubbling centre and stir once or twice so it ribbons through the broth — the classic cloud of egg that turns the fiery bowl silky and whole.\n\n⚖️ THE EAT — SCORCHING, WITH RICE. Season with salt and pepper to taste, scatter the spring onion over, and serve SCORCHING hot with a bowl of rice on the side. The ritual: alternate spoonfuls of the fiery broth and bites of rice until the bowl and the morning are both set to rights.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~240 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 14g · Carbs 18g · Fat 10g · Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Best the morning it is made — the sprouts keep their bite. The broth keeps 2 days in the fridge; reheat gently and add a fresh handful of sprouts and a fresh egg to revive it properly.","chefNotes":"Three rules: BLOOM the gochugaru for the deep fierce red (the bowl is a fire summoned), keep the SPROUTS with a little bite (they are the body, not a garnish that cooks away), and serve SCORCHING hot with rice beside — the alternation of fire and rice IS the ritual. The egg ribbons at the end are the silk.","pairsWith":"A bowl of rice, always · A cold side of kimchi · The morning, reclaimed","goesWith":["Dwaeji Gukbap","Kimchi Jjigae","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Haejangguk means hangover soup — the fierce, spicy, bean-sprout-heavy broth that Korea has sworn by for centuries as the morning-after cure and the start-the-day-right bowl. It is the country's great restorative, served from the early-morning soup houses that open before the day begins, and its reputation is as cultural as culinary: the bowl of fire and bean sprouts is Korea's answer to the night before and the cold morning alike.","howThisFeels":"The fierce red bowl steaming up into your face, the sprouts still with their bite, the egg clouding the broth, the first scorching spoonful putting the world to rights — the taste of the morning reclaimed, one fiery mouthful at a time.","leftovers":["The broth keeps 2 days — reheat with fresh sprouts and a fresh egg for a properly revived bowl.","Add leftover broth to a pan of rice with kimchi for a fast spicy rice soup.","The broth is the base for a quick noodle soup — add fresh noodles and a handful of greens."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Kongnamul Haejangguk","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":18,"howThisFeels":"The classic morning-after bowl — bloomed chilli, generous bean sprouts, cabbage and the egg cloud, served scorching with rice.","trivia":"The classic is the bean-sprout version — the fiery broth built on the sprout's clean bite, the egg cloud, and the scorching bowl that starts the day.","delta":{},"ingredients":"150g bean sprouts · 1 egg · 100g napa cabbage · 30g onion · 2 cloves garlic · 15g gochugaru · 10ml soy · 5ml sesame oil · 400ml stock · 1 spring onion · pepper","method":["Bloom the garlic and gochugaru in sesame oil 30 seconds; add the soy, then the stock; bring to a rolling bubble.","Add the bean sprouts and cabbage; simmer 10-12 minutes until the sprouts are tender with bite.","Crack the egg in and stir once so it ribbons through; season.","Serve scorching hot with the spring onion and a bowl of rice beside — fire and rice, in alternation."]},{"name":"Vegan Haejangguk (Mushroom-Deep)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":16,"howThisFeels":"The same fierce bowl with a deep mushroom stock standing in for the seafood depth — the fire and the sprouts carry it completely.","trivia":"Skip the fish sauce and deepen with a shiitake-kelp stock, and the bowl becomes fully plant-based — the gochugaru bloom and the sprouts are the soul, and the egg is optional for the omnivorous.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"1 egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"kelp and shiitake to deepen the stock"},{"item":"extra tofu cubes for the bowl (optional)"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Simmer a strip of kelp and two dried shiitake in the broth base 10 minutes, then remove them. Skip the fish sauce and the egg — the gochugaru bloom and the generous sprouts carry the fierce, restorative character completely. Add tofu cubes for the body if you like. The morning-after fire is fully plant-based."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-gyeran-mari","name":"Gyeran-mari","nameAlt":"Korean rolled omelette — the yellow-spangled banchan jewel","aliases":["gyeran mari","rolled omelette korea","korean egg roll","gyeran-mari"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","egg","banchan","lunchbox","quick","vegetarian"],"diet":["vegetarian","omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["lunchbox","everyday","banchan","picnic","party"],"mood":"Bright & cheerful","time":"Prep 10 min · Cook 10 min","health":"A light, protein-forward banchan — eggs with a little color and crunch from the carrot and spring onion; one of the healthier, cheeriest small plates of the Korean table.","allergen":"Egg · soy (the soy dipping sauce) · sesame (sesame oil) · gluten (soy sauce)","budget":"★★★ — eggs and a few vegetables; among the cheapest bright things on the Korean table","audience":"The lunchbox and banchan hero — the yellow omelette roll tucked into children's lunchboxes and set out on every banchan table, made for smiling at.","season":"A year-round table constant — the bright banchan that belongs to every season equally","ingredients":"3 eggs · 20g carrot, finely diced · 2 spring onions, finely sliced · 10g onion, very finely diced · a pinch of salt · a pinch of sugar · 5ml sesame oil · oil for the pan · optional garnish: toasted sesame seeds · DIP: soy sauce + a drop of sesame oil","method":"GYERAN-MARI IS THE KOREAN ROLLED OMELETTE — a bright yellow, gently rolled egg cake flecked with carrot and spring onion, sliced into thick coins and set out on the banchan table or packed into a lunchbox. It is the cheerful, modest jewel of the Korean side-plate world, and its simple craft is one of the first things taught to Korean children in the kitchen. THE METHOD IS THE THIN LAYER AND THE ROLL.\n\n⚖️ THE EGG — BEATEN SMOOTH, NOT FOAMY. Whisk the eggs with the salt, a pinch of sugar (the sugar is the Korean touch — it keeps the sweetness balanced against the soy dip) and the carrot, spring onion and onion. Whisk until the yolk and white are FULLY combined — a smooth, even yellow is the whole look. A few wisps of white left in the mixture is the beginner's giveaway.\n\n⚖️ THE PAN — OILED, LOW-MEDIUM, AND THIN. Heat a small non-stick pan over LOW-MEDIUM heat with a light film of oil. Pour in a THIN layer of the egg mixture — just enough to cover the base — and let it set gently without browning. The omelette should stay pale-yellow and tender, not browned; LOW heat is the rule.\n\n⚖️ THE ROLL — FROM ONE SIDE, TIGHT. When the thin layer is just set, begin rolling it from one side into a log, using a spatula, while it is still soft. Then pour another thin layer of the raw egg into the pan BESIDE the log, let it set, and roll the first log over the new layer to add another ring. Repeat until all the egg is used — the result is a neat, yellow, onion-and-carrot spangled log.\n\n⚖️ THE PRESS AND THE SLICE. Press the finished log gently in the pan for the last minute (or wrap it briefly in a clean cloth) to set the shape into a tidy rounded rectangle, then let it rest a minute off the heat. Slice into thick coins, scatter with sesame seeds, and serve with the soy-and-sesame dip. The coins show the sheer, spangled cross-section — the whole charm of gyeran-mari in every bite.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 10 min","kcal":"~180 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 13g · Carbs 4g · Fat 12g · Sodium ~300mg (before dip)","storage":"Best fresh and tender. Leftovers keep 2 days in the fridge (the roll firms as it cools) and are beloved cold in a lunchbox — slice them in the morning and they are just right at noon. Re-crisp softly if you prefer them warm.","chefNotes":"The three touches: the eggs BEATEN SMOOTH (a uniform yellow is the whole look), the pan on LOW-MEDIUM (a pale, tender omelette — browned gyeran-mari is the beginner's mistake), and the pinch of SUGAR (the balanced sweetness against the soy dip is the Korean signature). Rolling soft layers builds the spangled ring.","pairsWith":"The soy-and-sesame dip · A lunchbox with rice · The banchan table's other small plates · Cold barley tea","goesWith":["Bibimbap","Gimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Gyeran-mari — literally egg roll — is the gentle jewel of the banchan table, and for many Koreans the first dish they learned to make in a parent's kitchen: beaten eggs, a little colour, and a patient roll. Its cheerful yellow cross-section spangled with carrot and spring onion is the small, bright proof that the Korean table's side dishes are as much about charm as sustenance, and its place in the lunchbox is as constant as rice.","howThisFeels":"The warm yellow coins of tender rolled egg, the flecks of orange and green in every slice, the first bite soft and faintly sweet against the soy dip — the cheerful, comforting little plate that makes the whole table feel brighter.","leftovers":["The rolled omelette slices are perfect cold in a lunchbox the next day.","Chop leftover gyeran-mari over a bowl of rice with sesame for a fast egg rice bowl.","Slice it thin and tuck into a gimbap for a yellow ribbon's worth of extra charm."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Vegetable Gyeran-mari","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":10,"howThisFeels":"The classic bright roll — carrot, spring onion and onion spangled through the tender yellow layers, sliced and served with the soy dip.","trivia":"The classic is the vegetable-flecked original — carrot, spring onion and a whisper of sugar beaten into smooth yellow, rolled in tender layers.","delta":{},"ingredients":"3 eggs · 20g carrot, finely diced · 2 spring onions · 10g onion · pinch of salt · pinch of sugar · 5ml sesame oil · frying oil · sesame seeds · soy-sesame dip","method":["Beat the eggs smooth with the salt, sugar and the fine vegetables.","Heat a small pan on low-medium with a film of oil; pour a thin layer; let set gently, pale and tender.","Roll the set layer from one side into a log; add another thin layer beside it; roll the log over it; repeat until the egg is gone.","Press into a neat shape, rest, slice into coins, scatter with sesame, and serve with the dip."]}]},
{"id":"korea-kkakdugi","name":"Kkakdugi","nameAlt":"Radish kimchi — the bright, crunchy cube of the Korean table","aliases":["kkakdugi","radish kimchi","kkakdugi kimchi","cubed radish kimchi"],"course":"side","type":["side","kimchi","radish","fermented","banchan","vegetarian"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","banchan","bbq","side","make-ahead"],"mood":"Bright & crunchy","time":"Prep 20 min · Ferment 1-2 days (then it only deepens)","health":"A genuinely healthful fermented vegetable — fresh radish, chilli and a little sugar, bright with lactobacilli and crunch; the lighter, sharper sibling of napa-cabbage kimchi.","allergen":"Shellfish (the optional fish sauce) · soy (the optional fish sauce) · sesame (sesame seeds) · chilli","budget":"★★★ — a fresh radish and pantry seasonings; among the cheapest bright things a kitchen ferments","audience":"The everyday banchan and barbecue companion — the crunchy, cooling-crisp cube that appears beside nearly every Korean table and pairs gloriously with grilled meat.","season":"WINTER is its classic season — radish kimchi is the cold-season kimchi, made in quantity with the winter radish and kept in the crock — but it brightens tables year-round","ingredients":"300g Korean radish or daikon, peeled and cubed (1-2cm) · 15g coarse salt · 1 tsp sugar · THE PASTE: 15g gochugaru (Korean chilli flakes) · 10g gochujang (optional, a little extra depth) · 1 tsp grated garlic · 1 tsp grated ginger · 10ml fish sauce (optional — skip for vegan) · 1 spring onion, sliced · 1 tsp toasted sesame seeds","method":"KKAKDUGI IS THE RADISH KIMCHI — bright red cubes of Korean radish with a fierce crunch, fermented in the chilli-garlic-ginger paste until they turn translucent-edged, tangy and deeply good. It is the winter kimchi, made in quantity with the crisp cold-season radish, and it is the everyday banchan and the barbecue's best friend. THE METHOD IS THE SALT AND THE CUBE.\n\n⚖️ THE RADISH — CUBED, THEN SALTED FOR THE CRUNCH. Peel the radish and cut it into pleasing 1-2cm cubes — kkakdugi's bright white cubes are its signature shape. Toss the cubes with the coarse salt and the sugar, and let them sit 30-40 minutes. The salt draws out the radish's water and firms the flesh, which is what gives kkakdugi its famous crackling crunch. Drain (reserving a tablespoon of the salty water), and rinse the cubes briefly.\n\n⚖️ THE PASTE — THE BRIGHT RED COAT. Mix the gochugaru, gochujang, garlic, ginger, fish sauce (if using) and the reserved salted water into a thick red paste. Toss the drained radish cubes through the paste until every face is coated — the cubes should look jewel-bright, not patchy.\n\n⚖️ THE JAR AND THE FERMENT — 1-2 DAYS AT ROOM TEMPERATURE. Pack the coated cubes into a clean jar, pressing them down, and leave at room temperature for 1-2 days to begin fermenting — the juice will rise and the cubes will start to turn translucent at the edges. Then move to the fridge, where the fermentation slows and deepens. Taste daily: kkakdugi is its most fiercely crunchy at day 2-3 and its most deeply tangy after a week.\n\n⚖️ THE EAT — CRUNCHY, BRIGHT, ALONGSIDE EVERYTHING. Serve kkakdugi as the banchan, the barbecue companion (it cuts grilled fat perfectly), or the crunchy bright partner to stews and noodles. It is kimchi's second voice: cabbage kimchi is soft and silk; kkakdugi is the crack.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Ferment 1-2 days (then it only deepens)","kcal":"~60 kcal per 100g","nutrition":"Carbs 12g · Protein 1g · Fat 1g · Probiotic-rich and bright","storage":"Kkakdugi keeps 2-3 weeks refrigerated in a sealed jar, growing naturally tangier as it ages — the perfect ferment: make it, and it keeps giving. The fridge slows the ferment, so the crunch lasts for days and the tang deepens for weeks.","chefNotes":"Three rules: CUBE the radish and SALT it 30-40 minutes (the salt-draw is what makes the crackling crunch), COAT every face in the bright red paste (jewel-bright, not patchy), and FERMENT 1-2 days at room temperature before the fridge (the start of the tang, then the slow deep). The crunchy-raw window at day 2-3 is the classic love.","pairsWith":"Grilled meat (the essential barbecue pairing) · Every banchan table · Stews and noodle soups · A cold beer","goesWith":["Samgyeopsal","Galbi","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Kkakdugi is the winter kimchi of Korea — made in quantity with the crisp cold-season radish and kept in the crock where it ferments through the cold months, growing tangier as the season goes. It is kimchi's second voice, the crackle to napa cabbage's silk, and it is the essential bright cube beside grilled meat and the everyday table. The bright white cubes cutting through the red paste are one of the most familiar sights of the Korean banchan table.","howThisFeels":"The first crack of the salted radish cube, the bright chilli-garlic heat, the cool, juicy crunch — the tangy, crackling cube that makes every plate beside it taste sharper and every barbecue bite cleaner.","leftovers":["The deeper-tangy week-old kkakdugi is the chef's favourite — the perfect partner to stews and the crackle in a rice bowl.","Add well-soured kkakdugi (and a little of its juice) to a pan of kimchi fried rice for a radish-pop.","Spoon the bright cubes over cold noodles with a thread of sesame for a fast, tangy bowl."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Kkakdugi","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":14,"howThisFeels":"The winter classic — salted radish cubes, bright red paste, a 1-2 day ferment and the great crackling crunch.","trivia":"The classic is the whole-cube winter method — salt-drawn radish in the chilli-garlic-ginger paste, fermented to crackle.","delta":{},"ingredients":"300g radish, cubed · 15g coarse salt · 1 tsp sugar · 15g gochugaru · 10g gochujang · 1 tsp garlic · 1 tsp ginger · fish sauce (optional) · 1 spring onion · sesame seeds","method":["Cube the radish; toss with the salt and sugar; rest 30-40 minutes; drain, reserving a spoon of the water; rinse briefly.","Mix the gochugaru, gochujang, garlic, ginger, fish sauce (if using) and reserved water into a paste.","Coat every radish face in the paste; pack into a clean jar, pressing down.","Ferment at room temperature 1-2 days, then move to the fridge.","Serve from day 2-3 for the fiercest crunch — the bright cube of every banchan table."]}]},
{"id":"korea-jeyuk-bokkeum","name":"Jeyuk Bokkeum","nameAlt":"Spicy stir-fried pork — the loud, beloved pan of the Korean table","aliases":["jeyuk bokkeum","spicy pork stir fry","jeyuk","dwaeji bulgogi"],"course":"main","type":["main","pork","stir-fry","spicy","quick","everyday"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["everyday","weeknight","quick","drinking-food","bbq-adjacent"],"mood":"Bold & fast","time":"Prep 15 min · Marinate 20 min-1 h · Cook 8 min","health":"A bold, moreish white-meat stir-fry — pork shoulder in the spicy-sweet glaze with onion and pepper; rice is the required partner, and the balance is honest indulgence.","allergen":"Soy (soy sauce, gochujang) · sesame (sesame oil, seeds) · chilli","budget":"★★★ — pork shoulder is a cheap cut and the glaze is pantry; one of the great budget crowd-pleasers of the Korean table","audience":"The every-table pan — the spicy pork dish that anchors the weeknight dinner and the drinking table alike, and one of the first lessons in the Korean stir-fry school.","season":"Year-round and fast — the loud, hungry pan for any night","ingredients":"200g pork shoulder, thinly sliced · 1 onion, sliced · ½ green pepper, sliced (or a couple of green chillies, for heat) · 30g carrot, in matchsticks · 2 spring onions, in 4cm lengths · 20g gochujang · 10g gochugaru · 10ml soy sauce · 10ml honey or sugar · 1 tbsp minced garlic · 1 tsp grated ginger · 5ml sesame oil · toasted sesame seeds · oil for the pan","method":"JEYUK BOKKEUM IS THE SPICY STIR-FRIED PORK OF THE KOREAN TABLE — thin slices of pork shoulder tossed in a fierce, sweet-savoury gochujang glaze with onion, pepper and spring onion, seared fast in a screaming pan. It is a peer of bulgogi and the star of the home table, ordered as often at the drinking table as the dinner table. THE METHOD IS THE MARINADE AND THE FAST PAN.\n\n⚖️ THE MARINADE — THE PORK DRINKS IT BEFORE THE PAN. Mix the gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, garlic, ginger and sesame oil into a thick red paste. Massage it into the thin pork slices and let them marinate 20 minutes to an hour — the paste is the flavour, and the brief rest lets the pork drink it rather than wear it. (Overnight is even better, honestly.)\n\n⚖️ THE PAN — SEARING HOT, AND THE SPACE. Heat a wok or wide pan until it is smoking hot with a splash of oil. Add the marinated pork in a single layer — do NOT crowd it — and let it sear without stirring for a full minute so the sugars catch and caramelise against the iron. This initial char is the whole difference between jeyuk and stewed pork.\n\n⚖️ THE VEGETABLES — FAST AND BRIGHT. Toss the pork, add the onion, pepper and carrot, and stir-fry over high heat for 2-3 minutes until the vegetables are just cooked and the sauce is glossy and clinging. The pork should be cooked through but the pan should be loud, glossy and quick.\n\n⚖️ THE FINISH — SPRING ONION AND SESAME, STRAIGHT TO THE TABLE. Toss in the spring onion for the last 30 seconds, scatter with sesame seeds, and serve immediately with a bowl of steamed rice. Lift the pan to the table — jeyuk is eaten loud and now, the glossy red slices over rice the way the whole table wants them.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Marinate 20 min-1 h · Cook 8 min","kcal":"~420 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 30g · Carbs 24g · Fat 22g · Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"Best the minute it leaves the pan. Leftovers keep 2 days in the fridge and are superb cold wrapped in lettuce, or reheated in a flash and tipped over rice. The marinated raw pork freezes for a month — a future pan is then 10 minutes away.","chefNotes":"Three rules: MARINADE the pork 20 minutes to an hour (the paste is the flavour — it must drink, not wear), SEAR it untouched for a full minute over screaming heat (the caramelised char is the whole craft), and never CROWD the pan (space is what lets it sear rather than stew). The spring onion in the last 30 seconds keeps the bright finish.","pairsWith":"Steamed rice, always · Cold beer · Lettuce leaves for quick ssam wraps · Kimchi on the side","goesWith":["Bulgogi","Samgyeopsal","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Jeyuk bokkeum is the spicy sibling at the heart of the Korean pork table — thin-sliced shoulder in the gochujang glaze, seared loud and fast, the drinking-table and dinner-table favourite alike. It shares its marinade DNA with bulgogi but speaks with a fiercer, saucier voice, and its glossy red pan is one of the great weeknight sights of the Korean kitchen.","howThisFeels":"The loud sizzle of the marinated pork hitting the screaming pan, the caramelised char catching, the glossy red slices lifted steaming over a bowl of rice — the bold, fast, deeply satisfying pan that owns the hungry table.","leftovers":["Wrap leftover jeyuk and rice in lettuce leaves for a fast ssam lunch.","Tip reheated jeyuk over a bowl of rice with a fried egg for the hungriest 2-minute dinner.","The leftover pan glaze alone would sauce a quick noodle or vegetable stir-fry beautifully."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Jeyuk Bokkeum","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The classic fierce pan — marinated pork shoulder seared hot, glossy red with onion, pepper and spring onion, over steaming rice.","trivia":"The classic is the gochujang-glazed pork shoulder, seared loud and fast — the drinking-table and weeknight favourite in one.","delta":{},"ingredients":"200g pork shoulder, sliced · 1 onion · ½ green pepper · 30g carrot · 2 spring onions · 20g gochujang · 10g gochugaru · 10ml soy · 10ml honey · 1 tbsp garlic · 1 tsp ginger · 5ml sesame oil · sesame seeds · pan oil","method":["Mix the gochujang, gochugaru, soy, honey, garlic, ginger and sesame into a paste; massage into the pork; rest 20 minutes to an hour.","Heat the pan screaming hot; sear the pork in a single layer, untouched 1 minute, for the caramelised char.","Toss, add the onion, pepper and carrot; stir-fry 2-3 minutes until glossy.","Finish with spring onion 30 seconds; scatter sesame; serve immediately over rice."]},{"name":"Dak Jeyuk (Chicken, Budget)","icon":"🐔","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"The same fierce red pan with chicken thigh — cheaper, just as glossy, and the weeknight favourite of many a Korean home.","trivia":"Chicken thigh takes the same gochujang glaze and cooks in the same fast pan — a beloved budget fork that many Korean homes actually prefer.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"200g pork shoulder, thinly sliced","to":"200g chicken thigh, thinly sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use chicken thigh in place of the pork shoulder — same marinade, same fast pan. Chicken needs a little longer to cook through (add about a minute to the toss), and the thigh's fat keeps it as silky as the pork. Everything else — the caramelised char, the bright vegetables, the spring onion finish — is identical."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-kalguksu","name":"Kalguksu","nameAlt":"Knife-cut noodle soup — the handmade bowl of the Korean corner house","aliases":["kalguksu","knife cut noodles","korean noodle soup","kalguksu soup"],"course":"main","type":["main","noodles","soup","handmade","comfort","one-bowl"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["comfort","everyday","winter","lunch","restaurant-style"],"mood":"Warm & handmade","time":"Prep 15 min (+rest the dough 30 min) · Cook 20 min","health":"A comforting, honest noodle soup — handmade wheat noodles in a light kelp-anchovy broth with vegetables and a little protein; the warm, filling bowl of the Korean corner house.","allergen":"Gluten (wheat noodles) · soy (soy sauce in the broth) · sesame (sesame oil) · shellfish (optional clams) · egg (the omelette garnish)","budget":"★★★ — flour, broth and vegetables; the handmade rustic bowl priced for everyone","audience":"The corner-house classic — the handmade noodle soup that anchors the Korean neighbourhood restaurant, and the comforting home bowl for cold days.","season":"Winter and the cold comfort-bowl season — though its fans would eat it in rain without blinking","ingredients":"100g plain flour (+ a little for dusting) · 60ml cold water · a pinch of salt · 400ml kelp-anchovy broth (or light stock) · 50g courgette, in thin half-moons · 40g onion, sliced · 1 small potato, very thinly sliced (the classic chunky add) · 1 egg (for the ribbon garnish) · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 1 spring onion, sliced · optional: 4 clams or a little shredded chicken (the classic proteins)","method":"KALGUKSU IS THE KNIFE-CUT NOODLE SOUP OF KOREA — broad, hand-cut wheat noodles in a light, kelp-anchovy broth, with courgette, onion, potato and a ribbon of egg, the handmade soul of the corner noodle house. The name says everything: kal (knife), guksu (noodles) — the noodles ARE the dish, cut by hand with a knife and dropped straight into the waiting broth. THE METHOD IS THE DOUGH AND THE DROP.\n\n⚖️ THE DOUGH — RESTED, THEN CUT BY HAND. Mix the flour, salt and cold water into a firm dough and knead it 5 minutes until smooth. Cover and rest 30 minutes — the rest relaxes the gluten and makes the noodles tender rather than tough. Roll the rested dough out on a floured board to a thin sheet (about 2mm) and cut it into rough, wide ribbons with a knife. The irregular handmade widths are the whole charm.\n\n⚖️ THE BROTH — LIGHT, KELP-ANCHOVY, THE CLEAN BASE. The classic kalguksu broth is the light kelp-anchovy stock (or a good light stock), seasoned with just the soy, salt and a thread of sesame. It must be light and clean — the broth is the canvas, not the star.\n\n⚖️ THE VEGETABLES AND THE DROP. Bring the broth to a gentle bubble with the courgette, onion and potato, and simmer 5 minutes until the vegetables soften. Drop the hand-cut noodles in and simmer 6-8 minutes until they are cooked through and the broth has thickened slightly from their starch — that gentle starchiness is the signature of a real homemade kalguksu.\n\n⚖️ THE RIBBON AND THE BOWL. Just before serving, beat the egg and stream it into the bubbling soup in a ribbon, or make a thin omelette and slice it to float on top. Ladle the noodles and broth into a deep bowl, finish with the spring onion and a thread of sesame oil, and eat immediately with the spoon and chopsticks working together — the handmade ribbons of noodle are the whole point, and the bowl is the comfort of a Korean corner house in winter.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min (+rest 30 min) · Cook 20 min","kcal":"~380 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 16g · Carbs 62g · Fat 8g · Sodium ~1000mg","storage":"Best the moment the noodles are cooked — handmade noodles wait for no one. The broth keeps 2 days in the fridge; cook a fresh batch of noodles into it to rebuild the bowl. The raw, cut noodles freeze well on a floured tray for a month.","chefNotes":"Three rules: REST the dough 30 minutes (the tender noodle is the whole craft — a rushed dough is the tough noodle), CUT it by hand into rough wide ribbons (the handmade irregularity is the charm), and DROP it into a LIGHT broth — the clean kelp-anchovy canvas and the gentle starch-thicken when the noodles cook are the signature.","pairsWith":"Kimchi on the side · A little soy and sesame at the table · The coldest day of winter","goesWith":["Seolleongtang","Gamjatang","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Kalguksu — knife-cut noodles — is the handmade soul of the Korean corner noodle house, named literally for the knife that cuts its broad, irregular ribbons straight into the waiting broth. It is the bowl of the neighbourhood restaurant, the cold-day comfort, and the proof that the simplest of Korean dishes — flour, water, a light broth and vegetables — can be one of the most satisfying in the language.","howThisFeels":"The broad, handmade ribbons of noodle slipping in the light warm broth, the potato soft, the egg ribbon bright, the spoon and chopsticks working together — the warm, honest comfort of a bowl cut by hand and made just for you.","leftovers":["The broth keeps 2 days — new noodles, new bowl.","Chop leftover noodles into a pan with a little soy and sesame for a fast kalguksu fried noodle.","The raw cut noodles freeze on a floured tray — a future bowl is 15 minutes from the freezer."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Kalguksu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"The corner-house classic — hand-cut ribbons in the light kelp-anchovy broth with courgette, potato and the egg ribbon.","trivia":"The classic is the handmade original — rested dough cut by knife, dropped into the light clean broth with the vegetables and the ribbon of egg.","delta":{},"ingredients":"100g flour · 60ml cold water · pinch of salt · 400ml kelp-anchovy broth · 50g courgette · 40g onion · 1 small potato · 1 egg · 10ml soy · 5ml sesame oil · 1 spring onion","method":["Knead the flour, salt and water into a firm dough; rest 30 minutes.","Roll thin; cut into broad, rough ribbons with a knife.","Simmer the courgette, onion and potato in the broth 5 minutes.","Drop the noodles in; simmer 6-8 minutes until cooked and the broth gently thickens.","Ribbon the egg in, finish with spring onion and sesame, and serve immediately."]},{"name":"With Clams or Chicken (The Coastal & Home Versions)","icon":"🦪","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The same handmade bowl with a handful of clams or a little shredded chicken in the broth — the classic enrichments of the regional corner houses.","trivia":"Clams in the broth are the coastal touch (their clean sweetness deepens the kelp base), and a little shred of chicken is the home version — both turn the simple bowl into a fuller meal.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"4 clams, scrubbed, or 40g cooked chicken, shredded"}],"addStep":[{"text":"For the clam version, add the scrubbed clams with the vegetables so they open into the broth (5-7 minutes), then ladle the bowl over them. For the chicken version, stir the warm shredded chicken through before serving. Either way, the handmade noodles and the clean broth stay the heart — the protein simply deepens the bowl."}]}}]},
{"id":"korea-miyeok-guk","name":"Miyeok-guk","nameAlt":"Seaweed soup — the birthday bowl of Korea","aliases":["miyeok guk","seaweed soup korea","birthday soup","miyeok soup"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","seaweed","birthday","comfort","everyday"],"diet":["vegetarian","omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Korea","occasion":["birthday","celebration","everyday","family","comfort"],"mood":"Gentle & nourishing","time":"Prep 10 min (+soak 30 min) · Cook 25 min","health":"A genuinely nourishing, mineral-rich bowl — wakame seaweed, a little beef, garlic and sesame; the traditional Korean birthday meal, eaten for its mother's sake.","allergen":"Sesame (sesame oil) · soy (the optional soy sauce) · beef (in the classic) · seafood (the iodised seaweed family)","budget":"★★★ — dried seaweed is cheap and the broth is pantry; one of the most affordable meaningful bowls in Korean food","audience":"THE BIRTHDAY BOWL — every Korean eats miyeok-guk on their birthday, in gratitude to the mother who gave birth, and the same warm, gentle bowl anchors the everyday table and the morning after.","season":"A year-round constant — the birthday bowl on birthdays, and the gentle, nourishing bowl for every ordinary day in between","ingredients":"10g dried miyeok (wakame seaweed) · 50g beef, thinly sliced (optional — the classic; skip for a lighter/vegetarian bowl) · 2 cloves garlic, minced · 10ml sesame oil · 5ml soy sauce (optional) · 400ml water or light stock · salt · 1 spring onion, finely sliced · toasted sesame seeds","method":"MIYEOK-GUK IS THE BIRTHDAY SOUP OF KOREA — a gentle, mineral-rich soup of wakame seaweed and a little beef, the bowl every Korean eats on their birthday in gratitude to the mother who gave them life. It is the warmest of Korean food rituals: the birthday bowl, and the same gentle soup anchors the everyday table and the morning-after table. THE METHOD IS THE SOAK AND THE WARM SIMMER.\n\n⚖️ THE SEAWEED — SOAKED, THEN PREPARED WITH CARE. Soak the dried miyeok in cold water 30 minutes — it expands enormously, so a little goes far. Drain, and cut it into manageable lengths. (Miyeok is the wakame of the sushi world — the gentle green seaweed that softens in any warm liquid.)\n\n⚖️ THE SESAME BLOOM AND THE BEEF — THE WARM BASE. Heat the sesame oil in a pot and sauté the beef slices (if using) with the garlic for 2-3 minutes until the beef is just brown — the sesame-garlic oil is the soup's fragrant base. Add the drained miyeok and toss it through the warm oil for a minute so it takes up the flavour.\n\n⚖️ THE SIMMER — GENTLE, AND THE COLOUR OF THE BROTH. Add the water or stock and the soy (if using), bring to a gentle bubble, and simmer 15-20 minutes until the broth has taken on the seaweed's soft, mineral green and the miyeok is tender. Season with salt to taste — the broth should be clean, warm and gently savoury, never heavy.\n\n⚖️ THE BIRTHDAY BOWL — EAT IT WITH THE THANKSGIVING. Serve hot, finished with the spring onion and sesame seeds, with rice alongside or stirred in. The ritual is the meaning: on your birthday, the bowl says thank you to your mother. It is also the gentle morning-after bowl and the everyday warm broth — the soft, mineral, deep-comfort taste of being looked after, available any day at all.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min (+soak 30 min) · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~180 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 12g · Carbs 6g · Fat 10g · Iodine-rich seaweed · a gentle, mineral bowl","storage":"Best the day it is made — the seaweed stays bright and the broth clean. Keeps 3 days in the fridge (it deepens gently) and freezes well for a month. Reheat gently and add a splash of water; the seaweed softens further as it sits.","chefNotes":"Three gentle rules: SOAK the miyeok 30 minutes (it expands enormously — a little goes far), give it a minute in the warm sesame-garlic oil with the beef (the fragrant base of the whole bowl), and SIMMER gently 15-20 minutes (the clean, mineral green is the colour of done). The birthday bowl is the meaning — season it warm and kind, never heavy.","pairsWith":"A bowl of rice, always · A birthday wish · Kimchi on the side · A quiet morning","goesWith":["Tteokguk","Bibimbap","Korea-staple-gochujang"],"trivia":"Miyeok-guk is the birthday soup of Korea — the gentle seaweed and beef broth that every Korean eats on their birthday, in the old tradition that thanks the mother for the gift of life by offering her the most nourishing bowl the kitchen knows. Wakame or miyeok, the seaweed's older name, is among the most mineral-dense foods of the sea, and the warm, green, deeply plain broth is simultaneously Korea's most meaningful bowl and its most everyday comfort.","howThisFeels":"The warm, mineral-green broth with the soft seaweed and a little beef, the sesame-garlic fragrance rising, the first spoon clean and gently savoury — the bowl that tastes like gratitude, whether it is your birthday or just the day you needed the kindest thing in the kitchen.","leftovers":["The bowl keeps 3 days and freezes a month — the gentle green warmth is never far away.","Tip leftover miyeok-guk over a bowl of hot rice for a fast seaweed rice soup.","The seaweed broth makes a clean, mineral base for a future vegetable or noodle soup."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Beef Miyeok-guk","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"The traditional birthday bowl — wakame, a little beef, sesame-garlic base and the clean, mineral broth.","trivia":"The classic is the beef version — the family birthday bowl, the warm mineral soup eaten in gratitude to the mother.","delta":{},"ingredients":"10g dried miyeok · 50g beef, sliced · 2 cloves garlic · 10ml sesame oil · 5ml soy · 400ml water or stock · salt · spring onion · sesame seeds","method":["Soak the miyeok 30 minutes; drain; cut into lengths.","Sauté the beef and garlic in the sesame oil 2-3 minutes; add the miyeok; toss 1 minute in the warm oil.","Add the water and soy; simmer 15-20 minutes until the broth is a soft mineral green and the seaweed is tender.","Season with salt; finish with spring onion and sesame; serve with rice — the birthday bowl."]},{"name":"Vegan Miyeok-guk (No Beef)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":14,"howThisFeels":"The same gentle, mineral-green bowl without the beef — the garlic-sesame base and the seaweed carry it completely, and the birthday meaning is unchanged.","trivia":"Simply omit the beef and deepen the broth with a strip of kelp — the wakame and sesame-garlic are the bowl's soul, and the vegetable version is just as beloved.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"50g beef, thinly sliced (optional — the classic; skip for a lighter/vegetarian bowl)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"1 strip kelp for the stock"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the beef entirely and simmer a strip of kelp in the water for 10 minutes for added savoury depth, then remove it. The garlic-sesame bloom and the softening miyeok carry the whole gentle bowl — the birthday meaning and the mineral green are all intact in the fully plant-based version."}]}}]},
];